@prefix prim: <urn:silmaril:prim:#> .
@prefix silm: <urn:silmaril:entity#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# =====================================================================================
# realization.ttl — the REALIZATION monad ρ of the Primitive Floor (W2 · SP1, plan Task 4).
# =====================================================================================
# The gluing morphism of the twin-olog colimit taiji: ρ maps each FormalType (yin,
# formal.ttl) to its PhysicalEncoding(s) (yang, physical.ttl) as Kleisli arrows of the
# Frame monad. Every realization IS a Frame — it carries prim:frameOutput (the encoding
# it produces) and prim:frameEffect (a typed prim:Effect individual), so a bare mapping
# without an effect is not admitted (unary law: T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y)).
# The realization monad ≡ the Frame monad: the same Yoneda-point structure the byte-frame
# law names, not an analogy. Polymorphism is the whole point — one FormalType realizes as
# MANY encodings (ℝ → Float64 | Float32; ℤ → INT8..128; ℕ → UINT8..64; Codepoint →
# UTF-8/16/32) and each realization carries its own effect. The monad unit prim:realize_unit
# is the trivial identity Kleisli arrow (prim:isMonadUnit true, prim:frameEffect NoEffect),
# witnessing the left/right unit law; byte-descent = Kleisli composition of realizations
# down to the prim:Bit floor, whose ladder edges are the prim:byteDescendsTo chain in
# physical.ttl. Corpus-agnostic: pure shape, zero corpus instances.
#
# Property-name contract (binding across the floor, matching primitives.queries.sparql):
#   realizesAs   : FormalType   -> Realization       (a formal type realizes AS an arrow)
#   realizesFrom : Realization  -> FormalType        (the arrow's source, back-link)
#   encoding     : Realization  -> PhysicalEncoding  (the arrow's chosen physical target)
#   frameOutput  : Realization  -> PhysicalEncoding  (the Frame output coordinate = encoding)
#   frameEffect  : Realization  -> Effect            (the Frame error|effect coordinate)
#   isMonadUnit  : Realization  -> xsd:boolean       (true only for the identity unit arrow)
#
# Design spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-07-w2-primitive-floor-design.md (§5)
# Plan:        docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-07-w2-sp1-primitive-floor.md (Task 4)
# Consumes:    formal.ttl (FormalType tower) + physical.ttl (PhysicalEncoding individuals)
# Acceptance:  q_number_realized (litmus half #2), q_frame_effect, q_monad_unit GREEN.
# =====================================================================================


# =====================================================================================
# Properties introduced by the realization monad
# =====================================================================================

prim:realizesAs a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "realizesAs" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:range prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:comment "The forward gluing edge of the colimit taiji: from a FormalType (yin) to a prim:Realization arrow that carries it into the physical olog. This is the property the what-is-a-number litmus (q_number_realized) walks — prim:Real prim:realizesAs ?r ; ?r prim:encoding ?e ; ?e prim:bitWidth 64 — so a FormalType with no realizesAs edge is an ungrounded, broken primitive the SHACL dual-grounding law rejects. One formal type may realizesAs many arrows: that fan-out is exactly the polymorphism of the realization monad." .

prim:realizesFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "realizesFrom" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "The back-link of a realization arrow to the FormalType it departs from — the inverse direction of prim:realizesAs, recorded on the Realization so the Kleisli arrow's source is directly navigable without an inverse join. Together with prim:encoding it makes a Realization a self-describing morphism (source formal type, target physical encoding, effect); the taiji colimit round-trip reads the physical target's prim:interpretAs against this realizesFrom to confirm yin and yang name the same primitive." .

prim:encoding a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "encoding" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:range prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:comment "The target edge of a realization arrow: from the prim:Realization to the concrete prim:PhysicalEncoding (yang) it lands in. It is the second hop of the litmus walk FormalType --realizesAs--> Realization --encoding--> PhysicalEncoding, and it is the encoding whose prim:interpretAs back-pointer must close the mutual colimit back to the realization's source formal type. Distinct from prim:frameOutput only in role: encoding names the chosen carrier format, frameOutput exposes that same encoding as the Frame's output coordinate." .

prim:frameOutput a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "frameOutput" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:range prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:comment "The output coordinate of the Frame a realization IS: the prim:PhysicalEncoding value the Kleisli arrow produces. In the unary law's T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) this is X, and the byte-stream carrier closure requires X to descend through the identified byte-carrier system — so frameOutput is ALWAYS a prim:PhysicalEncoding, never a bare formal type. There is no boundary exemption: even the monad unit prim:realize_unit outputs a genuine physical encoding, the zero-width prim:UnitEncoding, whose prim:interpretAs closes back to the terminal prim:Unit; that is how the identity arrow's output still descends to the byte floor. Every Realization MUST declare exactly one frameOutput and exactly one prim:frameEffect; a mapping missing either, or one whose output is a non-byte-carrying formal class, is not a Frame and not a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, and q_frame_effect plus the SHACL RealizationShape/EncodingShape reject it." .

prim:frameEffect a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "frameEffect" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:range prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:comment "The error-or-effect coordinate of the Frame a realization IS: the typed prim:Effect the Kleisli arrow carries alongside its output. In the unary law's T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) this is Y. It is what distinguishes a realization from a bare function — realizing ℝ as Float64 is not free, it costs an IEEE754-rounding effect; realizing an unbounded Bignum into a bounded ByteVector costs a may-overflow effect. The identity unit arrow carries prim:NoEffect, the neutral element witnessing the monad's unit law." .

prim:isMonadUnit a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "isMonadUnit" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment "Flags a Kleisli identity arrow that is a monad unit η — the trivial identity morphism A ⤳ A (prim:kDom = prim:kCod) whose composition with any arrow f is f unchanged (the left/right unit law η_A >=> f ≡ f and f >=> η_B ≡ f) and which introduces prim:NoEffect. The domain is prim:KleisliArrow because the unit is fundamentally an identity ARROW indexed by an object: this floor carries one per object needed as a unit witness — prim:k_id_Real (η at Real, the left-unit witness's domain identity) and prim:k_id_Float64 (η at Float64, the right-unit witness's CODOMAIN identity) — plus the realization-level unit prim:realize_unit whose composable core is exactly such an identity arrow. The acceptance ASK q_monad_unit selects both a left-unit and a right-unit Composition whose unit operand carries prim:isMonadUnit true with kDom = kCod and reads the kDom/kCod signatures to confirm each composition is well-typed, so the flag is a checkable trace of the Frame monad's two-sided unit law, not a decoration." .


# =====================================================================================
# The Realization class — a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the realization monad
# =====================================================================================

prim:Realization a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Realization" ;
    rdfs:comment "A realization ρ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad gluing one FormalType (yin) of formal.ttl to one PhysicalEncoding (yang) of physical.ttl, and thereby the gluing morphism of the colimit taiji. A Realization is not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), so it declares prim:realizesFrom (its source formal type), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput (its target encoding = the Frame output X), and prim:frameEffect (the typed cost Y of choosing that encoding). One formal type realizesAs many Realizations — that fan-out is the polymorphism the monad exists to model, and the chosen Realization is what a colimit-taiji primitive instance selects. Composing Realizations is Kleisli composition, byte-descending to the prim:Bit floor via the physical carrier ladder; the identity of that composition is the monad unit prim:realize_unit." .


# =====================================================================================
# The Effect class + its typed individuals (the Frame error|effect coordinate)
# =====================================================================================

prim:Effect a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Effect" ;
    rdfs:comment "The typed error-or-effect a realization Frame carries in its Y coordinate — the cost or caveat incurred when an abstract formal value is forced into a concrete physical encoding. Effect is the reason realization is monadic rather than a bare total function: mapping ℝ onto a finite binary float rounds, holding ℝ to a finite base-10 decimal rounds in base ten, quantizing a continuous Instant onto a fixed epoch tick rounds temporally, narrowing Float64 to Float32 loses low bits, forcing an unbounded Bignum into a bounded ByteVector may overflow, and encoding a Unicode Codepoint may lose through surrogate or normalization handling. Its ten current individuals, in alphabetical order — prim:Base10Precision, prim:EncodingLoss, prim:EpochPrecision, prim:MayOverflow, prim:NarrowingLoss, prim:NoEffect, prim:OrderingCanonicalization, prim:OverflowDomain, prim:RoundingIEEE754, prim:Uninhabited — are the typed alternatives this iteration admits (spec §5/§9). This is NOT a fixed nine or a permanently closed enumeration: it is the current, extensible set of typed effects, and further effect kinds are minted only when a concrete consumer needs one (YAGNI, spec §8) — prim:EpochPrecision was itself added exactly so, for the Instant realization. prim:NoEffect is the neutral element the monad unit prim:realize_unit carries, and prim:Uninhabited is the honest edge the initial type Void carries." .

prim:RoundingIEEE754 a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "IEEE-754 rounding" ;
    rdfs:comment "The rounding effect incurred when a real number is realized into a finite-precision IEEE-754 float (Float16/32/64/80/128): the infinite real line is mapped onto a finite grid of representable values, so all but exactly-representable reals are rounded to the nearest grid point under the format's rounding mode." .

prim:NarrowingLoss a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "narrowing loss" ;
    rdfs:comment "The additional loss effect incurred when a wider realization is narrowed to a fewer-bit encoding — for example a Float64-sourced real forced into Float32 — where low-order mantissa bits and dynamic range are discarded beyond the base IEEE-754 rounding of the target format itself." .

prim:OverflowDomain a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "overflow domain (width-bounded)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The bounded-domain effect of realizing an integer (ℤ) or natural (ℕ) into a fixed-width machine encoding (INT8..128 / UINT8..64): the encoding represents only a finite window of the unbounded integer line, so values outside its width wrap, saturate, or trap. The bound is exact and known per width; it is the honest cost of a fixed carrier." .

prim:EncodingLoss a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "encoding loss (surrogate / normalization)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The lossy-transform effect of realizing an abstract Unicode Codepoint (or a lexical/temporal value) through a concrete text encoding: surrogate-pair handling in UTF-16, over-long or ill-formed sequence rejection in UTF-8, and normalization-form differences mean the byte round-trip is not always the identity on the abstract value without a stated normalization discipline." .

prim:MayOverflow a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "may overflow (unbounded → bounded)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The effect of realizing an arbitrary-precision value (a Bignum) into a bounded physical carrier such as a ByteVector of 1..128 byte occurrences or a 128-bit machine word: a mathematically unbounded value meets a bounded carrier, so a sufficiently large magnitude cannot be represented and the realization may overflow — the typed admission of the unbounded/bounded mismatch." .

prim:Base10Precision a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "base-10 precision (decimal coefficient + scale)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The base-ten precision effect incurred when a value is realized as a base-10 fixed-point Decimal — a finite coefficient paired with a decimal scale — rather than a binary IEEE-754 float. It is the effect spec §5 names for the Real → Decimal realization: a real number is held to a finite number of decimal places, so it rounds on the base-10 grid (exact for values such as 0.1 that binary floats cannot represent, and lossy for irrationals and non-terminating decimals). It is distinct from prim:RoundingIEEE754 (binary grid) precisely because the grid is base ten, and distinct from prim:NoEffect because a finite decimal scale is still a bounded precision." .

prim:NoEffect a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "no effect (exact / lossless)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The neutral effect: the realization is exact and lossless, introducing no rounding, narrowing, overflow, or encoding loss — the identity element of effect composition. It is what the monad unit prim:realize_unit carries (witnessing the unit law) and what a faithful realization such as a Boolean into a one-byte BOOLEAN encoding carries, since the two truth values map bijectively onto 0 and 1." .

prim:OrderingCanonicalization a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "ordering canonicalization (unordered → serialized order)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonicalization effect incurred when an inherently unordered formal value — a Set, whose identity has no element order — is realized into a necessarily ordered byte serialization: the realization must impose a canonical sort so the bytes are deterministic, which means two presentations differing only in element order map to the SAME bytes. The mapping is therefore not the identity on presentation order (order information is deliberately discarded), even though the set's membership is preserved exactly. It is distinct from prim:NoEffect precisely because a real transformation (canonical sorting) occurs, and distinct from prim:EncodingLoss because no membership is lost — only the meaningless input ordering is normalized away." .

prim:Uninhabited a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "uninhabited (the initial type Void has no values)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The honest edge effect (spec §9f) carried by the realization of prim:Void, the initial/empty type: Void has no values at all, so a value of Void can never be constructed and its realization into the empty 0-byte prim:VoidEmpty encoding is the realization of an impossible value. It is deliberately NOT prim:NoEffect — a lossless map of nothing is still qualitatively different from a lossless map of a real value — and it is how universality of dual-grounding stays total without a PROVISIONAL-dodge: Void is genuinely realized, its typed cost being that the domain is empty. It is the effect-level dual of the terminal Unit's prim:NoEffect (Unit has exactly one value; Void has none)." .


# =====================================================================================
# ρ realizations — the spec §5 table. Each is a Frame: realizesFrom + encoding +
# frameOutput + frameEffect. A FormalType realizesAs each of its arrows (fan-out =
# monad polymorphism). Encodings are the individuals authored in physical.ttl.
# =====================================================================================

# ---- Real ℝ → IEEE-754 floats (rounding; +narrowing when target is narrower) -----------
# spec §5's Real row also named a Real → Decimal column. It was formerly realized here through
# a shared prim:Ascii text carrier whose prim:interpretAs closes to prim:Codepoint (text, not a
# number) and was marked silm:isProvisional — a non-reflexive proxy the panel flagged (§9d) and
# an inaccurate effect/encoding for a Real. It is REMOVED at the source: prim:Real realizes only
# through the binary IEEE-754 float carriers below, and base-10 fixed point is the job of the
# distinct prim:Decimal formal type, which carries its OWN reflexive packed-BCD realization
# (prim:Decimal_Realization → prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding → interpretAs prim:Decimal, below).
prim:Real prim:realizesAs
    prim:realize_Real_Float64 , prim:realize_Real_Float32 , prim:realize_Real_Float16 .

prim:realize_Real_Float64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℝ ⟼ Float64 · 8B · LE" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Real ; prim:encoding prim:Float64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Float64 ; prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Real_Float64 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Real (the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Float64 (64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the value rounds onto the finite IEEE-754 binary grid (prim:RoundingIEEE754) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Real (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Real_Float32 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℝ ⟼ Float32 · 4B · LE" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Real ; prim:encoding prim:Float32 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Float32 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 , prim:NarrowingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Real_Float32 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Real (the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Float32 (32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the value rounds onto the finite IEEE-754 binary grid (prim:RoundingIEEE754); and additionally loses low-order mantissa bits and dynamic range when narrowed (prim:NarrowingLoss) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Real (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Real_Float16 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℝ ⟼ Float16 · 2B · LE" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Real ; prim:encoding prim:Float16 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Float16 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 , prim:NarrowingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Real_Float16 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Real (the real numbers ℝ, a subclass of Complex and superclass of Rational) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Float16 (16 bits / 2 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the value rounds onto the finite IEEE-754 binary grid (prim:RoundingIEEE754); and additionally loses low-order mantissa bits and dynamic range when narrowed (prim:NarrowingLoss) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Real (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

# NOTE (honesty, spec §9b/§9d/§9f): the former prim:realize_Real_Decimal and
# prim:realize_Decimal_Ascii proxy arrows — both silm:isProvisional, both routing through the
# shared prim:Ascii text carrier whose interpretAs is prim:Codepoint — have been REMOVED here.
# They were the last two isProvisional nodes in the floor and the last non-reflexive number
# realizations. prim:Decimal is now grounded ONLY by its own reflexive packed-BCD arrow
# prim:Decimal_Realization (encoding prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding, interpretAs prim:Decimal),
# integrated below; a base-10 carrier is resolvable in-scope work, so isProvisional (reserved
# for genuinely undecidable Präriehund gaps) never applied to it. No proxy Real→text arrow
# remains; ℝ realizes only through the binary IEEE-754 floats above.

# ---- Integer ℤ → two's-complement INT8..128 (width-bounded overflow domain) ---------
prim:Integer prim:realizesAs
    prim:realize_Integer_Int8 , prim:realize_Integer_Int16 ,
    prim:realize_Integer_Int32 , prim:realize_Integer_Int64 , prim:realize_Integer_Int128 .

prim:realize_Integer_Int8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℤ ⟼ INT8" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Integer ; prim:encoding prim:Int8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Int8 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Integer_Int8 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Integer (the integers ℤ, a subclass of Rational and superclass of Natural) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Int8 (8 bits / 1 bytes, endian-neutral), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Integer (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Integer_Int16 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℤ ⟼ INT16" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Integer ; prim:encoding prim:Int16 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Int16 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Integer_Int16 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Integer (the integers ℤ, a subclass of Rational and superclass of Natural) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Int16 (16 bits / 2 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Integer (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Integer_Int32 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℤ ⟼ INT32" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Integer ; prim:encoding prim:Int32 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Int32 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Integer_Int32 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Integer (the integers ℤ, a subclass of Rational and superclass of Natural) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Int32 (32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Integer (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Integer_Int64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℤ ⟼ INT64" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Integer ; prim:encoding prim:Int64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Int64 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Integer_Int64 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Integer (the integers ℤ, a subclass of Rational and superclass of Natural) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Int64 (64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Integer (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Integer_Int128 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℤ ⟼ INT128" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Integer ; prim:encoding prim:Int128 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Int128 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Integer_Int128 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Integer (the integers ℤ, a subclass of Rational and superclass of Natural) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Int128 (128 bits / 16 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Integer (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

# ---- Natural ℕ → unsigned UINT8..64 (width-bounded overflow domain) -----------------
prim:Natural prim:realizesAs
    prim:realize_Natural_Uint8 , prim:realize_Natural_Uint16 ,
    prim:realize_Natural_Uint32 , prim:realize_Natural_Uint64 .

prim:realize_Natural_Uint8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℕ ⟼ UINT8" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Natural ; prim:encoding prim:Uint8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Uint8 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Natural_Uint8 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Natural (the natural numbers ℕ, a subclass of Integer, the counting floor of the number tower) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Uint8 (8 bits / 1 bytes, endian-neutral), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Natural (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Natural_Uint16 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℕ ⟼ UINT16" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Natural ; prim:encoding prim:Uint16 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Uint16 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Natural_Uint16 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Natural (the natural numbers ℕ, a subclass of Integer, the counting floor of the number tower) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Uint16 (16 bits / 2 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Natural (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Natural_Uint32 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℕ ⟼ UINT32" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Natural ; prim:encoding prim:Uint32 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Uint32 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Natural_Uint32 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Natural (the natural numbers ℕ, a subclass of Integer, the counting floor of the number tower) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Uint32 (32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Natural (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Natural_Uint64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℕ ⟼ UINT64" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Natural ; prim:encoding prim:Uint64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Uint64 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Natural_Uint64 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Natural (the natural numbers ℕ, a subclass of Integer, the counting floor of the number tower) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Uint64 (64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Natural (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

# ---- Bignum grounding: the old R1 Bignum→ByteVectorBignum realization block was
#      removed here; the reflexive frag version (prim:Bignum_Realization →
#      prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding, interpretAs prim:Bignum) is integrated below
#      from frags/Bignum.ttl (spec §9b/§9d).

# ---- Codepoint → UTF-8 / UTF-16 / UTF-32 (encoding loss: surrogate / normalization) -
prim:Codepoint prim:realizesAs
    prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf8 , prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf16 , prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32 .

prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Codepoint ⟼ UTF-8 · 1..4B" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Codepoint ; prim:encoding prim:Utf8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Utf8 ; prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf8 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Codepoint (a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Utf8 (32 bits / 4 bytes, endian-neutral), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is surrogate, normalization or lexical variance means the byte round-trip is not always the identity without a stated discipline (prim:EncodingLoss) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Codepoint (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf16 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Codepoint ⟼ UTF-16 · 2|4B" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Codepoint ; prim:encoding prim:Utf16 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Utf16 ; prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf16 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Codepoint (a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Utf16 (32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is surrogate, normalization or lexical variance means the byte round-trip is not always the identity without a stated discipline (prim:EncodingLoss) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Codepoint (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Codepoint ⟼ UTF-32 · 4B" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Codepoint ; prim:encoding prim:Utf32 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Utf32 ; prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Codepoint (a Unicode scalar value, the atom of the text tower) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Utf32 (32 bits / 4 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is surrogate, normalization or lexical variance means the byte round-trip is not always the identity without a stated discipline (prim:EncodingLoss) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Codepoint (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

# ---- Bool → BOOLEAN 1-byte 0/1 (bijective; no effect) -------------------------------
prim:Bool prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Bool_Boolean .

prim:realize_Bool_Boolean a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Bool ⟼ BOOLEAN · 1B (0/1)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Bool ; prim:encoding prim:Boolean ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Boolean ; prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Bool_Boolean — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Bool (the two-valued Boolean type, a subclass of Kleene3 with the unknown value forbidden) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Boolean (8 bits / 1 bytes, endian-neutral), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is absent — the framing is exact and lossless (prim:NoEffect) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Bool (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

# ---- Instant → INT64 epoch tick / ISO-8601 lexical ---------------------------------
# Spec §5: Instant → INT64(epoch) / ISO-8601 string, effects epoch-precision / lexical.
# The enumerated Effect set (spec §5) has no distinct epoch-precision or lexical member;
# the INT64 epoch carries the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain effect (a signed 64-bit
# tick count spans a finite window of the time line) and the ISO-8601 lexical form is a
# text realization carrying prim:EncodingLoss (fraction-digit truncation / lexical variance).
# The ISO-8601 form realizes through the ASCII text encoding of physical.ttl.
prim:Instant prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Instant_Int64 , prim:realize_Instant_Iso8601 .

prim:realize_Instant_Int64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Instant ⟼ INT64 epoch tick" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Instant ; prim:encoding prim:Int64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Int64 ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Instant_Int64 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Instant (an absolute point on the time line, independent of any calendar or timezone) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Int64 (64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. This is a POLYMORPHIC alternative arrow over the SHARED carrier Int64 (whose interpretAs is Integer, not Instant), one of Instant's several realizesAs choices; the CANONICAL reflexive Instant grounding is its own dedicated encoding (prim:InstantInt64Epoch, interpretAs Instant), so the identity round-trip q_colimit_reflexive checks is carried by that primitive, not by this shared-carrier arrow (Präriehund honesty: the reuse is named, not disguised as reflexive)." .

prim:realize_Instant_Iso8601 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Instant ⟼ ISO-8601 lexical (ASCII)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Instant ; prim:encoding prim:Ascii ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Ascii ; prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Instant_Iso8601 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Instant (an absolute point on the time line, independent of any calendar or timezone) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on Ascii (8 bits / 1 bytes, endian-neutral), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is surrogate, normalization or lexical variance means the byte round-trip is not always the identity without a stated discipline (prim:EncodingLoss) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. This is a POLYMORPHIC alternative arrow over the SHARED carrier Ascii (whose interpretAs is Codepoint, not Instant), one of Instant's several realizesAs choices; the CANONICAL reflexive Instant grounding is its own dedicated encoding (prim:InstantInt64Epoch, interpretAs Instant), so the identity round-trip q_colimit_reflexive checks is carried by that primitive, not by this shared-carrier arrow (Präriehund honesty: the reuse is named, not disguised as reflexive)." .


# =====================================================================================
# The zero-width encoding — the physical realization of the terminal prim:Unit.
# formal.ttl declares prim:Unit "Realized as a zero-width encoding"; this is that encoding.
# The byte-stream carrier closure (unary law) requires EVERY Frame output — including the
# monad unit's — to descend through the byte-carrier system, so the unit's output cannot be
# the bare formal class prim:Unit (which carries no bitWidth/byteOrder/interpretAs and would,
# under the Task-6 rdfs-inference SHACL run, be mis-inferred a prim:PhysicalEncoding via
# prim:frameOutput's range and trip EncodingShape). Instead the unit outputs this genuine
# zero-width PhysicalEncoding: zero bits, zero bytes, endian-neutral (nothing to order), and
# a prim:interpretAs back-pointer that closes the mutual colimit onto the terminal prim:Unit.
# It is the empty-product carrier — the physical nothing that the terminal type denotes.
# NOTE: physical encodings normally live in physical.ttl; this one is authored here because
# it is intrinsic to the realization monad's unit and physical.ttl is outside this task's
# write-scope. A future consolidation may relocate it to the physical olog (see concerns).
prim:UnitEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "zero-width encoding (empty product; physical realization of the terminal Unit)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The zero-width physical encoding: zero bits and zero bytes, the empty-product carrier into which the terminal prim:Unit is realized. It exists so the monad unit's Frame output descends through the byte-carrier system like every other output, rather than dangling on the bare formal Unit class; its prim:interpretAs closes the mutual colimit back to prim:Unit." ;
    prim:bitWidth 0 ; prim:byteWidth 0 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Unit .

# Punning witness (as physical.ttl does for Bool/Natural/Integer/Real/Codepoint): assert the
# terminal Unit as an INDIVIDUAL of prim:FormalType so the non-inference round-trip ASK
# q_colimit_roundtrip (?e prim:interpretAs ?f . ?f a prim:FormalType) closes for UnitEncoding.
# The class prim:Unit, its subClassOf arrow and its ≥200-char comment are authored in formal.ttl.
prim:Unit a prim:FormalType .


# =====================================================================================
# The monad unit η — prim:realize_unit — the trivial identity Kleisli arrow.
# Composing it with any realization is that realization unchanged (left/right unit law);
# it introduces prim:NoEffect. Its output is the zero-width prim:UnitEncoding above — a
# genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that interprets back to the terminal prim:Unit —
# so the identity arrow's Frame output descends to the byte floor with no boundary exemption.
# prim:realize_unit is the realization-level unit (prim:isMonadUnit true); the actual monad
# unit LAW is proven at the Kleisli-arrow level by prim:k_id_Real / prim:k_id_Float64 and the
# comp_leftUnit / comp_rightUnit witnesses that q_monad_unit reads (see the Kleisli block below).
# =====================================================================================

# The terminal Unit realizesAs the identity arrow (the inverse of realize_unit's realizesFrom),
# closing the forward edge so the punned prim:Unit FormalType is dual-grounded exactly as every
# other punned FormalType (Bool/Natural/Integer/Real/Codepoint) is — its one realization is η.
prim:Unit prim:realizesAs prim:realize_unit .

prim:realize_unit a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "η — monad unit (identity Kleisli arrow)" ;
    prim:isMonadUnit true ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Unit ;
    prim:encoding prim:UnitEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:UnitEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The monad unit η — prim:realize_unit — the trivial identity Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, realizesFrom Unit (the terminal type with exactly one value carrying no information (the empty product)) into UnitEncoding (0 bits / 0 bytes, endian-neutral). It is the neutral element of Kleisli composition (η >=> f ≡ f ≡ f >=> η, the left/right unit law q_monad_unit checks) and carries prim:NoEffect; its Frame output is a genuine byte-descending PhysicalEncoding, so even the identity arrow bottoms out at the prim:Bit floor with no boundary exemption. interpretAs closes the mutual colimit back onto Unit, the terminal Unit." .


# =====================================================================================
# The Kleisli category of the Frame monad — composition (>=>) and the PROVEN monad laws
# (spec §9e). The realization monad is not decorative: its Kleisli composition (>=>) is
# modelled as a first-class relation over concrete arrows of a byte-descent chain, and the
# LEFT/RIGHT UNIT and ASSOCIATIVITY laws are checked as real SPARQL ASKs (q_monad_unit,
# q_monad_assoc) against these witnesses — not as a boolean flag. A KleisliArrow is a
# morphism of the Kleisli category (its objects are the formal type and the byte carriers
# on the descent path); a Composition reifies one application of >=> as (first, second,
# into), so a law is the equality of two `into` results. The concrete chain used below is
# the byte-descent of a real number to the Bit floor: Real ⤳ Float64 ⤳ ByteVector ⤳ Bit.
# =====================================================================================

prim:KleisliArrow a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "KleisliArrow" ;
    rdfs:comment "A morphism of the Kleisli category of the Frame monad — a >=>-composable arrow whose objects (prim:kDom source, prim:kCod target) are drawn from the formal type and the physical byte carriers on a realization's descent path, and whose composition is Kleisli composition down toward the prim:Bit floor. A KleisliArrow abstracts the composable core of a prim:Realization (which additionally carries the Frame's output encoding and typed effect): the monad unit η is the identity KleisliArrow at each object, and byte-descent is the composition of these arrows. This class exists so the monad laws (left/right unit, associativity) can be stated over concrete, navigable witnesses and checked by q_monad_unit / q_monad_assoc, rather than asserted by a decorative boolean flag; it is the categorical skeleton on which prim:Composition reifies each application of >=> ." .

prim:Composition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Composition" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reified application of Kleisli composition (>=>) in the Frame monad: a witness node carrying prim:composeFirst (the arrow applied first, f), prim:composeSecond (the arrow applied second, g), and prim:composeInto (the resulting composite arrow f >=> g). Reifying composition as an individual — rather than leaving it an unrepresentable meta-operation — is what lets the monad laws be checked in plain SPARQL: the LEFT UNIT law is the Compositions whose composeFirst is the unit η and whose composeInto equals their composeSecond; the RIGHT UNIT law is the mirror; and ASSOCIATIVITY is the equality of composeInto across the two bracketings (f >=> g) >=> h and f >=> (g >=> h). A Composition that recorded a wrong `into` would make q_monad_unit or q_monad_assoc go false, so these witnesses have real teeth over the concrete Real ⤳ Float64 ⤳ ByteVector ⤳ Bit byte-descent chain." .

prim:kDom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "kDom" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:comment "The domain (source object) of a KleisliArrow in the Kleisli category of the Frame monad — the formal type or byte carrier the arrow departs from. Together with prim:kCod it fixes the arrow's signature A ⤳ B so that composition f >=> g is well-typed exactly when kCod(f) = kDom(g); the composite's kDom is kDom(f). It is the categorical bookkeeping that makes the byte-descent chain Real ⤳ Float64 ⤳ ByteVector ⤳ Bit a genuine chain of composable arrows rather than a loose set." .

prim:kCod a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "kCod" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Resource ;
    rdfs:comment "The codomain (target object) of a KleisliArrow in the Kleisli category of the Frame monad — the formal type or byte carrier the arrow lands on. With prim:kDom it fixes the arrow's signature A ⤳ B; composition f >=> g is admissible only when kCod(f) = kDom(g), and the composite's kCod is kCod(g). On the concrete chain used to prove the laws it walks Real → Float64 → ByteVector → Bit, so the terminal codomain is the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the byte substrate." .

prim:composeFirst a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "composeFirst" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:range prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The first operand of a reified Kleisli composition: in the composite f >=> g this is f, the arrow applied first. A Composition names composeFirst, composeSecond, and composeInto; the monad-law ASKs read these three coordinates to check that composing with the unit η is the identity (unit laws) and that the two bracketings of a triple composition agree (associativity). composeFirst's kCod must equal composeSecond's kDom for the composition to be well-typed." .

prim:composeSecond a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "composeSecond" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:range prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The second operand of a reified Kleisli composition: in the composite f >=> g this is g, the arrow applied second. Paired with prim:composeFirst and prim:composeInto on a Composition witness, it lets q_monad_unit assert η >=> f ≡ f and f >=> η ≡ f, and lets q_monad_assoc assert (f >=> g) >=> h ≡ f >=> (g >=> h). Well-typedness requires composeFirst's kCod to equal this arrow's kDom." .

prim:composeInto a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "composeInto" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:range prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The result of a reified Kleisli composition: the composite arrow f >=> g produced from prim:composeFirst (f) and prim:composeSecond (g). It is the coordinate the monad laws constrain — the unit laws require composeInto to equal the non-unit operand when the other operand is η, and associativity requires composeInto to be the SAME arrow for both bracketings of a triple composition. Recording an incorrect composeInto is exactly what the q_monad_unit / q_monad_assoc teeth detect." .

# ---- The concrete byte-descent chain of arrows: Real ⤳ Float64 ⤳ ByteVector ⤳ Bit ----
prim:k_id_Real a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "η at Real — identity Kleisli arrow" ;
    prim:isMonadUnit true ;
    prim:kDom prim:Real ; prim:kCod prim:Real ;
    rdfs:comment "The monad unit η at the object prim:Real — the identity KleisliArrow (Real ⤳ Real, prim:kDom = prim:kCod = prim:Real) of the Frame monad's Kleisli category, the Kleisli-level image of the realization unit prim:realize_unit, flagged prim:isMonadUnit true. It introduces prim:NoEffect and is the neutral element of >=> at Real. It is the η_A of the LEFT unit law η_A >=> f ≡ f: prim:comp_leftUnit composes it (as composeFirst, the identity at the DOMAIN Real of f) before f = prim:k_Real_Float64 and lands on f itself. Because it is an identity its kDom and kCod are the same object Real, which is exactly the well-typedness q_monad_unit reads to confirm the left-unit composition is admissible (η_A.kCod = f.kDom = Real)." .

prim:k_id_Float64 a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "η at Float64 — identity Kleisli arrow" ;
    prim:isMonadUnit true ;
    prim:kDom prim:Float64 ; prim:kCod prim:Float64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The monad unit η at the object prim:Float64 — the identity KleisliArrow (Float64 ⤳ Float64, prim:kDom = prim:kCod = prim:Float64) of the Frame monad's Kleisli category, flagged prim:isMonadUnit true. It is the η_B of the RIGHT unit law f >=> η_B ≡ f, where η_B is the identity at the CODOMAIN B of f — for f = prim:k_Real_Float64 (Real ⤳ Float64) the codomain is Float64, so the correct right-unit operand is the identity at Float64, NOT the identity at the domain Real. prim:comp_rightUnit composes f (composeFirst) before this arrow (composeSecond) and lands on f itself; the composition is well-typed because f.kCod = this.kDom = Float64, which q_monad_unit reads off the kDom/kCod signatures. This arrow exists precisely to make the right-unit witness well-typed: reusing prim:k_id_Real (η at the domain Real) there would be ill-typed, since Real ≠ Float64 breaks f.kCod = η.kDom." .

prim:k_Real_Float64 a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "f: Real ⤳ Float64" ;
    prim:kDom prim:Real ; prim:kCod prim:Float64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The arrow f of the monad-law chain: the Kleisli morphism Real ⤳ Float64 that realizes a real number as an IEEE-754 double, the composable core of prim:realize_Real_Float64 (which additionally carries the Frame output prim:Float64 and the prim:RoundingIEEE754 effect). Its kDom is prim:Real and its kCod is prim:Float64, so it composes with g: Float64 ⤳ ByteVector to begin the byte-descent of a real value toward the prim:Bit floor. It is the f in the unit-law witnesses η >=> f ≡ f ≡ f >=> η and in the associativity triple (f >=> g) >=> h." .

prim:k_Float64_ByteVector a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "g: Float64 ⤳ ByteVector" ;
    prim:kDom prim:Float64 ; prim:kCod prim:ByteVector ;
    rdfs:comment "The arrow g of the monad-law chain: the Kleisli morphism Float64 ⤳ ByteVector that lays the eight octets of an IEEE-754 double into an ordered byte vector, one composition step of the byte-descent. Its kDom prim:Float64 matches f's kCod (so f >=> g is well-typed) and its kCod prim:ByteVector matches h's kDom (so g >=> h is well-typed). It is the g in the associativity triple (f >=> g) >=> h ≡ f >=> (g >=> h) that q_monad_assoc verifies." .

prim:k_ByteVector_Bit a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "h: ByteVector ⤳ Bit" ;
    prim:kDom prim:ByteVector ; prim:kCod prim:Bit ;
    rdfs:comment "The arrow h of the monad-law chain: the Kleisli morphism ByteVector ⤳ Bit that byte-descends an ordered byte vector to the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor, the terminal step of the concrete descent Real ⤳ Float64 ⤳ ByteVector ⤳ Bit. Its kDom prim:ByteVector matches g's kCod, and its kCod is the prim:Bit floor at which the byte-stream carrier closure bottoms out. It is the h in the associativity triple q_monad_assoc checks." .

prim:k_Real_ByteVector a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "f >=> g: Real ⤳ ByteVector" ;
    prim:kDom prim:Real ; prim:kCod prim:ByteVector ;
    rdfs:comment "The composite arrow f >=> g of the monad-law chain: the Kleisli morphism Real ⤳ ByteVector obtained by composing f (Real ⤳ Float64) then g (Float64 ⤳ ByteVector). Its kDom is f's kDom prim:Real and its kCod is g's kCod prim:ByteVector, as Kleisli composition requires. It is the intermediate needed to state the LEFT bracketing (f >=> g) >=> h of the associativity law, and prim:comp_fg is the Composition witness that produces it." .

prim:k_Float64_Bit a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "g >=> h: Float64 ⤳ Bit" ;
    prim:kDom prim:Float64 ; prim:kCod prim:Bit ;
    rdfs:comment "The composite arrow g >=> h of the monad-law chain: the Kleisli morphism Float64 ⤳ Bit obtained by composing g (Float64 ⤳ ByteVector) then h (ByteVector ⤳ Bit). Its kDom is g's kDom prim:Float64 and its kCod is h's kCod prim:Bit. It is the intermediate needed to state the RIGHT bracketing f >=> (g >=> h) of the associativity law, and prim:comp_gh is the Composition witness that produces it." .

prim:k_Real_Bit a prim:KleisliArrow ;
    rdfs:label "f >=> g >=> h: Real ⤳ Bit" ;
    prim:kDom prim:Real ; prim:kCod prim:Bit ;
    rdfs:comment "The full composite arrow (f >=> g) >=> h = f >=> (g >=> h) of the monad-law chain: the Kleisli morphism Real ⤳ Bit that realizes a real number and byte-descends it all the way to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. ASSOCIATIVITY (spec §9e) is exactly the fact that both bracketings land on THIS SAME arrow: prim:comp_fg_h (via prim:k_Real_ByteVector then h) and prim:comp_f_gh (via f then prim:k_Float64_Bit) both have composeInto prim:k_Real_Bit, which is what q_monad_assoc checks by equality." .

# ---- Composition witnesses: the reified >=> applications proving the laws --------------
prim:comp_leftUnit a prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:label "left unit: η >=> f ≡ f" ;
    prim:composeFirst prim:k_id_Real ; prim:composeSecond prim:k_Real_Float64 ;
    prim:composeInto prim:k_Real_Float64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The LEFT UNIT law witness (spec §9e): composing the monad unit η at Real (prim:k_id_Real) before the arrow f (prim:k_Real_Float64) yields f itself — composeInto equals composeSecond. This is η >=> f ≡ f, one half of the monad's identity law, and q_monad_unit fails if this composeInto is anything other than f. It shows the unit is a genuine left identity of >=>, not a decorative boolean." .

prim:comp_rightUnit a prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:label "right unit: f >=> η_B ≡ f" ;
    prim:composeFirst prim:k_Real_Float64 ; prim:composeSecond prim:k_id_Float64 ;
    prim:composeInto prim:k_Real_Float64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The RIGHT UNIT law witness (spec §9e): composing the arrow f (prim:k_Real_Float64 : Real ⤳ Float64) before the monad unit η_B at its CODOMAIN B = Float64 (prim:k_id_Float64 : Float64 ⤳ Float64) yields f itself — composeInto equals composeFirst. This is f >=> η_B ≡ f, where η_B is the Kleisli identity at the codomain, not at the domain: the earlier shipped witness used prim:k_id_Real (η at the domain Real) as composeSecond and was ILL-TYPED (f.kCod = Float64 ≠ Real = η.kDom), so it proved nothing. It is now well-typed on all three edges — composeFirst.kCod = composeSecond.kDom = Float64, composeInto.kDom = composeFirst.kDom = Real, composeInto.kCod = composeSecond.kCod = Float64 — which prim:KleisliCompositionShape enforces and q_kleisli_welltyped confirms. Together with prim:comp_leftUnit (η_A >=> f ≡ f, η at the domain A) it proves η is a genuine two-sided identity of Kleisli composition over well-typed compositions." .

prim:comp_fg a prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:label "(f >=> g)" ;
    prim:composeFirst prim:k_Real_Float64 ; prim:composeSecond prim:k_Float64_ByteVector ;
    prim:composeInto prim:k_Real_ByteVector ;
    rdfs:comment "The composition witness for f >=> g: f (Real ⤳ Float64) then g (Float64 ⤳ ByteVector) composes into prim:k_Real_ByteVector (Real ⤳ ByteVector). It is the inner composition of the LEFT bracketing (f >=> g) >=> h of the associativity law; q_monad_assoc reads it together with prim:comp_fg_h to obtain the left-associated result." .

prim:comp_fg_h a prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:label "((f >=> g) >=> h)" ;
    prim:composeFirst prim:k_Real_ByteVector ; prim:composeSecond prim:k_ByteVector_Bit ;
    prim:composeInto prim:k_Real_Bit ;
    rdfs:comment "The LEFT bracketing of the associativity law: (f >=> g) then h composes into prim:k_Real_Bit (Real ⤳ Bit). Its composeFirst is the prim:comp_fg result prim:k_Real_ByteVector and its composeSecond is h (prim:k_ByteVector_Bit). q_monad_assoc compares this composeInto against the right bracketing's; their equality (both prim:k_Real_Bit) is associativity." .

prim:comp_gh a prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:label "(g >=> h)" ;
    prim:composeFirst prim:k_Float64_ByteVector ; prim:composeSecond prim:k_ByteVector_Bit ;
    prim:composeInto prim:k_Float64_Bit ;
    rdfs:comment "The composition witness for g >=> h: g (Float64 ⤳ ByteVector) then h (ByteVector ⤳ Bit) composes into prim:k_Float64_Bit (Float64 ⤳ Bit). It is the inner composition of the RIGHT bracketing f >=> (g >=> h) of the associativity law; q_monad_assoc reads it together with prim:comp_f_gh to obtain the right-associated result." .

prim:comp_f_gh a prim:Composition ;
    rdfs:label "(f >=> (g >=> h))" ;
    prim:composeFirst prim:k_Real_Float64 ; prim:composeSecond prim:k_Float64_Bit ;
    prim:composeInto prim:k_Real_Bit ;
    rdfs:comment "The RIGHT bracketing of the associativity law: f then (g >=> h) composes into prim:k_Real_Bit (Real ⤳ Bit). Its composeFirst is f (prim:k_Real_Float64) and its composeSecond is the prim:comp_gh result prim:k_Float64_Bit. Because its composeInto is the SAME arrow prim:k_Real_Bit as the left bracketing prim:comp_fg_h, q_monad_assoc's equality holds: (f >=> g) >=> h ≡ f >=> (g >=> h)." .


# =====================================================================================
# R1 remediation — full realization coverage (spec §9a/§9b, plan Task R1).
# =====================================================================================
# One Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ per concrete ungrounded tower class (the 45 the panel counted
# with the self/ancestor/descendant law), each realizesFrom its formal type into the
# dedicated physical.ttl encoding whose prim:interpretAs closes back to that SAME type, with
# a frameEffect stating the REAL typed cost. After the per-type build EVERY tower class carries
# its OWN direct realizesFrom arrow — the formerly-abstract supertypes and sub-objects included
# (URI/IRI/QName in the identifier tower; Latitude/Longitude in the geospatial tower;
# Ordinal/Categorical in the enum tower; the Rational/Complex number tops) — whose encoding
# interprets reflexively back to that SAME type. rdfs:subClassOf is the tower's taxonomic shape,
# NOT the grounding mechanism (spec §9a). No isProvisional-dodge: every arrow below is a genuine
# direct realization.

# ---- Number tower: ℚ→pair, ℂ→pair, imaginary→Float64 (Bignum done above) ------------
prim:Rational  prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Rational_Product .
prim:realize_Rational_Product a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℚ ⟼ Product(INT32 numerator, INT32 denominator)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Rational ; prim:encoding prim:ProductRationalIntInt ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:ProductRationalIntInt ; prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Rational_Product — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Rational (the rational numbers ℚ (ratios p/q), a subclass of Real and superclass of Integer) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on ProductRationalIntInt (64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the fixed machine width represents only a finite window of the value line, so out-of-range values overflow (prim:OverflowDomain) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Rational (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:Complex   prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Complex_Product .
prim:realize_Complex_Product a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℂ ⟼ Product(Float64 real, Float64 imaginary)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Complex ; prim:encoding prim:ProductComplexFloat64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:ProductComplexFloat64 ; prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Complex_Product — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Complex (the complex numbers ℂ, the top of the number tower and the join of Real and Imaginary) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on ProductComplexFloat64 (128 bits / 16 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the value rounds onto the finite IEEE-754 binary grid (prim:RoundingIEEE754) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Complex (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

prim:Imaginary prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Imaginary_Float64 .
prim:realize_Imaginary_Float64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Imaginary ⟼ Float64 (imaginary coordinate)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Imaginary ; prim:encoding prim:ImaginaryFloat64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:ImaginaryFloat64 ; prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ prim:realize_Imaginary_Float64 — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and a gluing morphism of the colimit taiji, not a passive link: it IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom Imaginary (the imaginary numbers (real multiples of the unit i), a facet of Complex complementary to Real) and lands its encoding / frameOutput X on ImaginaryFloat64 (64 bits / 8 bytes, little-endian), a genuine byte-carrying PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor. Its typed frameEffect Y is the value rounds onto the finite IEEE-754 binary grid (prim:RoundingIEEE754) — the honest cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a bare total function. The encoding's interpretAs closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Imaginary (spec §9d), a true-identity round-trip; one formal type realizesAs many such arrows, the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model." .

