# =====================================================================================
# INTEGRATED per-type fragments (from basicttl/primitives/frags/*.ttl) — the reflexive,
# type-specific colimit grounding of every tower class. Each frag contributes its punning
# witness (prim:X a prim:FormalType), its dual-grounding forward edge (prim:X realizesAs …),
# its type-specific prim:PhysicalEncoding (yang, interpretAs closing reflexively onto X), and
# its prim:Realization (the Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ). The colimit prim:Primitive atoms are
# assembled into taiji.ttl. Bignum/Instant/Decimal use the reflexive frag encodings, superseding
# the old non-reflexive proxies. Merged additively, deduped by URI (rdflib set semantics).
# =====================================================================================

prim:Bag a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: length-prefixed element+multiplicity run → Bag -----
# An UNORDERED, MULTIPLICITY-BEARING, VARIABLE-length carrier, NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous
# product and NOT an order-preserving sequence: a UINT32 cardinality prefix (the number of DISTINCT
# elements) followed by exactly that many contiguous (element-encoding, UINT32 multiplicity-count)
# pairs, all elements of the SAME element type, where the per-element COUNT records how many times
# that element occurs and position carries no meaning. It reuses the reusable prim:ByteVector
# ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl (which byteDescendsTo prim:Octet), whose
# schema-qualified Empty|Link algebra physically holds the (element, count) pairs while the unordered
# MULTISET semantics live in the formal type. Because a bag's cardinality is not pinned, the carrier
# is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1
# the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (≤ 128,
# the ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits), since the total span is the
# cardinality prefix plus the sum of the (member width + count width) pairs and varies with the
# multiset. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 cardinality prefix and every UINT32 multiplicity
# count are little-endian unsigned integers (least-significant octet first). NO prim:signedness is
# declared: signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) and
# does not apply to a bag frame (as prim:List, prim:Set and prim:Tuple likewise omit it — the counts
# are themselves unsigned occurrences but the FRAME as a whole is not a signed scalar).
# prim:interpretAs prim:Bag makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:BagMultisetEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "length-prefixed element+multiplicity run (UINT32 distinct-cardinality prefix + (element, UINT32 count) pairs) · octet-granular · LE → Bag [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Bag ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the bag / multiset: a LENGTH-PREFIXED run of (element-encoding, MULTIPLICITY-COUNT) pairs — a UINT32 distinct-element-cardinality prefix (the machine type prim:Uint32 of physical.ttl, little-endian) followed by exactly that many contiguous (element, UINT32 count) pairs, ALL elements of one element type, where the per-element count records HOW MANY TIMES the element occurs and position is meaningless — laid over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl. The unary law's UNORDERED SET/bag family has its own separately identified alternatives and NEVER supplies sequence order by convention, so the physical run is deliberately given a canonical pair order purely to make the bytes deterministic; the abstract Bag itself remains orderless and its identity is exactly the multiset of (element → multiplicity) bindings. Because a bag's cardinality is not pinned, the carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR — prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet of the code space, cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits; the scalar prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 cap does not bind a variable-length run) — since a bag's total span is the cardinality prefix PLUS the sum of its (member width + count width) pairs and so varies with the multiset. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 cardinality prefix and every UINT32 multiplicity count serialize least-significant octet first; NO prim:signedness is declared because signedness is an integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) inapplicable to a bag frame, exactly as prim:List, prim:Set and prim:Tuple omit it — a multiplicity count is an unsigned occurrence-counting cardinality (like a stream ordinal or block length in the unary law), a distinct coordinate from the bit-width carrier and never a signed scalar. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): every pair is the abutment of TWO floor prim:PhysicalEncodings — the element IS itself the ONE element type's own encoding and the multiplicity IS a prim:Uint32 count occurrence — so a bag of prim:Codepoint lays (prim:Utf8 octet run, UINT32 count) pairs, a bag of prim:Integer lays (prim:Int32 two's-complement word, UINT32 count) pairs, a bag of prim:Triple lays (prim:TripleNTriples line, UINT32 count) pairs, and the bag's bytes are exactly the distinct-cardinality prefix followed by those homogeneous (element, count) pairs abutted in canonical order; because the element type is uniform and both the cardinality and the per-element counts are explicit, there is NO per-element tag and NO terminator, which is precisely why a bag is a homogeneous prim:ByteVector-family run and NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:CompositeCarrier product (mixed member widths, arity without a length prefix). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ordered-vector carrier prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of the cardinality prefix, of every member element, and of every multiplicity count bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Bag is the direct subclass of prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's unordered branch and the PARENT of prim:Set (a Set is exactly a Bag whose every element multiplicity is at most one, so Set adds the distinctness invariant and drops the count field); it is kept distinct from the ordered prim:List / prim:Vector (order and positional duplicates ARE identity), the fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:Tuple (no length prefix, mixed types), and its subtype prim:Set (multiplicities capped at one) — for a Bag only membership-with-multiplicity is identity. TYPED EFFECT: because the abstract Bag has NO order, imposing the canonical serialization order on its (element, count) pairs is a real, typed transformation — two presentations differing only in input pair order map to the SAME bytes, so the realization carries prim:OrderingCanonicalization (the meaningless input pair ordering is deliberately normalized away); it is NOT prim:NoEffect precisely because a genuine transformation (canonical pair ordering) occurs, and NOT prim:EncodingLoss because no MEMBERSHIP and no MULTIPLICITY is lost — the counts are stored explicitly so every element→multiplicity binding is preserved EXACTLY, only the input ordering is normalized. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Bag closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the bag type itself (spec §9d), not onto its subtype prim:Set nor a bare prim:ByteVector, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Bag to the encoding ------------------
# Bag ⟼ length-prefixed element+multiplicity run as a Frame: realizesFrom Bag, encoding/frameOutput
# the multiset encoding above, frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization (a Bag has no intrinsic
# order, so serializing its (element, count) pairs imposes a canonical order — a real transform that
# maps differently-ordered presentations to identical bytes while preserving membership-with-
# multiplicity exactly). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Bag is a
# concrete, directly-realizable unordered multiplicity-bearing collection grounded on the
# len+counts-run-over-ByteVector carrier. This ADDS a second realizesAs edge alongside the
# pre-existing prim:realize_Bag_LenCounts (monad polymorphism).
prim:Bag prim:realizesAs prim:BagRealizesMultiset .

prim:BagRealizesMultiset a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Bag ⟼ length-prefixed element+multiplicity run · octet-granular · LE; frameEffect ordering-canonicalization" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Bag ;
    prim:encoding prim:BagMultisetEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:BagMultisetEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Bag — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Bag (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's unordered branch — the PARENT of prim:Set, since a Set is a Bag whose every multiplicity is at most one) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:BagMultisetEncoding (yang X — the length-prefixed run of (element, multiplicity-count) pairs of the bag's members over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Bag realizesAs this canonical len+counts arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_Bag_LenCounts (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a run-length-flattened repetition (each element emitted multiplicity-many times without a count field), a hash-multiset bucket table, or a sparse count-map are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same bag realization, and this fragment commits to the canonically-ordered (element, count)-pair length-prefixed run as the general deterministic canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization — because a Bag has NO intrinsic order, realizing it into a necessarily ordered byte serialization must impose a canonical order on its (element, count) pairs, so two presentations of the same multiset that differ only in input pair order map to the SAME bytes; the mapping is therefore NOT the identity on presentation order (that meaningless ordering is deliberately discarded), even though the bag's MEMBERSHIP-WITH-MULTIPLICITY is preserved exactly by the explicitly stored counts. This is the SAME typed cost the sibling prim:Set incurs (a Set is a Bag with unit-capped multiplicities, and likewise has no intrinsic order), and it is in deliberate contrast to prim:List, whose realization carries prim:NoEffect because a list's order and positional duplicates ARE its identity and are laid down verbatim. It is distinct from prim:EncodingLoss because no membership and no count is lost — only the input pair ordering is normalized away — and distinct from prim:OverflowDomain, which if it arises belongs to an individual member encoding (an prim:Int32 member's width bound) or to a multiplicity count exceeding UINT32, not to the bag framing itself. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Bag is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type — an unordered multiplicity-bearing collection — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy." .

prim:Bignum a prim:FormalType .

# The dual-grounding forward edge: prim:Bignum realizesAs its own Kleisli/Frame arrow.
prim:Bignum prim:realizesAs prim:Bignum_Realization .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:Bignum — the yang facet.
#     A prim:ByteVector of ≤128 byte occurrences (NOT UINT128), each occurrence a prim:Octet,
#     holding a big-endian two's-complement magnitude. The mutual-colimit back-pointer
#     prim:interpretAs prim:Bignum makes the round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY (spec §9d).
# =====================================================================================

prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ByteVector(≤128 bytes, big-endian two's-complement magnitude) → Bignum" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:BE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Bignum ;
    rdfs:comment "The arbitrary-precision-integer carrier — the yang facet of the Bignum taiji. Subtyping: prim:Bignum is a subclass of prim:Integer in value (it denotes an integer) but differs in realization by refusing a fixed machine width; it is the unbounded escape hatch from the INT8..128 machine words and the sibling of prim:Decimal within the Number tower. Physical realization: an ordered prim:ByteVector of 1..128 byte occurrences, each occurrence exactly one prim:Octet, holding the integer's two's-complement magnitude most-significant octet first (prim:BE, prim:Signed, prim:TwosComplement). It is octet-granular under the two-cap model (spec §9c): prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule while prim:byteCount 128 is the occurrence-count ceiling of the ByteVector cap (up to 1024 bits) — deliberately NOT the UINT128 machine word the panel flagged as a dodge (spec §9b). Byte descent: the ByteVector byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, which byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble, which byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every occurrence bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor and no part of the carrier escapes the byte substrate. Typed effect: a mathematically unbounded value meets this bounded carrier, so a magnitude exceeding 128 octets cannot be held — the honest prim:MayOverflow (unbounded → bounded) cost carried on the gluing Frame. The prim:interpretAs prim:Bignum back-pointer closes the mutual colimit reflexively (physical → interpretAs → formal = Bignum), a true identity rather than the old non-reflexive UINT128 → Natural proxy." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ for prim:Bignum — the gluing morphism (Kleisli/Frame arrow).
#     T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): realizesFrom prim:Bignum, output/encoding the
#     ByteVector encoding above, effect the typed prim:MayOverflow.
# =====================================================================================

prim:Bignum_Realization a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Bignum ⟼ ByteVector(≤128 bytes; may overflow)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Bignum ;
    prim:encoding prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:MayOverflow ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Bignum — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and the gluing morphism of the Bignum colimit taiji. It IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): its prim:realizesFrom is the source formal type prim:Bignum, its prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput is the target byte carrier prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding (the X coordinate, a genuine byte-descending prim:PhysicalEncoding, not a bare formal class), and its prim:frameEffect is the typed prim:MayOverflow (the Y coordinate). The effect is what makes this realization monadic rather than a total function: forcing an arbitrary-precision integer into a bounded prim:ByteVector of at most 128 octets means a sufficiently large magnitude cannot be represented, so the arrow may overflow — the honest typed admission of the unbounded → bounded mismatch (spec §9b), with NO silm:isProvisional dodge and NO reroute to UINT128 (this is a fully resolvable in-scope grounding, not a genuinely undecidable Präriehund gap). Composing this arrow byte-descends to the prim:Bit floor along the physical carrier ladder; the identity of that composition is the monad unit η. It is the concrete descent that the what-is-a-number litmus reads for the Bignum sibling of the Number tower." .

prim:BlankNode a prim:FormalType .

prim:EncodingLoss a prim:Effect .

# ---- (a) the TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding (yang) ---------------------------------------
prim:BlankNodeUtf8LocalId a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 graph-local existential label (_:label) → BlankNode" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:BlankNode ;
    rdfs:comment "The blank-node carrier: an interpreted UTF-8 octet stream holding a graph-local existential label (for example _:b0), the physical home of the Identifier tower's nameless leaf. It is octet-granular — prim:bitWidth 8 records the single-octet UTF-8 code-unit granule and prim:byteCount 128 the ByteVector-family occurrence ceiling of the second width cap (spec §9c, up to 1024 bits) — and prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering to fix. It is modelled with its OWN carrier rather than folded under the URN carrier (prim:UrnUtf8) precisely because a BlankNode is the one identifier that has no Uniform Resource Name: in formal.ttl it is a direct subclass of prim:FormalType, deliberately NOT of prim:URN, so it cannot lawfully borrow the URN grounding (force-fitting it under URN would be the honesty violation the Präriehund rule forbids). Physically each label octet is one prim:Octet (cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255) that prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence to the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the physical.ttl carrier ladder, so the whole label bottoms out at the byte substrate with no escape. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:BlankNode makes yang hold yin: the round-trip physical -> interpretAs -> formal lands back on prim:BlankNode itself, a TRUE IDENTITY rather than a shared-carrier proxy." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ (the Kleisli/Frame gluing arrow) -------------------------------
prim:realize_BlankNode_Utf8LocalId a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "BlankNode ⟼ UTF-8 graph-local label (_:label); freshen-on-merge loss" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:BlankNode ;
    prim:encoding prim:BlankNodeUtf8LocalId ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:BlankNodeUtf8LocalId ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ that carries the formal prim:BlankNode (yin) into its physical prim:BlankNodeUtf8LocalId carrier (yang): it prim:realizesFrom prim:BlankNode, prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput the UTF-8 graph-local-label encoding (the Frame output coordinate X, which descends through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor), and carries exactly one typed prim:EncodingLoss in its Frame's error-or-effect coordinate Y. The effect is genuine, not decorative: a blank node denotes there-exists-something with no global name, and its identity is scoped to a single graph or document, so on graph MERGE the local label must be FRESHENED (an incoming _:b0 renamed) to avoid accidentally identifying two distinct existentials — the byte-level label is therefore not preserved as the identity of the abstract existential across a merge, which is precisely the surrogate/relabelling loss prim:EncodingLoss names (the same effect the Codepoint and URN text realizations carry). Because it declares both a byte-descending frameOutput and a typed frameEffect it is a lawful Frame in the unary law's T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) shape — a Kleisli arrow of the realization monad, not a bare total mapping. One formal type may realizesAs many such arrows (monad polymorphism); this is BlankNode's canonical, and only in-scope, realization this iteration." .

prim:BlankNode prim:realizesAs prim:realize_BlankNode_Utf8LocalId .

prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet .

prim:Octet      prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble .

prim:Nibble     prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit .

# Typed-effect witness (idempotent with realization.ttl): assert the effect individual this
# fragment's realization Frame carries, so its Y coordinate is genuinely typed even in
# isolation. The full ≥200-char comment for prim:NoEffect lives in realization.ttl.
prim:NoEffect a prim:Effect .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) TYPE-SPECIFIC prim:PhysicalEncoding for prim:Blob — the yang facet.
#     Octet-granular ByteVector-family carrier; prim:interpretAs prim:Blob makes the
#     colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY. Second width cap (spec §9c): prim:byteCount ≤ 128.
# =====================================================================================
prim:BlobOpaqueByteVector a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ByteVector(≤128 opaque octets) → Blob" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Blob ;
    rdfs:comment "The opaque-binary physical carrier for prim:Blob (the fragment's yang facet): an ordered prim:ByteVector of up to 128 raw octet occurrences holding hexadecimal / serialized-binary content with no interpreted internal structure — the identity carrier the whole binary/opaque tower is built on. It is octet-granular, so prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet width of that granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the occurrence-count ceiling of the SECOND width cap (spec §9c, up to 1024 bits) rather than the scalar bitWidth ≤ 128 cap. prim:byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because uninterpreted bytes carry no cross-octet ordering to fix — endianness is a property of a multi-octet numeric reading, which an opaque Blob deliberately withholds. Subatomically the carrier descends prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, bottoming at the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the ladder, so no opaque payload escapes the byte substrate (Directive 9: the base unit remains bit and byte vectors); an opaque payload wider than 128 occurrences escalates to a prim:Stream / prim:Container per the unary law's stream → block → container → value closure rather than silently truncating. prim:interpretAs prim:Blob closes the mutual colimit reflexively — the round-trip lands back on prim:Blob itself, a true identity (spec §9d), never a proxy formal type." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) prim:Realization — the Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ gluing prim:Blob to its ByteVector
#     carrier. A genuine Frame: realizesFrom + encoding + frameOutput + frameEffect.
#     Concrete type ⇒ its OWN realization (direct grounding, spec §9a); NO isProvisional.
# =====================================================================================
prim:Blob prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Blob_Opaque .

prim:realize_Blob_Opaque a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Blob ⟼ ByteVector(≤128 opaque octets); verbatim byte-identity, NoEffect" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Blob ;
    prim:encoding prim:BlobOpaqueByteVector ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:BlobOpaqueByteVector ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ of the Blob taiji — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom prim:Blob (its yin source), takes prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:BlobOpaqueByteVector as its output coordinate X, and carries prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect as its effect coordinate Y. prim:Blob is a CONCRETE tower type — the root of the binary/opaque tower and the parent of prim:Hash — so it carries this realization of its OWN rather than inheriting one from an ancestor or borrowing one from a realized descendant (spec §9a direct-for-concrete); opaque bytes are a fully resolvable, in-scope carrier, so there is deliberately NO silm:isProvisional dodge here (spec §9b/§9f). The effect is prim:NoEffect precisely because the realization is the byte-IDENTITY: the raw octets are laid into the ByteVector verbatim with no rounding, narrowing, overflow-in-value, or encoding transform, so the abstract opaque value is preserved exactly — this is the 'ByteVector identity' the primitive is named for, and it is what makes the round-trip a reflexive identity rather than a lossy proxy. It is a real Frame, not a bare mapping, and its output descends through the byte-carrier system (ByteVector → Octet → Nibble → Bit) to the width-1 floor with no boundary exemption." .

prim:BoolUint8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT8(0/1) → Bool (one-octet truth code, UEFI/C BOOLEAN convention)" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Bool ;
    rdfs:comment "The type-specific physical encoding of prim:Bool: a single prim:Octet code cell holding exactly one of two values — 0 for false, 1 for true — the UINT8(0/1) grounding spec §5 names first and the UEFI/C BOOLEAN machine-type convention. It declares prim:bitWidth 8 and prim:byteWidth 1 (one whole octet) with prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral, since a single-octet code has no cross-octet ordering; signedness is deliberately omitted because a truth value is neither signed nor unsigned — only two of the octet's 256 ordinals (0 and 1) are used, so no integer sign domain applies. Subatomic byte descent: this octet carrier descends prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit via prim:byteDescendsTo, bottoming at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue-trust-root floor; the same truth value can equally ride a single prim:Bit directly (the sub-octet packed sibling), the octet form being the addressable machine grounding. It references the ISA/UEFI machine-type ladder (BOOLEAN/UINT8) and the carrier ladder in physical.ttl. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Bool closes yang onto yin, so the physical facet holds the formal facet and the round-trip is a true reflexive identity onto prim:Bool, not a shared-carrier proxy." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ: a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad ----------------
# Bool realizesAs this arrow; the arrow realizesFrom Bool into prim:BoolUint8, exposing that
# same encoding as the Frame output and carrying prim:NoEffect (the two truth values map
# bijectively onto {0,1} — no rounding / narrowing / overflow / encoding loss).
prim:Bool prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Bool_Uint8 .

prim:realize_Bool_Uint8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Bool ⟼ UINT8(0/1) · 1B (bijective; no effect)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Bool ;
    prim:encoding prim:BoolUint8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:BoolUint8 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Bool as a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad — the unary byte-frame law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) with T = prim:Bool, X = prim:BoolUint8, and Y = prim:NoEffect. It declares prim:realizesFrom prim:Bool (its source formal type), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:BoolUint8 (its chosen physical target, exposed as the Frame's output coordinate), and prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect. The effect is genuinely NoEffect — the neutral element of effect composition — because the two Boolean truth values map BIJECTIVELY onto the two code states {0, 1}: no rounding, no narrowing, no width-bounded overflow, and no encoding loss is incurred, so the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract truth value. Because its output encoding byte-descends prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, this arrow's Frame output reaches the width-1 prim:Bit floor with no boundary exemption. This is the gluing morphism the colimit-taiji Bool primitive selects (prim:gluedBy), and it is a genuine realization — not an isProvisional placeholder — since prim:Bool is a concrete, fully resolvable leaf type." .

prim:CRS_EpsgUint32 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT32 EPSG code → CRS (coordinate-reference-system registry identifier)" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:CRS ;
    rdfs:comment "The coordinate-reference-system carrier: an unsigned 32-bit EPSG registry code (for example 4326 for WGS-84 geographic, or 3857 for Web-Mercator) identifying the datum, coordinate system and axis order that give every prim:Coordinate, prim:Point, prim:Line and prim:Polygon its meaning. Physically it reuses the exact-width machine type prim:Uint32 of physical.ttl: four whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:byteOrder prim:LE), each octet of cardinality 256 and ordinal range 0..255, byte-descending prim:Uint32 → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit along the physical carrier ladder's prim:byteDescendsTo chain to the width-1 prim:Bit floor — the unary-law byte-stream carrier closure, subatomic to the bit. The width is UINT32 rather than UINT16 because the EPSG/registry code space overruns the 16-bit window (ESRI 102100, community codes to 998999, legacy 900913 all exceed 65535), so a UINT16 encoding would overflow the identifier namespace; the governing ceiling is the fixed-width scalar cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c), so no prim:byteCount is declared (this is a scalar, not a variable-length ByteVector). prim:signedness prim:Unsigned marks that the underlying code is a non-negative index; the mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:CRS is what re-reads those four octets as a coordinate-reference-system rather than as a bare prim:Natural, and it closes the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal reflexively onto prim:CRS (a TRUE IDENTITY, not a proxy). CRS is a concrete leaf of the geospatial tower (rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType with no tower subclasses of its own), so it is realized directly here." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the REAL typed effect.
# =====================================================================================
prim:CRS prim:realizesAs prim:realize_CRS_EpsgUint32 .

prim:realize_CRS_EpsgUint32 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "CRS ⟼ UINT32 EPSG code (exact registry identifier; no numeric loss)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:CRS ;
    prim:encoding prim:CRS_EpsgUint32 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:CRS_EpsgUint32 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ of the CRS colimit slice: a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad that carries the formal prim:CRS type (yin) into its physical prim:CRS_EpsgUint32 encoding (yang), and is itself a Frame — the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). Its prim:realizesFrom is prim:CRS (the source formal type), its prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput are both prim:CRS_EpsgUint32 (the Frame output coordinate X, a genuine byte-descending PhysicalEncoding that bottoms out at prim:Bit via prim:Octet), and its prim:frameEffect is prim:NoEffect (the Frame error|effect coordinate Y). The effect is genuinely NoEffect and not a rounding or overflow cost: an EPSG code is an exact, lossless registry identifier — the datum, ellipsoid, projection parameters and axis order it names are looked up from the registry, not stored in and reconstructed from the 32 bits, so a code round-trips to the identical code with no numeric approximation and no truncation of information. Composing this arrow with the monad unit η leaves it unchanged (left/right unit law); byte-descent is the Kleisli composition of this realization down through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor. There is no silm:isProvisional here — CRS is a fully resolvable type, so its realization is a real arrow, not a hedged one." .

prim:Categorical a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific physical encoding — Categorical as a UINT32 identity-label code.
# =====================================================================================
# The carrier is the exact-width machine type prim:Uint32 of physical.ttl: four whole prim:Octet
# occurrences laid little-endian, each octet of cardinality 256 and ordinal 0..255, descending
# prim:Uint32 → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit along the physical carrier ladder's
# prim:byteDescendsTo chain to the width-1 prim:Bit floor (unary-law byte-stream carrier closure).
# UINT32 (not UINT8/UINT16) is the canonical label width because a categorical vocabulary may hold
# far more than 256 or 65,536 members while staying finite (ISO country/currency registries, MIME-type
# catalogues, the glossary category spaces of the mole-of-glossaries); UINT8/UINT16 are the sibling
# polymorphic facets a small category set may narrow onto (monad fan-out). Fixed-width scalar, so the
# governing ceiling is the scalar cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c); NO prim:byteCount is declared
# (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap, not a scalar's). prim:signedness prim:Unsigned
# because a member label is a non-negative index. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs
# prim:Categorical is what re-reads those four octets as an UNORDERED identity label rather than as a
# bare prim:Natural or the generic prim:Enumeration, making the round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:Categorical_Uint32Index a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT32 identity-label code → Categorical (unordered finite named-member label) [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ;
    prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Categorical ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of an unordered categorical enumeration: an unsigned 32-bit code (UINT32) assigning each explicitly named member of the set a distinct integer LABEL, 32 bits laid across four whole prim:Octet occurrences. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Categorical rdfs:subClassOf prim:Enumeration rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType — it is the UNORDERED complement of prim:Ordinal and a concrete leaf, so it shares the finite-named-set carrier of its parent prim:Enumeration but re-interprets it: where prim:Ordinal demands order-preserving codes (numeric order of codes reproduces member order), Categorical treats the code as an ARBITRARY IDENTITY LABEL on which ONLY EQUALITY is admissible — comparison beyond =/≠ is meaningless and any implied numeric ordering of the codes is SPURIOUS and must not be leaked. It is realized directly here (spec §9a direct-for-concrete) rather than only inheriting the ancestral prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index, because that ancestor encoding interprets back to the generic prim:Enumeration, a DIFFERENT formal type; this type-specific encoding interprets back to prim:Categorical itself so the round-trip is a true identity (spec §9d). WIDTH CHOICE: UINT32 rather than UINT8/UINT16 because a categorical vocabulary may hold far more than 256 or 65,536 members while staying finite (ISO country/currency registries, MIME-type catalogues, the mole-of-glossaries category spaces); the ~4.29-billion-code window labels any in-scope category set, and the narrower widths are the sibling polymorphic facets a small set may commit to. The governing ceiling is the fixed-width scalar cap prim:bitWidth 32 ≤ 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately NO prim:byteCount (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap) — and prim:signedness prim:Unsigned marks the label as a non-negative index (there is no negative member ordinal). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 32 bits occupy exactly four whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:LE — least-significant octet first), each prim:Octet of cardinality 256 and ordinal range 0..255; each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit (equivalently through prim:Byte, which is kept distinct from prim:Octet per the unary law's Byte ≠ Octet), so via the physical.ttl carrier ladder every bit of this encoding bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it (the byte-stream carrier closure, subatomic to the bit). Count 256, ordinal 255 and bit width 128 remain three distinct navigable coordinates per the unary law. NO COMPOSITE MEMBERS: a categorical value is a SINGLE atomic label code, not a product of components, so this is a scalar carrier and not a CompositeCarrier product; the code-to-member mapping (which label denotes which member) is part of the categorical set's identity, held in its schema rather than serialized alongside each value. TYPED EFFECT: the assignment of a distinct label to each member is a BIJECTION onto a chosen finite range, so storing and reading back the label is exact and lossless — its realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect; crucially the realization imposes NO order on a single value (unlike prim:Set's ordering-canonicalization, there is nothing to sort — one categorical value is one code), and it must not fabricate one, which is exactly why the effect is NoEffect and not an ordering effect. It is genuinely NoEffect and not prim:OverflowDomain because the set is finite and its labels are chosen up front to fit the width, so within the type no value overruns the carrier. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Categorical closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Categorical itself (spec §9d), NOT onto the bare prim:Natural (whose UINT32 layout it structurally mirrors), NOT onto its taxonomic supertype prim:Enumeration (whose generic carrier prim:Categorical does NOT depend on for grounding — it carries this own reflexive UINT32 label carrier), and NOT onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the REAL typed effect.
# =====================================================================================
# Categorical ⟼ UINT32 identity-label code as a Frame: realizesFrom Categorical, encoding/frameOutput
# the encoding above, frameEffect the exact-and-lossless prim:NoEffect. The forward dual-grounding edge
# prim:Categorical prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Categorical_Uint32Index is asserted so the fragment is
# self-sufficient for the dual-grounding law when read standalone. A total, well-typed realization — no
# isProvisional dodge, because Categorical is a concrete, directly-realizable unordered-named-set type
# grounded exactly on the UINT32 label carrier.
prim:Categorical prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Categorical_Uint32Index .

prim:realize_Categorical_Uint32Index a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Categorical ⟼ UINT32 identity-label code (exact label bijection; frameEffect NoEffect)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Categorical ;
    prim:encoding prim:Categorical_Uint32Index ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Categorical_Uint32Index ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Categorical — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Categorical (yin, formal.ttl — a finite, explicitly named set of members with NO meaningful order among them, the unordered complement of prim:Ordinal and a concrete leaf subclass of prim:Enumeration) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:Categorical_Uint32Index (yang X — the unsigned 32-bit identity-label code), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through four prim:Octet occurrences to prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. DIRECT GROUNDING (spec §9a direct-for-concrete): prim:Categorical is a concrete leaf and is grounded by THIS, its OWN direct realization — not by any ancestor. Categorical ⊂ Enumeration in formal.ttl is pure taxonomic subtyping (the tower shape), NOT the PRIMARY grounding mechanism; this own arrow seals the round-trip reflexively onto Categorical, whereas the generic Enumeration carrier interprets back to the different type Enumeration. STEP B additionally materialises the ancestral grounding, so Categorical IS ALSO grounded through its realized ancestor prim:Enumeration by the real, resolvable path prim:agp_Categorical_Enumeration (via the transport prim:rt_sub_Categorical_Enumeration landing in rho(Enumeration) = prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index); the direct grounding is unchanged and the also-grounded-through-ancestor fact is now a genuine edge (dual-grounded two ways). WHAT CATEGORICAL ADDS OVER ITS PARENT is a CONSTRAINT on how the shared UINT32 codes are read, not a new carrier: the codes are arbitrary identity labels admitting only equality, so the realization must not leak a spurious order (its sibling prim:Ordinal instead demands order-preserving codes) — that discipline is a facet of this realization, riding on the same 32-bit code space. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Categorical realizesAs this canonical UINT32 label arrow; a UINT8 code (for a category set of ≤256 members), a UINT16 code (≤65,536 members), or a UINT64 code are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same categorical realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the UINT32 label as Categorical's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — the assignment of a distinct label to each named member is a bijection onto a finite range, so the realization is exact and lossless; it introduces no rounding (there is no continuum to round), no narrowing, and no overflow (the set is finite and its labels are chosen up front to fit the width), and it imposes no ordering on a single categorical value (there is exactly one code per value, nothing to canonicalize), so prim:NoEffect — the neutral element of effect composition that the monad unit η also carries — is the honest and complete effect. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Categorical is a concrete, directly-realizable unordered-named-set type, not an unresolved abstract supertype; composing this arrow with η leaves it unchanged (left/right unit law), and byte-descent is the Kleisli composition of this realization down through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor." .

prim:Codepoint a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) the type-specific physical encoding of prim:Codepoint ----------------------
# UTF-32 direct scalar — a fixed 4-octet code unit whose 32-bit unsigned value equals the
# Unicode scalar value directly. Fresh frag-local node (distinct from the physical.ttl
# prim:Utf32) so this fragment is self-contained and merges additively. Fixed-width SCALAR:
# governed by the prim:bitWidth <= 128 cap (spec §9c), so NO prim:byteCount is declared (that
# is the ByteVector-family cap). prim:signedness prim:Unsigned records the honest physical fact
# that the underlying UTF-32 code unit is an unsigned 32-bit machine cell (the UINT32 kind),
# even though it prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint (a scalar identity), not prim:Natural.
prim:CodepointUtf32 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-32 (fixed 4B · LE, scalar value) → Codepoint [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of prim:Codepoint: a UTF-32 encoding — a single fixed-width 32-bit code unit, 4 octets little-endian, whose unsigned numeric value IS the Unicode scalar value (U+0000..U+10FFFF, surrogate range D800..DFFF excluded). SUBTYPING/POSITION: Codepoint is a concrete atom of the Text tower and a direct subclass of prim:FormalType; prim:Grapheme (a cluster = a base codepoint plus combining marks) and prim:String (a sequence of codepoints) are its SIBLINGS, not its subtypes, because a cluster and a sequence are built from codepoints by composition rather than by subtyping — so this encoding grounds the codepoint atom itself, not the composite text units above it. Among the Unicode transformation formats UTF-32 is chosen as the type-specific canonical carrier precisely because its fixed 32-bit code unit equals the scalar value directly (UTF-8's 1..4-octet and UTF-16's 2-or-4-octet forms encode the same scalar variably); it is therefore the tightest, non-composite grounding. PHYSICAL/SUBATOMIC DESCENT: the 32 bits occupy exactly 4 whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:LE — least-significant octet first); each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so via the physical.ttl carrier ladder every bit bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue-trust-root floor with nothing below it. A codepoint is atomic, so there are NO member encodings to descend into (unlike a composite String, which lays a sequence of these into an octet stream). WIDTH CAP: it is a fixed-width SCALAR encoding ceilinged by prim:bitWidth 32 <= 128 (spec §9c), so no prim:byteCount (the ByteVector-family cap) is declared; prim:signedness prim:Unsigned records that the underlying carrier is an unsigned 32-bit machine cell (the ISA/UEFI UINT32 kind of physical.ttl). EFFECT: mapping the abstract Unicode scalar through any concrete transformation format admits surrogate-pair and normalization-form edge cases, so its realization Frame carries prim:EncodingLoss. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Codepoint closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Codepoint itself (not onto prim:Natural via a shared UINT32 carrier), making the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet a TRUE IDENTITY." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ: a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad ---------------
# Codepoint realizesAs this arrow; the arrow realizesFrom Codepoint into prim:CodepointUtf32,
# exposing that same encoding as the Frame output and carrying prim:EncodingLoss (the honest
# surrogate/normalization cost of any abstract-scalar-through-concrete-format realization).
prim:Codepoint prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32Scalar .

prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32Scalar a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Codepoint ⟼ UTF-32 (fixed 4B · LE, scalar value); frameEffect encoding-loss" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Codepoint ;
    prim:encoding prim:CodepointUtf32 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:CodepointUtf32 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Codepoint — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary byte-frame law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) with T = prim:Codepoint, X = prim:CodepointUtf32, and Y = prim:EncodingLoss. It is not a passive link but IS a Frame: prim:realizesFrom prim:Codepoint (its source, yin), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:CodepointUtf32 (its target X, yang — the fixed 32-bit UTF-32 scalar carrier, exposed as the Frame's output coordinate), and prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss (its Y — the honest typed cost that forcing an abstract Unicode scalar through a concrete transformation format admits surrogate-pair handling and normalization-form differences, so the byte round-trip is not unconditionally the identity on the abstract value without a stated normalization discipline). MONAD POLYMORPHISM: prim:Codepoint realizesAs many arrows — UTF-8 (variable 1..4 octets), UTF-16 (2 or 4 octets, LE or BE), and this UTF-32 direct-scalar arrow; this fragment commits to the UTF-32 arrow as the codepoint's canonical, fixed-width, numerically-direct grounding whose code unit equals the scalar value. Because its output X byte-descends prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit via the physical carrier ladder, this arrow's Frame output reaches the width-1 prim:Bit floor with no byte-stream-closure exemption; X is a real byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding, never a bare formal class. This is the gluing morphism the colimit-taiji Codepoint primitive selects (prim:gluedBy), and it is a genuine realization — not an isProvisional placeholder — since prim:Codepoint is a concrete, fully resolvable leaf type." .

prim:Complex a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: Product(Float64 real, Float64 imaginary) → ℂ -------
# A categorical PRODUCT carrier, NOT a scalar and NOT a homogeneous ByteVector: two same-WIDTH but
# distinct-ROLE prim:Real members (real part, then imaginary part) laid contiguously. It reuses the
# reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier and the IEEE-754 machine-type ladder of physical.ttl
# — each member IS a prim:Float64 encoding (bitWidth 64, IEEE-754 double, LE) — so the pair is a
# fixed-width scalar-family product of total bitWidth 128, exactly meeting the scalar cap
# prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c, the two-64-bit-component ceiling); it declares NO prim:byteCount
# (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap, which this fixed-arity product is not) and NO
# prim:signedness (an integer facet; each member's ± sign is its own prim:IEEE754Sign field, not the
# integer Signed/Unsigned facet). prim:alignment 8 packs each Float64 member on its natural 8-byte
# boundary; prim:padding 0 records that the two equal-width members abut with no filler.
# prim:interpretAs prim:Complex makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:ComplexProductEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(Float64 real, Float64 imaginary) · 16B · LE · IEEE-754 → Complex [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 128 ;
    prim:byteWidth 16 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ;
    prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Complex ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the complex numbers ℂ: a categorical PRODUCT of two IEEE-754 binary64 (Float64) member encodings — the real part first, then the imaginary part — laid contiguously little-endian (least-significant octet first) over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl, for a total prim:bitWidth 128 and prim:byteWidth 16, aligned to the 8-byte Float64 boundary (prim:alignment 8) with no filler (prim:padding 0). MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): each of the two members IS a prim:Float64 encoding from the IEEE-754 float ladder — prim:bitWidth 64 partitioned as one prim:IEEE754Sign bit + an 11-bit prim:IEEE754Exponent field + a 52-bit prim:IEEE754Mantissa significand (1+11+52 = 64), prim:byteOrder prim:LE — each occupying 8 whole prim:Octet occurrences; the fixed arity two (a real coordinate and an imaginary coordinate) is part of the encoding's identity and carries no length prefix, which is exactly why a Cartesian pair is a prim:CompositeCarrier and NOT a homogeneous variable-length prim:ByteVector counted by prim:byteCount. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the product carrier prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each of the 16 constituent octets (8 per member) descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of both Float64 members bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (the byte-stream carrier closure) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Complex is the TOP of the number tower and the JOIN of its two axes — prim:Real ⊂ prim:Complex (a Real is a Complex whose imaginary member is zero) and prim:Imaginary ⊂ prim:Complex (an Imaginary is a Complex whose real member is zero) — and this pair-of-Float64 carrier is precisely that join made physical: the real member is the same IEEE-754 double that grounds prim:Real, the imaginary member the same double that grounds prim:Imaginary, so the composite reuses the real-axis carrier twice rather than inventing a new scalar. TYPED EFFECT: mapping the continuum of each real and imaginary coordinate onto the finite grid of representable doubles is lossy, so the realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the same finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real Float64 carries, now on both coordinates). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Complex closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto ℂ itself (spec §9d), not onto a shared proxy carrier nor onto the prim:Real of a single member, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Complex to the encoding ---------------
# ℂ ⟼ Product(Float64,Float64) as a Frame: realizesFrom Complex, encoding/frameOutput the product
# encoding above, frameEffect the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the real typed cost of the two
# IEEE-754 double members). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Complex
# is a concrete composite type directly grounded on the pair-of-doubles carrier. The forward
# dual-grounding edge prim:Complex prim:realizesAs prim:ComplexRealizesProduct is asserted so the
# fragment is self-sufficient for the dual-grounding law when read standalone.
prim:Complex prim:realizesAs prim:ComplexRealizesProduct .

prim:ComplexRealizesProduct a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: ℂ ⟼ Product(Float64 real, Float64 imaginary) · 16B · LE; frameEffect IEEE754-rounding" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Complex ;
    prim:encoding prim:ComplexProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:ComplexProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Complex — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Complex (yin, formal.ttl, the top of the number tower and join of the real and imaginary axes) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:ComplexProductEncoding (yang X — the real·imaginary product of two Float64 members), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier to prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Complex realizesAs this canonical Cartesian-pair arrow; a wider pair (two Float128 members) or a polar (magnitude, angle) pair, or an interleaved 2×float byte-vector, are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same complex realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the Float64-pair Cartesian form as ℂ's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 — each of the two members maps its real coordinate from the continuum ℝ onto the finite grid of representable IEEE-754 doubles, so all but exactly-representable values round to the nearest grid point; the effect is the SAME finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real Float64 realization carries, now borne on both the real and imaginary coordinates. Because the effect is a genuine non-neutral member (not prim:NoEffect), it records that realizing a complex number is never free — exactly why realization is monadic rather than a bare total function. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Complex is a concrete, directly-realizable composite number type, not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:Coordinate a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: IEEE-754 double (one axis position) → Coordinate ----
# Scalar fixed-width machine type; governed by the scalar width cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c),
# NOT the ByteVector byteCount cap — so no prim:byteCount is declared (this is a fixed-width scalar,
# not a variable-length ByteVector-family carrier, and certainly not the Product carrier of a Point
# tuple). No prim:signedness is declared either: prim:signedness is the integer-representation facet
# (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural), which does not apply to a float; the ± sign that lets an axis
# value straddle zero (latitude −90..+90, longitude −180..+180, signed elevations/offsets) is carried
# instead by the prim:IEEE754Sign field below. It reuses the physical.ttl machine-type kinds
# (prim:IEEE754Sign/Exponent/Mantissa), the byte-order individual prim:LE, and the carrier ladder to
# the prim:Bit floor — and its prim:interpretAs targets prim:Coordinate, which makes the colimit
# round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY onto Coordinate itself (not onto the bare prim:Real whose Float64 layout
# it structurally mirrors).
prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "IEEE-754 double (Float64 · 8B · LE) as one axis position → Coordinate [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ;
    prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 11 ;
    prim:mantissaBits 52 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Coordinate ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of a geospatial coordinate: a single IEEE-754 binary64 (double-precision) encoding holding ONE scalar position along ONE axis of a coordinate reference system, 64 bits laid out as one prim:IEEE754Sign bit + an 11-bit prim:IEEE754Exponent field (dynamic range) + a 52-bit prim:IEEE754Mantissa significand field (precision), so 1+11+52 = 64 = prim:bitWidth, 8 whole octets = prim:byteWidth. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Coordinate rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType and is the PARENT of prim:Latitude and prim:Longitude (each a Coordinate restricted to a bounded angular axis); it is realized directly here as a Real-tower double because a coordinate value IS a real number on one axis. Its two taxonomic subtypes prim:Latitude and prim:Longitude are NOT grounded through this ancestor: each carries its OWN direct realization onto its OWN reflexive carrier (prim:realize_Latitude_Float64 → prim:LatitudeFloat64, prim:realize_Longitude_Float64 → prim:LongitudeFloat64, each interpretAs closing onto itself), with the ±90° / ±180° bounds and prime-meridian/datum facets stated on those own arrows (spec §9a). MEMBER ROLE (the Product(Real…) hint, composite grounding, unary law): a Coordinate is NOT itself the coordinate tuple — the tuple (a whole position) is a Product(Real…) that lives one level up as the prim:Point geometry (physical.ttl prim:PointWkb), a fixed-arity product of Coordinates; this scalar encoding is precisely the Real-valued COMPONENT MEMBER that such a product concatenates (an x, a y, an elevation, a latitude, a longitude), so the Point carrier reuses this coordinate-axis carrier for each of its members rather than inventing a new scalar. Modelling the axis itself as a product would falsely conflate the coordinate with the position (Präriehund: name the fork, do not force-fit), so it stays scalar. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 64 bits occupy exactly 8 whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:LE — least-significant octet first); each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so via the physical.ttl carrier ladder every bit of this encoding bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it (the byte-stream carrier closure). It is a fixed-width SCALAR encoding, ceilinged by the scalar cap prim:bitWidth 64 ≤ 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately NO prim:byteCount (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap) and NO prim:signedness (an integer facet; the ± sign of the axis value is the prim:IEEE754Sign field, not the integer Signed/Unsigned facet). TYPED EFFECT: mapping a continuous axis position (a real coordinate) onto the finite grid of representable IEEE-754 doubles is lossy, so its realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754 — the SAME finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real Float64 carries, now borne on a coordinate axis (this is the physical source of geodetic coordinate round-off). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Coordinate closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Coordinate itself (spec §9d), NOT onto prim:Real (whose Float64 it structurally mirrors) and NOT onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Coordinate to the encoding ------------
# Coordinate ⟼ Float64(one axis position) as a Frame: realizesFrom Coordinate, encoding/frameOutput
# the encoding above, frameEffect the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the real typed cost of the
# IEEE-754 double axis value). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because
# Coordinate is a concrete, directly-realizable scalar geospatial type grounded on the Real-axis
# double carrier. The forward dual-grounding edge prim:Coordinate prim:realizesAs
# prim:CoordinateRealizesFloat64 is asserted so the fragment is self-sufficient for the
# dual-grounding law when read standalone. Latitude/Longitude are NOT covered through this
# ancestor — each carries its OWN direct realization + reflexive carrier below.
prim:Coordinate prim:realizesAs prim:CoordinateRealizesFloat64 .

prim:CoordinateRealizesFloat64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Coordinate ⟼ IEEE-754 double (Float64 · 8B · LE) axis position; frameEffect IEEE754-rounding" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Coordinate ;
    prim:encoding prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Coordinate — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Coordinate (yin, formal.ttl — a single scalar position along one axis of a CRS, the atom of the geospatial tower and parent of prim:Latitude / prim:Longitude) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding (yang X — the single IEEE-754 double holding the axis value), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through 8 prim:Octet occurrences to prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. DIRECT GROUNDING (spec §9a): this arrow directly grounds prim:Coordinate onto its own reflexive carrier. Its taxonomic subtypes prim:Latitude and prim:Longitude do NOT ride this ancestor: each carries its OWN direct realization onto its OWN reflexive encoding (prim:realize_Latitude_Float64 → prim:LatitudeFloat64; prim:realize_Longitude_Float64 → prim:LongitudeFloat64), their bounded angular ranges stated as facets on those own arrows rather than borrowed here. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Coordinate realizesAs this canonical Float64 axis arrow; a wider Float128 axis, a narrowed Float32 (which would add prim:NarrowingLoss), or a fixed-point/base-10 angular form are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same coordinate realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the Float64 axis position as Coordinate's canonical facet, the same machine type prim:Real grounds onto (a coordinate IS a real number on one axis). TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 — the axis position maps from the continuum onto the finite grid of representable IEEE-754 doubles, so all but exactly-representable values round to the nearest grid point; because the effect is a genuine non-neutral member (not prim:NoEffect) it records that realizing a coordinate is never free — the honest physical origin of geodetic round-off, and exactly why realization is monadic rather than a bare total function. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Coordinate is a concrete, directly-realizable scalar geospatial type, not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:Currency a prim:FormalType .

# Forward dual-grounding edge (FormalType → Realization): prim:Currency realizesAs this
# fragment's canonical Kleisli arrow, so the type is dual-grounded on ITSELF (direct-for-concrete
# half of spec §9a) in addition to inheriting the realized prim:UnitOfMeasure ancestor grounding.
prim:Currency prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Currency_Iso4217Alpha .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific PhysicalEncoding — ISO-4217 alphabetic code as a fixed 3×CHAR8 run
# =====================================================================================
# A fixed-arity, fixed-length composite of THREE prim:Char8 member encodings — the three
# upper-case Latin letters of an ISO-4217 alphabetic currency code (US-D, EU-R, JP-Y). It
# reuses the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl and the prim:Char8
# UEFI 8-bit character machine type (bitWidth 8, one Octet each). Total bitWidth 24, byteWidth 3,
# ceilinged by the SCALAR cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c); it declares NO prim:byteCount
# (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap, which this fixed 3-letter code is not).
# prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral because each CHAR8 member is a single Octet with no
# cross-octet ordering — the three-letter SEQUENCE order is positional identity, not endianness.
# prim:interpretAs prim:Currency makes the mutual-colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY (spec §9d)
# rather than a proxy landing on prim:Codepoint as a bare prim:Char8 would.
prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "3×CHAR8 ISO-4217 alphabetic code · 3B · endian-neutral → Currency [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 24 ;
    prim:byteWidth 3 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:alignment 1 ;
    prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Currency ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet (yang) of prim:Currency: an ISO-4217 alphabetic currency code — three upper-case Latin letters such as USD, EUR, or JPY — realized as a fixed-arity composite of THREE prim:Char8 member encodings laid contiguously over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl, for a total prim:bitWidth 24 and prim:byteWidth 3, tightly packed at prim:alignment 1 with prim:padding 0. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Currency is a subclass of prim:UnitOfMeasure (a currency IS the reference magnitude for the dimension of monetary value, just as the metre is the reference magnitude for length), and through it a subclass of prim:FormalType in the Quantity tower; a monetary quantity is a magnitude tagged with one of these codes. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law — the members are themselves floor carriers, turtles all the way down): each of the three positions IS a prim:Char8 encoding from physical.ttl (prim:bitWidth 8, prim:byteWidth 1, prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral, the UEFI 8-bit character machine type), occupying exactly one prim:Octet and itself interpreting to a prim:Codepoint; the fixed arity three is part of this encoding's identity and carries no length prefix, which is why the code is a prim:CompositeCarrier product and NOT a homogeneous variable-length prim:ByteVector counted by prim:byteCount. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the product carrier prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each of the three constituent octets descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit along the ladder edges authored in physical.ttl, so every one of the 24 bits bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (the byte-stream carrier closure) with nothing beneath it. prim:byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because a CHAR8 member is a single octet with no cross-octet ordering — the letter SEQUENCE order (first, second, third) is positional identity, not byte-order endianness. TYPED EFFECT: a fixed three-letter code is an exact, lossless, bijective label — each admitted code names exactly one currency and reads back exactly — so the realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect; the fact that a currency's EXCHANGE-RATE conversion factors against other currencies are time-varying is a separate documented facet of any currency-conversion arrow, NOT a representational loss on the code itself. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Currency closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Currency itself (spec §9d), not onto the prim:Codepoint proxy a bare prim:Char8 would land on, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The Realization — the Kleisli/Frame arrow of the realization monad ρ
# =====================================================================================
# T → Frame(output: X = prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha, error|effect: Y = prim:NoEffect): realizesFrom
# the formal source prim:Currency, encoding / frameOutput the physical target X (the 3×CHAR8
# encoding above), frameEffect the typed cost Y. One formal type realizesAs many arrows (monad
# polymorphism); this is Currency's canonical alphabetic-code arrow.
prim:realize_Currency_Iso4217Alpha a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Currency ⟼ 3×CHAR8 ISO-4217 alphabetic code · 3B; frameEffect NoEffect" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Currency ;
    prim:encoding prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Currency — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Currency (yin, formal.ttl — a monetary unit of measure, a subclass of prim:UnitOfMeasure for the dimension of monetary value) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha (yang X — the fixed 3×CHAR8 ISO-4217 alphabetic code), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier to prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Currency realizesAs this canonical alphabetic-code arrow; ISO-4217 ALSO defines a three-digit NUMERIC code (840 for USD, 978 for EUR, 392 for JPY) which fits in a single unsigned UINT16 (values 0..999) interpreting reflexively back to prim:Currency — that is the sibling polymorphic realization of the same formal type — and a monetary conversion that rescales an amount into a commensurable currency at a time-varying exchange rate is a different arrow again; one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the 3×CHAR8 alphabetic code as Currency's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — assigning a currency its fixed three-letter (or three-digit) code and reading it back is an exact, lossless, bijective operation with no rounding, narrowing, overflow, ordering-canonicalization, or encoding-loss on the code itself; the neutral element of effect composition. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Currency is a concrete, directly-realizable type (a genuine ISO-4217 code space), not an unresolved abstract supertype. ANCESTRAL GROUNDING (STEP B): beyond this direct grounding, prim:Currency is ALSO grounded through its realized ancestor prim:UnitOfMeasure by the real, resolvable path prim:agp_Currency_UnitOfMeasure, whose prim:ancestralViaTransport prim:rt_sub_Currency_UnitOfMeasure is the rho-image of the subtype arrow and lands in rho(UnitOfMeasure) = prim:UnitOfMeasureUint16Index; so Currency is dual-grounded two ways (direct plus transported), the direct grounding unchanged. prim:realizesFrom back-links the arrow to prim:Currency so its source is directly navigable, and prim:encoding = prim:frameOutput fixes the single canonical target carrier." .

prim:DateInt32DaysEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT32 (days since 1970-01-01 epoch) → Date" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ;
    prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Date ;
    rdfs:comment "The calendar-date carrier of the Temporal tower: a signed two's-complement INT32 (the prim:Int32 machine type of physical.ttl) counting whole DAYS from the proleptic-Gregorian epoch 1970-01-01, little-endian. It is a day count rather than an instant tick precisely because a prim:Date subtypes prim:FormalType directly and NOT prim:Instant — a Date is a timezone-free civil coordinate naming a whole day, so the same Date denotes different absolute instants in different zones and sub-day resolution is meaningless to it. This is a fixed 32-bit SCALAR (the scalar cap prim:bitWidth <= 128 governs it, so no prim:byteCount ByteVector cap is declared); it is not a composite, so it has no member encodings. Subatomically it descends through the carrier ladder as four prim:Octet occurrences, each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo the width-1 prim:Bit floor — thirty-two bits, two's-complement, so date-before-epoch values occupy the negative half of the window. The signed 32-bit day window spans roughly ±5.88 million years about 1970, and dates outside that finite span cannot be represented — the honest width-bounded typed cost carried on the realizing Frame as prim:OverflowDomain. prim:interpretAs prim:Date closes the mutual-colimit back-pointer reflexively at the Date level (a TRUE identity, not the prim:Integer a bare INT32 would land on), so yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The Realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad (the gluing morphism).
# =====================================================================================
# prim:Date realizesAs this arrow (the dual-grounding forward edge); the arrow itself IS a
# Frame: prim:realizesFrom its formal source prim:Date, prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput the
# INT32-days encoding above (the Frame output X, which descends to the byte floor), and
# prim:frameEffect the REAL typed effect Y. Realizing an unbounded calendar (the abstract
# Date line runs unboundedly in both directions) into a bounded fixed-width day count is not
# free: it costs the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain effect — that cost is exactly why the
# realization is a monadic Kleisli arrow and not a bare total function.
prim:Date prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Date_Int32Days .

prim:realize_Date_Int32Days a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Date ⟼ INT32 days-since-epoch · 4B · LE" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Date ;
    prim:encoding prim:DateInt32DaysEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:DateInt32DaysEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Date — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and the gluing morphism carrying the Temporal-tower type from the formal olog (yin) into the physical olog (yang). It IS a Frame in the unary law's sense T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): prim:realizesFrom prim:Date is its source, prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput both name prim:DateInt32DaysEncoding (the output coordinate X, a genuine byte-descending prim:PhysicalEncoding — four octets to the prim:Bit floor), and prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain is the typed error/effect coordinate Y. The effect is real, not decorative: the abstract calendar line is unbounded in both directions, but a signed INT32 day count represents only a finite ±5.88-million-year window about the 1970-01-01 epoch, so a date beyond that window overflows the fixed carrier — the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain cost. That non-trivial cost is precisely what makes realization monadic (a Kleisli arrow into Frame) rather than a bare lossless function; prim:Date realizesAs this arrow, dual-grounding the formal type, and composing this arrow with the monad unit η leaves it unchanged (the left/right unit law), byte-descending through prim:Octet to prim:Bit." .

prim:DateTime a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) type-specific physical encoding — Product(INT64 epoch, INT16 tz-offset) -----
prim:DateTimeEpochZoned a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(INT64 epoch-tick, INT16 tz-offset-minutes) → DateTime" ;
    prim:bitWidth 80 ; prim:byteWidth 10 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ; prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:DateTime ;
    rdfs:comment "The civil date-and-time carrier: a categorical Product over prim:CompositeCarrier of two two's-complement components laid contiguously little-endian — a signed prim:Int64 epoch-tick member (8 octets; ticks since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) followed by a signed prim:Int16 timezone-offset-in-minutes member (2 octets) — for a total prim:bitWidth 80, prim:byteWidth 10, aligned to the 8-byte boundary of its widest component with prim:padding 0. The INT16 offset is the load-bearing timezone facet named in the DateTime formal comment: it is what pins the civil naming to an absolute point on the time line, so its presence distinguishes a zoned DateTime from a floating local coordinate. Subatomically the whole product descends through prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet and thence prim:Nibble to the width-1 prim:Bit floor (10 octets = 20 nibbles = 80 bits); each member is itself an octet-granular machine encoding (prim:Int64 = 8 octets, prim:Int16 = 2 octets) whose bytes bottom out at Bit, so no coordinate escapes the byte substrate. prim:interpretAs prim:DateTime closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY — the round-trip physicalFacet -> interpretAs lands back on prim:DateTime, not on prim:Integer as a bare INT64 would — and the finite signed-64-bit epoch window is the honest, width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain cost the gluing Frame carries." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad ----------------
prim:DateTime prim:realizesAs prim:realize_DateTime_EpochZoned .

prim:realize_DateTime_EpochZoned a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "DateTime ⟼ Product(INT64 epoch, INT16 tz-offset)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:DateTime ;
    prim:encoding prim:DateTimeEpochZoned ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:DateTimeEpochZoned ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The DateTime realization ρ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad gluing the formal type prim:DateTime (yin, Tower 4 Temporal) to the physical encoding prim:DateTimeEpochZoned (yang). It IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): prim:realizesFrom names its source formal type prim:DateTime; prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput name its target output X = the INT64-epoch + INT16-tz Product encoding (a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding, never a bare formal class); and prim:frameEffect names the typed cost Y = prim:OverflowDomain, because the signed 64-bit epoch tick represents only a finite window of the unbounded time line, so instants outside that window wrap, saturate, or trap — the honest, exactly-known cost of committing an abstract civil moment to a fixed-width carrier. This is a real realization, not a silm:isProvisional dodge: DateTime is a resolvable, in-scope Temporal type (spec §9b), realized directly onto its OWN reflexive carrier whose prim:interpretAs targets prim:DateTime, so the colimit round-trip is a true identity rather than a reroute to a shared proxy such as INT64→Integer or ASCII→Codepoint." .

prim:Decimal a prim:FormalType .

# The dual-grounding forward edge: prim:Decimal realizesAs its own Kleisli/Frame arrow.
prim:Decimal prim:realizesAs prim:Decimal_Realization .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:Decimal — the yang facet.
#     A packed-BCD (binary-coded-decimal) prim:ByteVector of ≤128 byte occurrences: each
#     octet holds two base-10 digits as two prim:Nibble half-octets, with a sign nibble and
#     a decimal scale (exponent). It is a GENUINE base-10 numeric carrier — NOT the lexical
#     ASCII text form — so prim:interpretAs prim:Decimal makes the round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY
#     (spec §9d), closing back onto Decimal rather than onto Codepoint the way the shared
#     prim:Ascii proxy does.
# =====================================================================================

prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ByteVector(≤128 bytes, packed BCD coefficient + sign nibble + base-10 scale) → Decimal [type-specific canonical base-10 carrier]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:BE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:integerRepresentation prim:SignMagnitude ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Decimal ;
    rdfs:comment "The base-10 fixed-point carrier — the yang facet of the Decimal taiji, and the panel-mandated §9d fix that replaces the shared prim:Ascii lexical proxy (whose prim:interpretAs closes to prim:Codepoint, i.e. text, not a number). SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Decimal is a subclass of prim:Rational — every terminating decimal is a rational with a power-of-ten denominator — and the exact-decimal sibling of prim:Bignum within the Number tower; it exists precisely because the binary IEEE-754 realizations of prim:Real cannot represent values such as 0.1 exactly, so it keeps a base-10 coefficient and a decimal scale instead of a binary mantissa. PHYSICAL REALIZATION (packed BCD): an ordered prim:ByteVector of 1..128 byte occurrences holding a binary-coded-decimal coefficient most-significant decimal digit first (prim:BE) — each prim:Octet occurrence packs TWO base-10 digits as its two prim:Nibble half-octets, one dedicated sign nibble records ± (prim:Signed via prim:SignMagnitude, a sign-nibble-plus-magnitude scheme, NOT binary two's-complement), and a trailing base-10 scale nibble/octet fixes the decimal point (value = signed coefficient × 10^−scale). It is octet-granular under the two-cap model (spec §9c): prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet = two BCD digits) while prim:byteCount 128 is the occurrence-count ceiling of the ByteVector cap (up to 1024 bits), so it declares NO scalar bitWidth beyond 8 and stays under both caps. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ByteVector byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, each octet byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble (the half-octet that addresses exactly ONE base-10 digit, ordinal 0..9 within the nibble's 0..15 range — the honest reuse of the hexadecimal-digit carrier as a decimal-digit carrier), and each nibble byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every base-10 digit bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor and no part of the carrier escapes the byte substrate. TYPED EFFECT: a Decimal is a finite coefficient paired with a finite decimal scale, so it rounds on the base-TEN grid — exact for values such as 0.1 or 0.25 that binary floats cannot represent, and lossy for irrationals and non-terminating decimals — the honest prim:Base10Precision cost carried on the gluing Frame, distinct from prim:RoundingIEEE754 (binary grid) and from prim:NoEffect (a bounded decimal precision is still a bounded precision). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Decimal closes the mutual colimit reflexively (physical → interpretAs → formal = Decimal), a TRUE IDENTITY that fixes the panel-flagged non-reflexive Decimal → Ascii → Codepoint round-trip at the source (spec §9d)." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ for prim:Decimal — the gluing morphism (Kleisli/Frame arrow).
#     T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): realizesFrom prim:Decimal, output/encoding the
#     packed-BCD ByteVector encoding above, effect the typed prim:Base10Precision.
# =====================================================================================

prim:Decimal_Realization a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Decimal ⟼ ByteVector(packed BCD coefficient + sign + base-10 scale); frameEffect base-10-precision" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Decimal ;
    prim:encoding prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:Base10Precision ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Decimal — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and the gluing morphism of the Decimal colimit taiji, not a passive link. It IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): its prim:realizesFrom is the source formal type prim:Decimal (yin, formal.ttl, prim:Decimal ⊂ prim:Rational), its prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput is the target byte carrier prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding (the X coordinate — a genuine byte-descending prim:PhysicalEncoding, the packed-BCD prim:ByteVector, NOT a bare formal class and NOT the lexical prim:Ascii text proxy), and its prim:frameEffect is the typed prim:Base10Precision (the Y coordinate). MONADIC, NOT A TOTAL FUNCTION: the effect is what makes this realization monadic — forcing a real magnitude into a finite base-10 coefficient-and-scale rounds on the decimal grid; it is exact for terminating decimals such as 0.1 (which binary IEEE-754 cannot hold) and lossy for non-terminating decimals and irrationals, the honest typed cost of a bounded base-10 precision, distinct from the binary prim:RoundingIEEE754 of prim:Real's float arrows. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Decimal realizesAs this canonical packed-BCD arrow; a densely-packed-decimal (DPD) or IEEE-754 decimal32/64/128 form, or the lexical base-10 ASCII string form, are sibling polymorphic choices of the same base-10 realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the packed-BCD ByteVector as Decimal's canonical numeric (non-textual) facet. Composing this arrow byte-descends to the prim:Bit floor along the physical carrier ladder (ByteVector → Octet → Nibble → Bit); the identity of that composition is the monad unit η. It carries NO silm:isProvisional dodge because a native base-10 carrier is a fully resolvable in-scope grounding, not a genuinely undecidable Präriehund gap (spec §9b)." .

prim:DimensionUint8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT8 dimensional-registry index → Dimension" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Dimension ;
    rdfs:comment "The physical-dimension carrier (yang facet of the Dimension taiji): a single unsigned UINT8 index into the registry of base-dimension exponent vectors — length, mass, time, and their integer-exponent products such as length·time⁻¹ for velocity or mass·length²·time⁻² for energy. Subatomically it is exactly one prim:Octet (cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255), so its byte descent runs Octet → Nibble → Bit along the prim:byteDescendsTo ladder of physical.ttl and bottoms at the width-1 prim:Bit floor; being a scalar it has no member encodings. Its machine-type kind is prim:signedness prim:Unsigned — the same non-negative UINT8 kind that backs prim:Natural — here reinterpreted as a dimension index rather than a count, which is precisely why its prim:interpretAs is prim:Dimension and NOT prim:Natural: the mutual-colimit back-pointer must close reflexively onto the formal type this carrier means (spec §9d). byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because a single octet has no cross-octet ordering. The exponent-vector formula backing a Dimension is recovered by registry lookup on the exact index, so nothing about the value is lost in the encoding — the honest typed cost is prim:NoEffect (see the realization). The 256-slot index bounds the registry cardinality this iteration; a wider index is a deferred physical addition, not a loss on any admitted dimension." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow (the gluing morphism) -------------
# T → Frame(output: X = prim:DimensionUint8, error|effect: Y = prim:NoEffect). One formal
# type realizesAs many arrows (monad polymorphism); this is Dimension's canonical arrow.
prim:Dimension prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Dimension_Uint8Index .

prim:realize_Dimension_Uint8Index a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Dimension ⟼ UINT8 dimensional-registry index" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Dimension ;
    prim:encoding prim:DimensionUint8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:DimensionUint8 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Dimension — the Kleisli/Frame arrow of the realization monad that glues the formal type (yin, prim:Dimension of the Quantity tower, a direct subclass of prim:FormalType classifying which units are mutually convertible: two quantities add or compare only when their Dimensions match) to its physical carrier (yang, prim:DimensionUint8). As a Frame it is the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): its prim:frameOutput X is prim:DimensionUint8 (the UINT8 index that descends Octet → Nibble → Bit to the prim:Bit floor) and its prim:frameEffect Y is prim:NoEffect. NoEffect is the honest, exact cost here: a dimension is realized as an exact integer LABEL into a registry of base-dimension exponent vectors, and the full dimensional formula is recovered by looking the label up — no rounding, narrowing, encoding-loss, or overflow is incurred on any admitted dimension, so dimensional analysis reduces to lossless integer-vector arithmetic on the indexed formula. This arrow is a genuine, resolvable realization (concrete leaf type), so it carries no silm:isProvisional (spec §9b); prim:realizesFrom back-links it to prim:Dimension so the arrow's source is directly navigable, and prim:encoding = prim:frameOutput fixes the single canonical target carrier." .

prim:Duration a prim:FormalType .

# The dual-grounding forward edge: prim:Duration realizesAs its own Kleisli/Frame arrow.
prim:Duration prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Duration_Nanos .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:Duration — the yang facet.
#     A signed 64-bit two's-complement count of a fixed nanosecond tick, little-endian.
#     The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Duration makes the round-trip a
#     TRUE IDENTITY (§9d), unlike the shared prim:Int64 (interpretAs prim:Integer) a naive
#     reuse of the machine word would give.
# =====================================================================================

prim:DurationInt64Nanos a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT64 · 8B · LE · two's-complement nanosecond tick count → Duration" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ;
    prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteCount 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Duration ;
    prim:uefiName "INT64" ;
    rdfs:comment "The type-specific duration carrier — the yang facet of the Duration taiji and the temporal grounding of the Temporal tower's Duration leaf. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Duration is a direct concrete subclass of prim:FormalType in the Temporal tower — an UNANCHORED length of temporal displacement with no position on the time line, precisely the difference of two prim:Instant points; it is distinct from prim:Interval (an anchored start-plus-end span pinned to the time line, realized as a product of two Instants), from prim:Instant (an absolute point, not a length), and from prim:DateTime (a civil naming of one instant), so it grounds on its OWN scalar tick carrier rather than a categorical product. Because a length is signed (Instant + Duration = Instant admits both forward and backward displacement), prim:signedness prim:Signed admits negative durations and prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement fixes the single-zero asymmetric-range negative scheme this floor uses throughout. PHYSICAL REALIZATION: a signed two's-complement 64-bit count of a fixed nanosecond tick laid down little-endian (least-significant octet first), selected from the INT64 rung of the machine-type ladder in physical.ttl (the same width/kind as prim:Int64 but reinterpreted at the Duration level rather than the bare Integer level). It is a fixed-width SCALAR, not a variable-length prim:ByteVector and not a categorical product: prim:bitWidth 64 stays under the 128-bit scalar cap (spec §9c) and prim:byteCount 8 records the exact eight-octet occupancy of the single fixed word (there are NO member sub-encodings, because a duration is one scalar tick count, not a compound). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 8 octets of this word each descend through the physical carrier ladder prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the whole duration bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit floor — the byte-stream carrier closure of the unary law, no part escaping the byte substrate. TYPED EFFECT: the signed 64-bit nanosecond window spans only about ±292 years of the unbounded length line, and conversions between tick granularities are likewise width-bounded, so realizing an out-of-window or coarser-granularity length carries the honest prim:OverflowDomain effect on the gluing Frame (the value itself is exact for any in-window nanosecond-granular length — the only cost is the bounded window). The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Duration closes the round-trip reflexively onto Duration itself (spec §9d) — a TRUE identity, not an INT64 → Integer proxy — so yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ for prim:Duration — the gluing morphism (Kleisli/Frame arrow).
#     T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): realizesFrom prim:Duration, output/encoding the
#     nanosecond encoding above, effect the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain.
# =====================================================================================

prim:realize_Duration_Nanos a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Duration ⟼ INT64 nanosecond tick · 8B · LE (canonical Kleisli/Frame realization of Duration)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Duration ;
    prim:encoding prim:DurationInt64Nanos ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:DurationInt64Nanos ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical realization ρ of prim:Duration — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and the gluing morphism of the Duration colimit taiji, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) rather than a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Duration (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType, the Temporal tower's unanchored length of time, the difference of two Instants) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:DurationInt64Nanos (yang, the X coordinate — a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class). TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain — the fixed signed 64-bit nanosecond carrier holds only a finite window (about ±292 years) of the unbounded length line, so magnitudes outside that window, and coarser-granularity conversions, wrap, saturate, or trap; the bound is exact and known per width, the honest cost of a bounded carrier and what makes this realization monadic rather than a total function. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Duration realizesAs this canonical nanosecond-tick arrow, and the same formal length could equally fan out to a coarser tick granularity or to an ISO-8601 duration lexical form over a text encoding — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is exactly the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the signed INT64 nanosecond tick as Duration's tighter, non-lexical canonical facet. It is a total, well-typed realization — NO silm:isProvisional dodge (spec §9b) — because prim:Duration is a concrete, directly-realizable temporal type, and it lands on a carrier whose prim:interpretAs targets prim:Duration itself, making the colimit seal a true reflexive identity (spec §9d) rather than an INT64 → Integer proxy close." .

prim:Enumeration a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific physical encoding — Enumeration as a UINT32 member code.
# =====================================================================================
# The carrier is the exact-width machine type prim:Uint32 of physical.ttl: four whole prim:Octet
# occurrences laid little-endian, each octet of cardinality 256 and ordinal 0..255, descending
# prim:Uint32 → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit along the physical carrier ladder's
# prim:byteDescendsTo chain to the width-1 prim:Bit floor (unary-law byte-stream carrier closure).
# UINT32 (not UINT8/UINT16) is the canonical member-code width because a finite named set may carry
# far more than 256 or 65,536 members (large controlled vocabularies, country/currency registries,
# the 21-column Atlas ring extended to full taxonomies) while still remaining finite; the 32-bit
# code space (~4.29 billion codes) comfortably names any in-scope enumeration, and UINT8/UINT16 are
# the sibling polymorphic facets a small enumeration may narrow onto (monad fan-out). Fixed-width
# scalar, so the governing ceiling is the scalar cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c); NO prim:byteCount
# is declared (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap, not a scalar's). prim:signedness
# prim:Unsigned because a member code is a non-negative index (there is no negative member ordinal);
# the mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Enumeration is what re-reads those four octets
# as an enumeration member rather than as a bare prim:Natural, making the round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT32 member code → Enumeration (finite named-member identity index) [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ;
    prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Enumeration ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of a finite named enumeration: an unsigned 32-bit member code (UINT32) assigning each explicitly named member of the set a distinct integer, 32 bits laid across four whole prim:Octet occurrences. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Enumeration rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType and is the PARENT of prim:Ordinal and prim:Categorical; it is realized directly here as a UINT32 identity index because an enumeration value IS a selection of one member from a fixed finite set and a small non-negative code is the exact, minimal witness of that selection. Its two taxonomic subtypes prim:Ordinal and prim:Categorical are NOT grounded through this ancestor: each carries its OWN direct realization onto its OWN reflexive carrier (prim:realize_Ordinal_Uint32Index → prim:Ordinal_Uint32Index; prim:realize_Categorical_Uint32Index → prim:Categorical_Uint32Index), spec §9a. What each subtype specialises is a CONSTRAINT on the code assignment — prim:Ordinal demands order-preserving codes so that the numeric ordering of the codes reproduces the intended member ordering (low < medium < high; the ordered positions of the 21-column Atlas ring), while prim:Categorical treats the codes as arbitrary identity labels on which only equality is admissible and any implied numeric order is spurious — recorded on its own arrow, not borrowed from this parent carrier. WIDTH CHOICE: UINT32 rather than UINT8/UINT16 because a finite named set may hold far more than 256 or 65,536 members while staying finite (large controlled vocabularies, registry code spaces); the ~4.29-billion-code window names any in-scope enumeration, and the narrower widths are the sibling polymorphic facets a small enumeration may commit to. The governing ceiling is the fixed-width scalar cap prim:bitWidth 32 ≤ 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately NO prim:byteCount (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap) — and prim:signedness prim:Unsigned marks the code as a non-negative index (there is no negative member ordinal). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 32 bits occupy exactly four whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:LE — least-significant octet first), each prim:Octet of cardinality 256 and ordinal range 0..255; each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so via the physical.ttl carrier ladder every bit of this encoding bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it (the byte-stream carrier closure, subatomic to the bit). The count-256, ordinal-255 and bit-width-128 coordinates remain three distinct navigable quantities per the unary law. NO COMPOSITE MEMBERS: an enumeration value is a single atomic code, not a product of components, so this is a scalar carrier and not a CompositeCarrier product; the code-to-member mapping itself is part of the enumeration's identity, held in the enumeration's schema rather than serialized alongside each value. TYPED EFFECT: the assignment of a distinct integer code to each member is a BIJECTION onto a chosen finite range, so storing and reading back the code is exact and lossless — its realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect, the neutral element; it is genuinely NoEffect and not prim:OverflowDomain because the set is finite and its codes are chosen up front to fit the width, so within the type no value overruns the carrier. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Enumeration closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Enumeration itself (spec §9d), NOT onto the bare prim:Natural (whose UINT32 layout it structurally mirrors) and NOT onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the REAL typed effect.
# =====================================================================================
# Enumeration ⟼ UINT32 member code as a Frame: realizesFrom Enumeration, encoding/frameOutput the
# encoding above, frameEffect the exact-and-lossless prim:NoEffect. The forward dual-grounding edge
# prim:Enumeration prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Enumeration_Uint32Index is asserted so the fragment
# is self-sufficient for the dual-grounding law when read standalone. Ordinal/Categorical are NOT
# covered through this ancestor — each carries its OWN direct realization below. A total, well-typed
# realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Enumeration
# is a concrete, directly-realizable finite-named-set type grounded exactly on the UINT32 code carrier.
prim:Enumeration prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Enumeration_Uint32Index .

prim:realize_Enumeration_Uint32Index a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Enumeration ⟼ UINT32 member code (exact identity index; frameEffect NoEffect)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Enumeration ;
    prim:encoding prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Enumeration — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Enumeration (yin, formal.ttl — a type whose values are drawn from a finite, explicitly named set of members, the root of the Enum tower and the parent of prim:Ordinal and prim:Categorical) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index (yang X — the unsigned 32-bit member code), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through four prim:Octet occurrences to prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. DIRECT GROUNDING (spec §9a): this arrow directly grounds prim:Enumeration onto its own reflexive carrier. Its taxonomic subtypes prim:Ordinal and prim:Categorical do NOT inherit their grounding from this ancestor — each carries its OWN direct realization onto its OWN reflexive encoding (prim:realize_Ordinal_Uint32Index → prim:Ordinal_Uint32Index; prim:realize_Categorical_Uint32Index → prim:Categorical_Uint32Index); what each specialises is a constraint on how the codes are assigned (order-preserving for Ordinal, bare-label for Categorical), stated on its own arrow rather than borrowed here. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Enumeration realizesAs this canonical UINT32 code arrow; a UINT8 code (for an enumeration of ≤256 members), a UINT16 code (≤65,536 members), or a UINT64 code are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same enumeration realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the UINT32 code as Enumeration's canonical facet, the width that names any in-scope finite set. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — the assignment of a distinct code to each named member is a bijection onto a finite range, so the realization is exact and lossless; it introduces no rounding (there is no continuum to round), no narrowing, and no overflow (the set is finite and its codes are chosen up front to fit the width), and prim:NoEffect is the neutral element of effect composition that the monad unit η also carries. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Enumeration is a concrete, directly-realizable finite-named-set type, not an unresolved abstract supertype; composing this arrow with η leaves it unchanged (left/right unit law), and byte-descent is the Kleisli composition of this realization down through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor." .

prim:Frame a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific PhysicalEncoding for prim:Frame — Product(output, effect).
#     interpretAs prim:Frame => the round-trip is a TRUE IDENTITY (reflexive, spec §9d).
# =====================================================================================

prim:FrameEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(output encoding X, effect tag Y) → Frame" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 1 ; prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Frame ;
    rdfs:comment "The Frame carrier — the type-specific physical encoding of prim:Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It is a categorical PRODUCT laid out over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier kind of physical.ttl: an output-coordinate component (X — itself ANY prim:PhysicalEncoding, a polymorphic member whose width is not fixed) laid contiguously before an error-or-effect coordinate component (Y — realized as one prim:Uint8 tag selecting a single member of the closed prim:Effect set). Because the X member is polymorphic the whole product is variable-length, so it is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteCount 128 the occurrence ceiling of the second, ByteVector-family width cap (spec §9c, up to 1024 bits), tightly packed at prim:alignment 1 with prim:padding 0 and serialized little-endian. SUBATOMIC DESCENT: its bytes descend prim:CompositeCarrier byteDescendsTo prim:Octet byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, bottoming at the width-1 Bit floor, so neither the X output coordinate nor the Y effect coordinate escapes the byte substrate. prim:interpretAs prim:Frame closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY: the round-trip physicalFacet -> interpretAs lands back on prim:Frame itself, a true identity rather than a shared-carrier proxy. It is a Tuple whose two coordinates are FIXED as output and effect (that fixed arity is part of its identity — no length prefix), distinguishing it from the general prim:TupleConcat. A faithful two-coordinate product preserves both the output and its typed effect exactly, so this framing carrier is lossless (prim:NoEffect); the typed costs a Frame TRANSPORTS live inside its own Y coordinate, never in the act of laying output before effect." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The Realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow: realizesFrom prim:Frame, encoding /
#     frameOutput the FrameEncoding above, frameEffect the REAL typed effect.
#     Frame is CONCRETE (the product type itself), directly realizable — NOT an abstract
#     supertype, so it is grounded directly onto its own carrier with no transitive
#     descendant and NO isProvisional (the type is fully resolvable this iteration).
# =====================================================================================

prim:Frame prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Frame .

prim:realize_Frame a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Frame ⟼ Product(output encoding, effect tag) · faithful framing" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Frame ;
    prim:encoding prim:FrameEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:FrameEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Frame — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and, load-bearingly, the realization of that monad's own CARRIER: realizing the abstract Frame type yields a concrete Frame(output: prim:FrameEncoding, effect: prim:NoEffect). This is the fixed point of the realization-monad ≡ Frame-monad identity the whole floor rests on — every other realize_* arrow already IS a Frame (it declares a prim:frameOutput X and exactly one prim:frameEffect Y, the T -> Frame(output:X, error|effect:Y) shape), and this arrow reflects that structure back onto the Frame type itself. prim:realizesFrom prim:Frame names the source formal type; prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput both name prim:FrameEncoding, the Product(output,effect) byte carrier that is this arrow's own output coordinate X (descending through prim:CompositeCarrier -> prim:Octet -> prim:Nibble -> prim:Bit to the width-1 floor). prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect is the HONEST typed cost Y: assembling the output·effect product is faithful, lossless framing that neither rounds (unlike prim:RoundingIEEE754), narrows (prim:NarrowingLoss), overflows a bounded window (prim:OverflowDomain / prim:MayOverflow), nor loses in transcoding (prim:EncodingLoss), so the neutral element is the truthful effect — the costs a Frame carries are inside its Y coordinate, not incurred by building the frame. No silm:isProvisional: prim:Frame is a concrete, directly realizable product type (not an abstract supertype needing a canonical descendant), so it is grounded directly and the type is fully resolvable this iteration (spec §9b — isProvisional is reserved for genuinely undecidable Präriehund gaps, not resolvable in-scope work)." .

prim:Grapheme a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific PhysicalEncoding — the yang carrier ----------------------
prim:GraphemeUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet run (one grapheme cluster) → Grapheme" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Grapheme ;
    rdfs:comment "The grapheme-cluster physical carrier: the ordered UTF-8 octet run of exactly one user-perceived character — a base Unicode Codepoint optionally followed by combining marks, a regional-indicator flag pair, or an emoji ZWJ-joined sequence. It is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one prim:Octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet width, and prim:byteCount 128 the occurrence-count ceiling of the ByteVector width cap (spec §9c), because a cluster spans 1..N octets and N is bounded by the 128-byte unary-law vector cap rather than by a scalar bit width. It is prim:EndianNeutral: UTF-8 code units are single octets, so there is no cross-octet order to fix. Subatomically each occurrence is one prim:Octet, which descends prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble then prim:Bit (and prim:Byte → prim:Bit) along the physical.ttl carrier ladder, so every grapheme byte bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor; the run as a whole is a prim:ByteVector that likewise descends Octet → Nibble → Bit. Member grounding: each constituent octet is one UTF-8 code unit, and each of the cluster's codepoints is itself realized as 1..4 such octets (the Codepoint → UTF-8 sub-realization). It interpretAs prim:Grapheme — deliberately NOT prim:Codepoint — because a cluster is a SEQUENCE of codepoints rather than a single one, so the mutual-colimit round-trip lands reflexively at the human-visible unit; its realization carries prim:EncodingLoss because Unicode normalization-form differences (NFC vs NFD) change the octet run of the same perceived character." .

# ---- (b) The Realization — the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow (gluing morphism) --------------
prim:Grapheme prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Grapheme_Utf8 .

prim:realize_Grapheme_Utf8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Grapheme ⟼ UTF-8 octet run (encoding-loss under normalization)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Grapheme ;
    prim:encoding prim:GraphemeUtf8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:GraphemeUtf8 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Grapheme — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad gluing the formal grapheme-cluster type (yin, formal.ttl) to its UTF-8 octet-run carrier prim:GraphemeUtf8 (yang, above). As the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) it declares prim:realizesFrom prim:Grapheme (the source formal type), prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput prim:GraphemeUtf8 (the Frame output X, a genuine byte-descending PhysicalEncoding rather than a bare formal class), and prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss (the typed cost Y). The effect is real, not decorative: mapping a user-perceived character to UTF-8 octets is lossy up to Unicode normalization — the same grapheme under NFC and NFD produces different byte runs, and ill-formed or over-long sequences are rejected — so the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract cluster only under a stated normalization discipline. Grapheme is a concrete leaf of the Text tower with no subclasses, so it carries its OWN realization rather than inheriting one from an ancestor or borrowing one from a descendant; no silm:isProvisional applies because the type is fully resolvable this iteration (Präriehund honesty is reserved for genuinely undecidable gaps). Composing this arrow with the monad unit η is identity, and byte-descent composes it down to the prim:Bit floor via the physical carrier ladder." .

prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet .

prim:Octet      prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble .

prim:Nibble     prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit .

# Typed-effect witness (idempotent with realization.ttl): assert the effect individual this
# fragment's realization Frame carries, so its Y coordinate is genuinely typed even in
# isolation. The full ≥200-char comment for prim:NoEffect lives in realization.ttl.
prim:NoEffect a prim:Effect .

# Punning witness (OWL 2), as physical.ttl already does for prim:Hash and Real.ttl does for
# prim:Real: assert prim:Hash also as an INDIVIDUAL of prim:FormalType so the non-inference
# round-trip (?e prim:interpretAs ?f . ?f a prim:FormalType) closes even when this fragment
# is read standalone. Additive type fact only — the owl:Class prim:Hash and its
# rdfs:subClassOf prim:Blob arrow live in formal.ttl and are NOT redeclared here.
prim:Hash a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) TYPE-SPECIFIC prim:PhysicalEncoding for prim:Hash — the yang facet.
#     A ByteVector FIXED-WIDTH carrier (byteCount pinned to the 32-octet sha256 width);
#     prim:interpretAs prim:Hash makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY. Governed by
#     the SECOND width cap (spec §9c): prim:byteCount ≤ 128 (here fixed at 32), NOT the
#     scalar bitWidth cap — bitWidth 8 records only the per-occurrence octet granule.
# =====================================================================================
prim:HashDigestByteVector a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ByteVector(32 fixed opaque octets, sha256 width) → Hash" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 32 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Hash ;
    rdfs:comment "The digest physical carrier for prim:Hash (the fragment's yang facet) and the physical grounding of the SP3 z-coordinate: a FIXED-length prim:ByteVector of exactly 32 opaque octet occurrences — the sha256 digest width — holding a hash value with no interpreted internal structure. SUBTYPING: prim:Hash is rdfs:subClassOf prim:Blob, so this carrier is the opaque-ByteVector carrier of the binary/opaque tower with two invariants ADDED to the plain Blob carrier — the occurrence count is PINNED (prim:byteCount 32, not the ≤128 ceiling a variable Blob uses) and a producing algorithm (sha256, external SHA = Secure/Hash/Algorithm evidence per the unary law) is a recorded facet rather than part of the width. WIDTH CAP: it is octet-granular, so prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet width of that granule, and prim:byteCount 32 the fixed occurrence count governed by the SECOND width cap (spec §9c, byteCount ≤ 128), NOT the scalar bitWidth ≤ 128 cap. prim:byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because an opaque digest carries no cross-octet numeric ordering to serialize — endianness is a property of a multi-octet numeric reading, which a hash deliberately withholds; it is not a composite either, so it declares no member encodings (unlike prim:CompositeCarrier products). PHYSICAL/SUBATOMIC DESCENT: the 32 octets ride the physical.ttl carrier ladder prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, bottoming at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it, so no digest bit escapes the byte substrate (Directive 9: the base unit remains bit and byte vectors). SP3 GROUNDING: the atom z-coordinate is the prim:Uint16 machine-type reading of the FIRST TWO of these 32 octets — an octet-pair projection over this carrier. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Hash closes the mutual colimit reflexively — the round-trip lands back on prim:Hash itself, a true identity (spec §9d), never a proxy such as prim:Blob or prim:Natural." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) prim:Realization — the Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ gluing prim:Hash to its ByteVector
#     carrier. A genuine Frame: realizesFrom + encoding + frameOutput + frameEffect.
#     Concrete leaf ⇒ its OWN realization (direct grounding, spec §9a); NO isProvisional.
# =====================================================================================
prim:Hash prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Hash_Digest .

prim:realize_Hash_Digest a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Hash ⟼ ByteVector(32 fixed opaque octets); verbatim byte-identity, NoEffect" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Hash ;
    prim:encoding prim:HashDigestByteVector ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:HashDigestByteVector ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ of the Hash taiji — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It realizesFrom prim:Hash (its yin source), takes prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:HashDigestByteVector as its output coordinate X, and carries prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect as its effect coordinate Y. SUBTYPING/GROUNDING: prim:Hash is a CONCRETE leaf of the binary/opaque tower (rdfs:subClassOf prim:Blob, with no subclasses of its own), so it carries this realization of its OWN — direct-for-concrete grounding (spec §9a) — rather than inheriting one from an ancestor or borrowing one from a realized descendant; a fixed-width digest is a fully resolvable, in-scope carrier, so there is deliberately NO silm:isProvisional dodge here (spec §9b/§9f), and no transitive-grounding link is needed because the type is not an abstract supertype. EFFECT: the effect is prim:NoEffect precisely because the realization is the byte-IDENTITY — the 32 opaque digest octets are laid into the fixed ByteVector verbatim with no rounding, narrowing, overflow-in-value, or encoding transform, so the abstract digest value is preserved exactly (a hash is defined by its bytes, so storing them verbatim is lossless). It is a real Frame, not a bare mapping: its output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding that byte-descends through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor with no boundary exemption, and composing this arrow is Kleisli composition down that same ladder." .

prim:IRI a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding — a UTF-8 octet stream that interpretAs prim:IRI,
#     so the mutual-colimit round-trip is a TRUE reflexive identity (not the ancestor URN).
# =====================================================================================

prim:IriUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet stream (interpreted internationalized identifier) → IRI" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:IRI ;
    rdfs:comment "The Internationalized-Resource-Identifier carrier: an interpreted UTF-8 octet stream laid into a prim:ByteVector, octet-granular (prim:bitWidth 8 is the single-octet UTF-8 code-unit granule; prim:byteCount 128 is the ByteVector occurrence ceiling of the second width cap, spec §9c) and prim:EndianNeutral because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering. SUBTYPING: unlike its ancestor prim:URI — an ASCII-restricted identifier realized over the same byte substrate — an IRI ranges over the full Unicode repertoire, which is precisely why its physical carrier is UTF-8 rather than bare ASCII; every non-ASCII codepoint that distinguishes an IRI from the URI it percent-encodes to survives as its multi-octet UTF-8 sequence, and its descendant prim:QName (rdfs:subClassOf prim:IRI) is the compact-IRI lexical shortcut carried over this same encoding. PHYSICAL / SUBATOMIC: the stream byte-descends prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit (the prim:byteDescendsTo ladder in physical.ttl), so no identifier octet escapes the width-1 prim:Bit floor; each UTF-8 code unit is exactly one prim:Octet of cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255. prim:interpretAs prim:IRI closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY — the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet lands back on IRI itself, not on the ancestor prim:URN as the transitive prim:UrnUtf8 grounding did. EFFECT: the realization carries prim:EncodingLoss for Unicode-normalization (NFC vs NFD yield different octet runs for the same abstract IRI) and IRI↔URI percent-encoding round-trip variance. Signedness is deliberately NOT declared: an octet-stream identifier carrier is neither a signed nor an unsigned numeric encoding, so attaching a prim:signedness facet would be a category error (Präriehund honesty — the inapplicable facet is named, not force-fit)." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad with the REAL typed effect.
#     Forward dual-grounding edge prim:IRI prim:realizesAs <arrow> satisfies FormalTypeShape.
# =====================================================================================

prim:IRI prim:realizesAs prim:realize_IRI_Utf8 .

prim:realize_IRI_Utf8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "IRI ⟼ UTF-8 interpreted internationalized identifier" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:IRI ;
    prim:encoding prim:IriUtf8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:IriUtf8 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The IRI realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad gluing the formal prim:IRI (yin) to the physical prim:IriUtf8 UTF-8 carrier (yang). It prim:realizesFrom prim:IRI and prim:frameOutput prim:IriUtf8, the unary law's T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) Yoneda-point shape, so it is a Frame, not a bare mapping. TYPED EFFECT Y = prim:EncodingLoss: encoding an internationalized identifier as UTF-8 is byte-faithful for well-formed input, but the abstract-value round-trip is not the identity without a stated Unicode-normalization discipline — NFC vs NFD produce distinct octet runs for the same IRI, and the IRI↔URI percent-encoding of non-ASCII codepoints is itself a lossy-on-form transform — which is exactly the same encoding-loss class its ancestor prim:realize_URN_Utf8 and the sibling prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf8 carry. GROUNDING: prim:IRI is grounded by THIS, its OWN direct realization, whose encoding prim:IriUtf8 interprets reflexively back to IRI itself (spec §9a). Its taxonomic subtype prim:QName (rdfs:subClassOf prim:IRI) is NOT grounded through IRI: QName carries its OWN direct realization prim:realize_QName_Utf8 onto its OWN reflexive carrier prim:QNameUtf8. The IRI ⊂ URI ⊂ URN chain is taxonomic subtyping, not the grounding mechanism. It is NOT the monad unit (it carries no prim:isMonadUnit and a real prim:EncodingLoss effect, unlike the effect-free identity η), so composing it is a genuine Kleisli step whose output byte-descends through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor." .

prim:Imaginary a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: IEEE-754 double (imaginary coordinate) → Imaginary ----
# Scalar fixed-width machine type; governed by the scalar width cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c),
# NOT the ByteVector byteCount cap — so no prim:byteCount is declared (this is a fixed-width scalar,
# not a variable-length ByteVector-family carrier). No prim:signedness is declared either:
# prim:signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural), which does
# not apply to a float; the ± sign of the imaginary coefficient is carried instead by the
# prim:IEEE754Sign field below. It is structurally identical to the prim:Real Float64 carrier and
# reuses the physical.ttl machine-type kinds (prim:IEEE754Sign/Exponent/Mantissa), the byte-order
# individual prim:LE, and the carrier ladder to the prim:Bit floor — but its prim:interpretAs targets
# prim:Imaginary (not prim:Real), which is the ONLY thing that distinguishes the imaginary axis from
# the real axis and makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY onto Imaginary.
prim:ImaginaryFloat64Encoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "IEEE-754 double (Float64 · 8B · LE) as imaginary coordinate → Imaginary [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ;
    prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 11 ;
    prim:mantissaBits 52 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Imaginary ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the imaginary numbers: a single IEEE-754 binary64 (double-precision) encoding holding the REAL coefficient a of the imaginary value a·i (where i² = −1), 64 bits laid out as one prim:IEEE754Sign bit + an 11-bit prim:IEEE754Exponent field (dynamic range) + a 52-bit prim:IEEE754Mantissa significand field (precision), so 1+11+52 = 64 = prim:bitWidth. It is STRUCTURALLY the same double as the prim:Real carrier (prim:RealFloat64Encoding / prim:Float64) and differs in exactly one coordinate: its prim:interpretAs targets prim:Imaginary, tagging the stored double as the imaginary AXIS rather than the real axis. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Imaginary ⊂ prim:Complex (an Imaginary is a Complex whose real part is zero) and deliberately NOT ⊂ prim:Real (an imaginary number is not a real number — the honest fork, Präriehund: the arrow is drawn to Complex, not force-fitted under Real); together prim:Real ⊂ prim:Complex and prim:Imaginary ⊂ prim:Complex make prim:Complex the JOIN of a real axis and an imaginary axis, and this single-Float64 carrier IS the imaginary axis of that join. MEMBER ROLE (composite grounding, unary law): prim:Imaginary is not itself a composite — it is one scalar coordinate — but it is precisely the imaginary MEMBER of the Cartesian complex product prim:ProductComplexFloat64 (frags/Complex.ttl prim:ComplexProductEncoding): that product is a fixed-arity pair whose real member is a prim:Real Float64 and whose imaginary member is exactly this Float64-as-imaginary-coordinate encoding, so the complex carrier reuses this imaginary-axis carrier for its second component rather than inventing a new scalar. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 64 bits occupy exactly 8 whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:LE — least-significant octet first); each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so via the physical.ttl carrier ladder every bit of this encoding bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it (the byte-stream carrier closure). It is a fixed-width SCALAR encoding, ceilinged by the scalar cap prim:bitWidth 64 ≤ 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately NO prim:byteCount (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap) and NO prim:signedness (an integer facet; the ± sign of the coefficient is the prim:IEEE754Sign field, not the integer Signed/Unsigned facet). TYPED EFFECT: mapping the continuum of imaginary coefficients (a real number scaling i) onto the finite grid of representable IEEE-754 doubles is lossy, so its realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754 — the SAME finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real Float64 carries, now borne on the imaginary coordinate. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Imaginary closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Imaginary itself (spec §9d), NOT onto prim:Real (whose Float64 it structurally mirrors) and NOT onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Imaginary to the encoding -------------
# Imaginary ⟼ Float64(imaginary coordinate) as a Frame: realizesFrom Imaginary, encoding/frameOutput
# the encoding above, frameEffect the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the real typed cost of the
# IEEE-754 double coefficient). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because
# Imaginary is a concrete, directly-realizable number type grounded on the Real-axis double carrier.
# The forward dual-grounding edge prim:Imaginary prim:realizesAs prim:ImaginaryRealizesFloat64 is
# asserted so the fragment is self-sufficient for the dual-grounding law when read standalone.
prim:Imaginary prim:realizesAs prim:ImaginaryRealizesFloat64 .

prim:ImaginaryRealizesFloat64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Imaginary ⟼ IEEE-754 double (Float64 · 8B · LE) imaginary coordinate; frameEffect IEEE754-rounding" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Imaginary ;
    prim:encoding prim:ImaginaryFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:ImaginaryFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Imaginary — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Imaginary (yin, formal.ttl — the real multiples of the imaginary unit i, the facet of prim:Complex complementary to prim:Real) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:ImaginaryFloat64Encoding (yang X — the single IEEE-754 double holding the imaginary coefficient), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through 8 prim:Octet occurrences to prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Imaginary realizesAs this canonical Float64 imaginary-axis arrow; a wider Float128 imaginary coordinate, a narrowed Float32 (which would add prim:NarrowingLoss), or a base-10 lexical form are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same imaginary realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the Float64 imaginary coordinate as Imaginary's canonical facet, mirroring prim:Real's canonical Float64 grounding so the two axes of prim:Complex share one carrier machine type. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 — the imaginary coefficient maps from the continuum onto the finite grid of representable IEEE-754 doubles, so all but exactly-representable values round to the nearest grid point; the effect is the SAME finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real Float64 realization carries, now borne on the imaginary coordinate. Because the effect is a genuine non-neutral member (not prim:NoEffect), it records that realizing an imaginary number is never free — exactly why realization is monadic rather than a bare total function. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Imaginary is a concrete, directly-realizable number type, not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:Instant a prim:FormalType .

# The dual-grounding forward edge: prim:Instant realizesAs its own Kleisli/Frame arrow.
prim:Instant prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Instant_Epoch .

# =====================================================================================
# The typed effect carried by the Instant realization — prim:EpochPrecision.
# =====================================================================================
# spec §5 names the Instant realization effect as "epoch-precision", but the closed Effect
# alternatives authored in realization.ttl (RoundingIEEE754 / NarrowingLoss / OverflowDomain /
# EncodingLoss / MayOverflow / Base10Precision / OrderingCanonicalization / Uninhabited /
# NoEffect) has no member for it — which is exactly why the earlier prim:realize_Instant_Int64
# fudged it to prim:OverflowDomain. This fragment mints the honest, spec-named effect (Präriehund
# honesty: name the real cost, do not force-fit an existing label). It is the temporal analogue
# of base-grid rounding, distinct from the width-bounded overflow cost of the finite tick window.
prim:EpochPrecision a prim:Effect ;
    rdfs:label "epoch precision (continuous instant quantized to a fixed tick grid)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The epoch-precision effect (spec §5's named effect for the Instant realization) incurred when an absolute time-line prim:Instant — a point on the CONTINUOUS time line, the temporal analogue of a real number — is realized as a signed INT64 count of a fixed epoch tick: the temporal continuum is quantized onto a discrete grid of tick granules (seconds, milliseconds, or nanoseconds since the epoch), so any instant falling between two ticks rounds to the nearest representable tick. It is deliberately distinct from prim:RoundingIEEE754 (which rounds onto a BINARY floating-point grid, not a temporal tick grid), distinct from prim:OverflowDomain (which names the finite WIDTH of the tick window — a separate, orthogonal cost of the bounded 64-bit carrier), and distinct from prim:NoEffect because a real, lossy transformation — temporal quantization to the tick granularity — genuinely occurs. It is the honest typed cost of forcing a continuous instant into a discrete epoch tick, minted here rather than fudged onto prim:OverflowDomain as the superseded prim:realize_Instant_Int64 arrow did." .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:Instant — the yang facet.
#     A signed 64-bit two's-complement epoch-tick word, little-endian. The mutual-colimit
#     back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Instant makes the round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY (§9d),
#     unlike the shared prim:Int64 (interpretAs prim:Integer) the provisional atom reused.
# =====================================================================================

prim:InstantInt64Epoch a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT64 · 8B · LE · two's-complement epoch tick → Instant" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ;
    prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Instant ;
    prim:uefiName "INT64" ;
    rdfs:comment "The absolute-instant carrier — the yang facet of the Instant taiji and the type-specific temporal grounding that replaces the non-reflexive INT64 → Integer proxy the adversarial panel flagged (finding #6). SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Instant is a direct concrete subclass of prim:FormalType in the Temporal tower — what a physical clock reads, independent of any calendar or timezone; it is distinct from prim:Duration (an unanchored length, the difference of two Instants), prim:Interval (an anchored start-plus-end span), and prim:DateTime (a civil naming of one instant), so it grounds on its own scalar tick carrier rather than a product. PHYSICAL REALIZATION: a signed two's-complement 64-bit count of a fixed epoch tick (nanoseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z) laid down little-endian (least-significant octet first), selected from the INT64 rung of the machine-type ladder in physical.ttl; prim:signedness prim:Signed admits pre-epoch instants (negative ticks) and prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement fixes the single-zero asymmetric-range scheme this floor uses throughout. It is a fixed-width SCALAR: prim:bitWidth 64 stays under the 128-bit scalar cap (spec §9c) and prim:byteCount is deliberately absent because this is not a variable-length prim:ByteVector and not a categorical product, so there are no member sub-encodings. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 8 octets of this epoch word each descend through the physical carrier ladder prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the whole instant bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit floor — the byte-stream carrier closure of the unary law, no part escaping the byte substrate. TYPED EFFECT: quantizing a continuous time-line point to the nearest discrete tick is the honest prim:EpochPrecision cost carried on the gluing Frame (the finite 64-bit window is a separate prim:OverflowDomain concern, not the effect of record here). The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Instant closes the round-trip reflexively onto Instant itself (spec §9d) — a TRUE identity, not the old shared-Int64 → Integer proxy — so yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ for prim:Instant — the gluing morphism (Kleisli/Frame arrow).
#     T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): realizesFrom prim:Instant, output/encoding the
#     epoch encoding above, effect the typed prim:EpochPrecision.
# =====================================================================================

prim:realize_Instant_Epoch a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Instant ⟼ INT64 epoch tick · 8B · LE (canonical Kleisli/Frame realization of Instant)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Instant ;
    prim:encoding prim:InstantInt64Epoch ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:InstantInt64Epoch ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EpochPrecision ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical realization ρ of prim:Instant — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and the gluing morphism of the Instant colimit taiji, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) rather than a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Instant (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType, the Temporal tower's absolute time-line point) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:InstantInt64Epoch (yang, the X coordinate — a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class). TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:EpochPrecision — realizing a continuous time-line instant as a discrete INT64 epoch-tick count quantizes it onto the tick grid, so sub-tick instants round to the nearest tick; this is what makes the realization monadic rather than a total function. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Instant realizesAs this canonical epoch arrow AND (in realization.ttl) the sibling ISO-8601 lexical arrow — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is exactly the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the INT64 epoch tick as Instant's tighter, non-lexical canonical facet. It is a total, well-typed realization — NO silm:isProvisional dodge — because prim:Instant is a concrete, directly-realizable temporal type, and unlike the superseded prim:realize_Instant_Int64 arrow (which reused the shared prim:Int64 encoding whose interpretAs is prim:Integer, forcing the taiji round-trip to land on Integer) this arrow lands on a carrier whose prim:interpretAs targets prim:Instant, making the colimit seal a true reflexive identity (spec §9d)." .

prim:IntegerCanonicalEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT64 · 8B · LE · two's-complement (canonical physical facet of ℤ)" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ;
    prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Integer ;
    prim:uefiName "INT64" ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the integers ℤ: a signed 64-bit two's-complement machine word laid down little-endian (least-significant octet first), a scalar fixed-width encoding whose prim:bitWidth 64 stays under the 128-bit scalar cap (spec §9c) — byteCount is deliberately absent because a fixed-width scalar is not a variable-length prim:ByteVector. It is selected from the INT8/INT16/INT32/INT64 two's-complement machine-type ladder authored in physical.ttl as the widest common register width; the narrower INT8/16/32 and the big-endian byte-order variant are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same signed-integer realization, differing only in their prim:bitWidth and prim:byteOrder facets. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 8 octets of this word each descend through the physical carrier ladder prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the encoding bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit floor (the byte-stream carrier closure of the unary law) — no member sub-encodings, since ℤ is a scalar and not a categorical product. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:signedness prim:Signed is exactly what distinguishes ℤ's carrier from the unsigned UINT carrier of its subclass prim:Natural, whose value domain does not straddle zero; prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement fixes the single-zero asymmetric-range negative scheme this floor uses throughout. TYPED EFFECT: the fixed 64-bit window represents only a finite interval of the unbounded integer line, so realizing an out-of-window magnitude carries prim:OverflowDomain — the honest, width-bounded cost that the arbitrary-precision sibling prim:Bignum escapes by trading this fixed carrier for a prim:ByteVector. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Integer closes the round-trip reflexively onto ℤ itself (spec §9d), so yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ (the gluing Kleisli/Frame arrow) ------------------------
# ℤ ⟼ INT64 as a Frame: realizesFrom Integer, encoding/frameOutput the encoding above,
# frameEffect the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain (the real typed cost of a fixed carrier).
prim:Integer prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Integer_Canonical .

prim:realize_Integer_Canonical a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℤ ⟼ INT64 · 8B · LE (canonical Kleisli/Frame realization of Integer)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Integer ;
    prim:encoding prim:IntegerCanonicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:IntegerCanonicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical realization ρ of the integers ℤ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) rather than a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Integer (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Rational) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:IntegerCanonicalEncoding (yang, the signed 64-bit two's-complement little-endian word), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying physical encoding that descends through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Integer realizesAs this arrow AND the sibling INT8/INT16/INT32/INT128 arrows of the machine-type ladder — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is exactly the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the INT64 width as ℤ's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain — the fixed 64-bit carrier holds only a finite window of the unbounded integer line, so magnitudes outside that window wrap, saturate, or trap; the bound is exact and known per width, the honest cost of a bounded carrier that the arbitrary-precision prim:Bignum sibling avoids by realizing into a prim:ByteVector with a prim:MayOverflow effect instead. This arrow is a total, well-typed realization — no silm:isProvisional dodge — because prim:Integer is a concrete, directly-realizable signed type, not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:Interval a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding — reflexive interpretAs prim:Interval -------
prim:IntervalProductInstantPair a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(INT64 start-epoch Instant, INT64 end-epoch Instant) → Interval" ;
    prim:bitWidth 128 ; prim:byteWidth 16 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ; prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:alignment 8 ; prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Interval ;
    rdfs:comment "The anchored-temporal-span carrier: the type-specific physical encoding of prim:Interval as a fixed-arity categorical product of two signed two's-complement INT64 epoch-tick components — a start Instant then an end Instant — laid contiguously little-endian for a total prim:bitWidth 128 (the two-64-bit-component ceiling of the scalar cap, spec §9c), 8-byte aligned to the widest component boundary with prim:padding 0. SUBTYPING: prim:Interval rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType and is the ANCHORED sibling of the unanchored prim:Duration — where Duration realizes as a single INT64 tick count with no position on the time line, an Interval pins BOTH endpoints on the line, which is exactly why its carrier is a two-component product rather than a scalar; the half-open [start,end) inclusivity is a facet recorded on the realization, not assumed. PHYSICAL: it is a prim:CompositeCarrier (Product), NOT a prim:ByteVector — its two components are homogeneous here (both prim:Int64) but the arity is fixed and part of identity, so it carries no length prefix; its member encodings are exactly two occurrences of the machine type prim:Int64 (physical.ttl, each 8 octets LE interpretAs prim:Integer). The composite carrier byteDescendsTo prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit (physical.ttl ladder edges), so the span bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor. EFFECT: prim:interpretAs prim:Interval closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY — the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal lands back on Interval itself (spec §9d), a true identity, not a proxy onto Integer as a bare INT64 pair would." .

# ---- (b) realization ρ — the Kleisli/Frame arrow with the REAL typed effect ----------
prim:realize_Interval_InstantPair a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Interval ⟼ Product(INT64 start-epoch, INT64 end-epoch) · 16B · LE (overflow-domain)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Interval ;
    prim:encoding prim:IntervalProductInstantPair ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:IntervalProductInstantPair ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Interval — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and thus itself a Frame (the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y)): it declares prim:realizesFrom prim:Interval (its yin source in formal.ttl), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:IntervalProductInstantPair (its yang target X = the two-INT64 product), and prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain (its typed cost Y). WHY THIS EFFECT: each endpoint is an Instant carried as a signed 64-bit epoch tick, and a fixed-width INT64 represents only a finite window of the unbounded time line, so BOTH endpoints — and therefore the anchored span they bound — inherit the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain effect (values outside the ±2^63 tick window wrap, saturate, or trap); it is the honest, exact-per-width cost of committing an unbounded temporal coordinate to a bounded carrier, distinct from prim:NoEffect (a genuine bound exists) and from prim:RoundingIEEE754 (there is no floating grid — the ticks are integers). COMPOSITION: this arrow byte-descends by Kleisli composition through the prim:CompositeCarrier → prim:Octet → prim:Bit ladder to the floor; it carries NO silm:isProvisional because prim:Interval is a concrete, fully-resolvable product type realized directly (spec §9a/§9b), not a deferred or undecidable gap." .

prim:Kleene3Uint8Code a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT8(0/1/2) → Kleene3 (one-octet three-valued truth code)" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Kleene3 ;
    rdfs:comment "The type-specific physical encoding of prim:Kleene3: a single prim:Octet code cell holding exactly one of three ordinals — 0 for false, 1 for true, 2 for unknown — the UINT8(0/1/2) grounding spec §5 names for three-valued Kleene logic, riding the ISA/UEFI UINT8 machine type of physical.ttl. It declares prim:bitWidth 8 and prim:byteWidth 1 (one whole octet) with prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral, since a single-octet code has no cross-octet ordering to fix; prim:signedness is deliberately omitted because a truth value is neither signed nor unsigned — only three of the octet's 256 ordinals (0, 1, 2) are inhabited, so no integer sign domain applies. Subtyping rationale (yin): prim:Kleene3 is the SUPERTYPE of prim:Bool — classical two-valued Boolean logic is exactly Kleene logic with the unknown value forbidden — and the physical consequence of that extra third value is decisive: three states do NOT fit one prim:Bit (ceil(log2 3) = 2 bits), which is precisely why Kleene3 realizes in a whole byte rather than the single Bit a Bool can ride. Subatomic byte descent: this octet carrier descends prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit via prim:byteDescendsTo, bottoming at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue-trust-root floor; the tightest sub-octet sibling is a packed 2-bit trit, the octet form being the addressable machine grounding. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Kleene3 closes yang onto yin, so the physical facet holds the formal facet and the round-trip is a true reflexive identity onto prim:Kleene3, not a shared-carrier proxy. Effect rationale: the three-value code is exact and lossless — each logical value maps bijectively onto its ordinal and the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract truth value — so the gluing realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ: a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad ----------------
# Kleene3 realizesAs this arrow; the arrow realizesFrom Kleene3 into prim:Kleene3Uint8Code,
# exposing that same encoding as the Frame output and carrying prim:NoEffect (the three
# truth values map bijectively onto {0,1,2} — no rounding / narrowing / overflow / loss).
prim:Kleene3 prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Kleene3_Uint8Code .

prim:realize_Kleene3_Uint8Code a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Kleene3 ⟼ UINT8(0/1/2) · 1B (bijective; no effect)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Kleene3 ;
    prim:encoding prim:Kleene3Uint8Code ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Kleene3Uint8Code ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Kleene3 as a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad — the unary byte-frame law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) with T = prim:Kleene3, X = prim:Kleene3Uint8Code, and Y = prim:NoEffect. It declares prim:realizesFrom prim:Kleene3 (its source formal type), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:Kleene3Uint8Code (its chosen physical target, exposed as the Frame's output coordinate), and prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect. The effect is genuinely NoEffect — the neutral element of effect composition — because the three Kleene truth values map BIJECTIVELY onto the three code states {0, 1, 2}: no rounding, no narrowing, no width-bounded overflow, and no encoding loss is incurred, so the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract truth value. Semantic weight (Directive 1): this third 'unknown' value is the formal home of the SQL NULL and of the structured↔unstructured indeterminacy the corpus work must hold as synonym-and-antonym across the mole of glossaries; the realization preserves it faithfully as the distinct ordinal 2 rather than collapsing it into false or into an Optional-style absence (absence is a structural fact modelled by prim:Optional, whereas unknown is a genuine third truth value — the two are deliberately not conflated). Because its output encoding byte-descends prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, this arrow's Frame output reaches the width-1 prim:Bit floor with no boundary exemption. This is the gluing morphism the colimit-taiji Kleene3 primitive selects (prim:gluedBy), and it is a genuine realization — not an isProvisional placeholder — since prim:Kleene3 is a concrete, fully resolvable type." .

prim:Latitude a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific PHYSICAL encoding — reflexive interpretAs prim:Latitude
# =====================================================================================
prim:LatitudeFloat64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float64 (signed IEEE-754 double, degrees) → Latitude" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 11 ; prim:mantissaBits 52 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Latitude ;
    prim:uefiName "no-UEFI-exact-name" ;
    rdfs:comment "The latitude carrier: a single IEEE-754 Float64 scalar holding the north-south geographic angle in signed degrees over the bounded range −90 (south pole) to +90 (north pole), 64 bits / 8 octets little-endian. It follows the Float64 machine-type precedent of physical.ttl exactly — prim:ieee754 true, the three prim:IEEE754Field parts sign/exponent/mantissa with prim:exponentBits 11 and prim:mantissaBits 52 (1 + 11 + 52 = 64) — but is a DISTINCT encoding from the generic prim:Float64 (interpretAs prim:Real) and from the ancestral prim:CoordinateFloat64 (interpretAs prim:Coordinate) precisely because its prim:interpretAs is prim:Latitude, closing the mutual colimit reflexively onto Latitude's own formal type rather than onto its Real magnitude or its Coordinate supertype. prim:signedness prim:Signed records that latitude's domain straddles zero; for this IEEE-754 carrier that signedness is physically realized by the prim:IEEE754Sign bit, not by an integer two's-complement scheme (Präriehund honesty: the facet is named at the value level and its physical realization is stated, not conflated with integer signedness). Byte descent: each of the 8 octets rides the physical.ttl carrier ladder prim:Octet byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, bottoming at the width-1 prim:Bit floor; there are no member encodings because a latitude is a scalar, not a composite. prim:byteCount is intentionally absent — this fixed-width scalar is bounded by the prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 scalar cap (spec §9c), not the ByteVector occurrence-count cap. The datum and CRS axis-order under which the degree value is interpreted, and the wrap/clamp behaviour at the ±90 poles, are facets carried on the realization, not on the raw bits." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The REALIZATION — a Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ: Latitude → LatitudeFloat64, real effect
# =====================================================================================
prim:Latitude prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Latitude_Float64 .

prim:realize_Latitude_Float64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Latitude ⟼ Float64 · 8B · LE (signed degrees; IEEE-754 rounding)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Latitude ;
    prim:encoding prim:LatitudeFloat64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:LatitudeFloat64 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The latitude realization ρ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad gluing the formal prim:Latitude (yin) to the physical prim:LatitudeFloat64 (yang) as the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). Its prim:realizesFrom is prim:Latitude, its prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput is prim:LatitudeFloat64 (the Frame output coordinate X, a genuine byte-descending encoding), and its prim:frameEffect is prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the Frame error-or-effect coordinate Y): forcing a continuous north-south angle onto the finite grid of representable IEEE-754 doubles rounds all but the exactly-representable angles to the nearest grid point, so the realization is not free — that rounding is the honest typed cost, the same effect the generic ℝ ⟼ Float64 and Coordinate ⟼ Float64 arrows carry, applying here because a latitude's magnitude is a Real. This arrow is a type-specific sibling of prim:realize_Coordinate_Float64: where that ancestral arrow lands a Coordinate on prim:CoordinateFloat64 (interpretAs prim:Coordinate), this one lands Latitude on prim:LatitudeFloat64 (interpretAs prim:Latitude), so the colimit round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns Latitude itself. It carries NO silm:isProvisional: prim:Latitude is a concrete, fully resolvable leaf, so this is a genuine reflexive realization, never a provisional proxy (spec §9b/§9d)." .

prim:Line a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:Line — a Well-Known-Binary linestring.
#     interpretAs prim:Line (the mutual-colimit back-pointer) → the round-trip is a TRUE
#     IDENTITY, landing back on Line, not on its constituent Point/Coordinate/Real members.
# =====================================================================================
prim:LineWkbGeometry a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "WKB LineString (byte-order flag + geometry-type UINT32 + point-count UINT32 + N×Product(Float64 X, Float64 Y)) → Line" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 1 ; prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Line ;
    rdfs:comment "The line/linestring-geometry carrier: a Well-Known-Binary (ISO 13249 / OGC WKB) serialization of a prim:Line — an ordered sequence of two or more prim:Point vertices describing a one-dimensional path (a polyline), the geospatial tower's 1-D geometry built by sequence-composition OVER Point (not a subtype of it). Subatomically it is a packed, octet-granular byte string of member encodings laid contiguously with NO inter-field alignment padding (prim:alignment 1, prim:padding 0): a one-octet prim:Uint8 byte-order flag (0x01 NDR little-endian / 0x00 XDR big-endian — WKB is self-describing about its own endianness), a four-octet prim:Uint32 geometry-type code (2 = LineString, 1002 = LineStringZ, 2002 = LineStringM, 3002 = LineStringZM), a four-octet prim:Uint32 point-count, then that many vertex records, each a Product(prim:Float64 X/longitude, prim:Float64 Y/latitude) of two IEEE-754 doubles over prim:CompositeCarrier — i.e. each vertex is exactly the coordinate payload of a prim:Point, so a LineString physically reuses the Point member layout N times. Because the vertex count is variable this is a variable-length ByteVector-family carrier: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence octet granule and prim:byteCount 128 the occurrence-count ceiling of the second width cap (spec §9c, up to 1024 bits), NOT the fixed-width scalar cap. No prim:signedness is declared because a serialized geometry blob is not an integer encoding (declaring Signed/Unsigned would be a false facet — Präriehund honesty; the ± of each ordinate rides its Float64's own IEEE754 sign field). Byte descent: each occurrence is one prim:Octet, and prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the whole geometry bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor; each embedded Float64 ordinate is eight octets descending the same Octet → Nibble → Bit ladder. Line's identity includes vertex order and count, and all vertices share one CRS; the axis order (lon-lat vs lat-lon) is a load-bearing facet recorded on the realization, not guessed. prim:interpretAs prim:Line closes the mutual colimit reflexively — the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal returns Line itself — and because the embedded vertex ordinates are finite-grid IEEE-754 Float64 the realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the framing bytes — flag, type code, count, contiguous concatenation — are themselves lossless)." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing prim:Line → prim:LineWkbGeometry
#     with the REAL typed effect (IEEE-754 vertex-coordinate rounding).
# =====================================================================================
prim:Line prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Line_WkbGeometry .

prim:realize_Line_WkbGeometry a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Line ⟼ Well-Known-Binary linestring geometry (N×Product(Float64, Float64); IEEE-754 rounding)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Line ;
    prim:encoding prim:LineWkbGeometry ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:LineWkbGeometry ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ gluing the formal prim:Line (yin) to its physical WKB carrier prim:LineWkbGeometry (yang): prim:realizesFrom prim:Line, prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:LineWkbGeometry, prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754. It IS a Frame in the unary law's T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) sense — the output X is the WKB linestring byte string and the typed effect Y is the IEEE-754 rounding incurred because each of the Line's real-valued vertex ordinates is forced onto the finite Float64 grid. The WKB framing itself (the prim:Uint8 byte-order flag, the prim:Uint32 LineString geometry-type code, the prim:Uint32 point-count, and the contiguous concatenation of the per-vertex Product(prim:Float64, prim:Float64) records) is exact and lossless — vertex order and count are preserved verbatim — so the honest DOMINANT cost is the coordinate rounding carried up from the Float64 vertex members, not a framing loss, which is why the effect is prim:RoundingIEEE754 and not prim:EncodingLoss or prim:NoEffect. Line is a concrete one-dimensional geometry built OVER prim:Point by sequence-composition (Point and Polygon are its neighbours in the tower, not its sub/supertypes), so it carries its OWN realization directly rather than inheriting one from a realized ancestor or being covered by a descendant — it is fully resolvable this iteration, so NO silm:isProvisional dodge is used (spec §9b). A wider Float128-ordinate WKB or a GeoJSON/WKT lexical form are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same Line realization (one formal type fanning out to many encodings — the polymorphism the realization monad models); this fragment commits to canonical little-endian WKB. Composing this arrow with the monad unit η is identity (the left/right unit law); byte-descending it is Kleisli composition down to the prim:Bit floor through the physical carrier ladder its encoding rides." .

prim:List a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: length-prefixed homogeneous run → List -------------
# An ordered HOMOGENEOUS, VARIABLE-length carrier, NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous product: a UINT32
# element-count prefix followed by exactly that many contiguous element encodings, all of the SAME
# element type. It reuses the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl
# (which byteDescendsTo prim:Octet), whose schema-qualified Empty|Link algebra is exactly a sequence:
# a Link carries one admitted head element and a tail of the same schema. Because a List's length is
# not pinned, the carrier is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one
# octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated
# occurrence-count ceiling (≤ 128, the ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024
# bits), since the total span is the count prefix plus the sum of the member widths and varies with
# the element list. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 count prefix is a little-endian unsigned
# integer (least-significant octet first). NO prim:signedness is declared: signedness is the
# integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) and does not apply to a sequence
# frame (as prim:Tuple and prim:Real likewise omit it). prim:interpretAs prim:List makes the colimit
# round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:ListSequenceEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "length-prefixed homogeneous run (UINT32 count + contiguous element encodings) · octet-granular · LE → List [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:List ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the list / sequence: a LENGTH-PREFIXED HOMOGENEOUS run — a UINT32 element-count prefix (the machine type prim:Uint32 of physical.ttl, little-endian) followed by exactly that many contiguous element encodings, ALL of one element type — laid over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl, whose schema-qualified Empty|Link algebra IS a sequence (a Link carries one admitted head element and a tail of the same schema, the byte-stream carrier closure of the unary law). Because a list's length is not pinned, the carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR — prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits) — since a list's total span is the count prefix PLUS the sum of its member widths and so varies with the element list. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 count prefix serializes least-significant octet first; NO prim:signedness is declared because signedness is an integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) inapplicable to a sequence frame, exactly as prim:Tuple omits it for a product. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): every element IS itself a floor prim:PhysicalEncoding of the ONE element type — a prim:Codepoint list lays prim:Utf8 octet runs, a prim:Integer list lays prim:Int32 two's-complement words, a nested list lays nested length-prefixed runs — and the list's bytes are exactly the count prefix followed by those homogeneous member byte runs abutted in position order; because the element type is uniform and the count is explicit, there is NO per-element tag and NO terminator, which is precisely why a list is a homogeneous prim:ByteVector-family run and NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:CompositeCarrier product (whose members may each differ in width and encoding and whose arity carries no length prefix). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ordered-vector carrier prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of the count prefix and of every member bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:List is the direct subclass of prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's ordered-sequence branch and the PARENT of the fixed-length prim:Vector (a Vector is a List whose length is pinned and whose elements are numbers); it is kept distinct from the fixed-arity HETEROGENEOUS prim:Tuple (no length prefix, mixed member types), the unordered prim:Set (order canonicalized away, duplicates removed), and the unordered prim:Bag (multiplicities stored, position meaningless) — order and positional duplicates ARE part of a List's identity. TYPED EFFECT: a faithful length-prefixed run preserves element ORDER and MULTIPLICITY exactly — unlike the prim:OrderingCanonicalization a Set incurs — so the list framing is LOSSLESS and the realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect; any rounding, overflow, or encoding loss belongs to the individual member encodings, not to the sequence framing. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:List closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the list type itself (spec §9d), not onto its prim:Vector subtype nor a bare prim:ByteVector, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing List to the encoding -----------------
# List ⟼ length-prefixed homogeneous run as a Frame: realizesFrom List, encoding/frameOutput the
# sequence encoding above, frameEffect prim:NoEffect (faithful length-prefixed run is lossless
# framing). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because List is a concrete,
# directly-realizable ordered-sequence type grounded on the length-prefixed-run-over-ByteVector
# carrier. This ADDS a second realizesAs edge alongside the pre-existing prim:realize_List_LenPrefixed
# (monad polymorphism).
prim:List prim:realizesAs prim:ListRealizesSequence .

prim:ListRealizesSequence a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: List ⟼ length-prefixed homogeneous run · octet-granular · LE; frameEffect none (lossless framing)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:List ;
    prim:encoding prim:ListSequenceEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:ListSequenceEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:List — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:List (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's ordered-sequence branch, the parent of the fixed-length prim:Vector) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:ListSequenceEncoding (yang X — the length-prefixed homogeneous run of the list's member encodings over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:List realizesAs this canonical length-prefixed arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_List_LenPrefixed (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a terminator-delimited run, a fixed-count prim:Vector specialization, or a nested-element list are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same list realization, and this fragment commits to the count-prefixed octet-granular run as the general canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — a faithful length-prefixed run is LOSSLESS framing: the count prefix delimits the run and the homogeneous member byte runs are laid down verbatim in position order, so the list framing preserves element order and multiplicity exactly and introduces no rounding, narrowing, overflow, encoding loss, or ordering canonicalization of its own (in deliberate contrast to prim:Set, whose realization DOES carry prim:OrderingCanonicalization because a set has no intrinsic order); any such effect belongs to the individual member encodings (an prim:Int32 member's prim:OverflowDomain, a prim:Float64 member's prim:RoundingIEEE754), not to the sequence that concatenates them. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:List is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type — an ordered homogeneous sequence — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy." .

prim:Literal a prim:FormalType .

prim:EncodingLoss a prim:Effect .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding (yang carrier) --------------------------
# The RDF-literal carrier: an octet-granular UTF-8 byte-vector holding the lexical form,
# paired with a datatype-IRI-or-language-tag coordinate. It is a COMPOSITE over two member
# encodings of the physical olog: the lexical run is a UTF-8 codepoint stream (member encoding
# prim:Utf8, itself interpretAs prim:Codepoint), and the datatype/language coordinate is a
# URN (member encoding prim:UrnUtf8, interpretAs prim:URN). Octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 is
# the per-occurrence granule, prim:byteCount 128 the ByteVector occurrence-count ceiling
# (spec §9c, second width cap). Byte descent: the underlying carrier is prim:ByteVector, which
# prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit —
# bottoming at the width-1 Bit floor, so no literal byte escapes the substrate. Endian-neutral
# because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering.
prim:LiteralUtf8Datatype a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 lexical form + datatype/language URN coordinate → Literal" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Literal ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-literal carrier and physical (yang) facet of the Literal primitive: an octet-granular UTF-8 byte-vector of the lexical form paired with a datatype-IRI-or-language-tag coordinate. It is a COMPOSITE over two member encodings of the physical olog — the lexical run realizes as a UTF-8 codepoint stream (member encoding prim:Utf8 → prim:Codepoint), and the datatype/language coordinate realizes as a name (member encoding prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN). Subatomic byte descent: the underlying prim:ByteVector byteDescendsTo prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, so the width-1 Bit floor grounds every lexical octet; prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence octet granule and prim:byteCount 128 the ByteVector occurrence-count ceiling (spec §9c). Endian-neutral, because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering. Its prim:interpretAs back-pointer targets prim:Literal itself (not prim:Codepoint or prim:URN), so the mutual colimit closes REFLEXIVELY onto Literal — the whole dual-natured term, not one of its member coordinates. The typed cost of parsing the byte run back to the abstract term is prim:EncodingLoss: lexical-to-value canonicalization ('1.0' vs '1.00', normalization-form and language-subtag case folding) means the byte round-trip is not the identity on the lexical form without a stated canonicalization discipline." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ (the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing yin → yang) ---------------
# prim:Literal realizesAs this arrow (the forward gluing edge → dual-grounding of the concrete
# type on ITSELF, spec §9a). The arrow IS a Frame: realizesFrom prim:Literal (source, yin),
# encoding/frameOutput prim:LiteralUtf8Datatype (target X, yang), frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss
# (the typed error|effect Y). No isProvisional: Literal is a resolvable concrete type.
prim:Literal prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Literal_Utf8Datatype .

prim:realize_Literal_Utf8Datatype a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Literal ⟼ UTF-8 lexical form + datatype/language coordinate" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Literal ;
    prim:encoding prim:LiteralUtf8Datatype ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:LiteralUtf8Datatype ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:Literal — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad gluing the formal RDF-term type (yin) to its octet-granular UTF-8 lexical+datatype carrier (yang). It IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): prim:realizesFrom prim:Literal is the source, prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:LiteralUtf8Datatype is the output X (which descends through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Bit to the byte floor), and prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss is the typed cost Y. The effect is genuine and not decorative: lexical-to-value canonicalization (e.g. the decimal lexicals '1.0' and '1.00' denoting one value, Unicode normalization forms, and BCP-47 language-subtag case folding) means the lexical byte run does not round-trip as the identity without a canonicalization discipline, so the arrow is lossy exactly where the W3C literal algebra says it is. This is a direct, resolvable realization of a concrete type — no isProvisional dodge (Präriehund honesty reserves that for genuinely undecidable gaps). TRANSITIVE-GROUNDING LINK: a typed literal additionally grounds, via its datatype URN, into another value tower — 'x'^^xsd:decimal into the Number tower (prim:Decimal), 'abc'@en into the Text tower (prim:String), '2026-08-08'^^xsd:date into the Temporal tower (prim:Date) — a semantic descent by datatype, distinct from the rdfs:subClassOf descent, that the datatype coordinate of prim:LiteralUtf8Datatype physically carries." .

prim:Longitude a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) type-specific PhysicalEncoding — interpretAs prim:Longitude (TRUE IDENTITY) --
prim:LongitudeFloat64 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Float64 (east-west angular degrees) → Longitude" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 11 ; prim:mantissaBits 52 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Longitude ;
    rdfs:comment "The type-specific east-west geographic-coordinate carrier: a single IEEE-754 Float64 scalar holding a longitude as signed angular degrees in the range −180..+180 about the prime meridian, occupying prim:bitWidth 64 / prim:byteWidth 8, laid little-endian (prim:LE). Structurally it IS a double — one IEEE-754 sign bit (prim:IEEE754Sign), an 11-bit biased exponent (prim:IEEE754Exponent, prim:exponentBits 11) and a 52-bit significand (prim:IEEE754Mantissa, prim:mantissaBits 52), summing 1+11+52 = 64 — so its ± sign is the IEEE-754 sign FIELD, not the integer-only prim:signedness facet (which the machine-type prim:Float64 likewise omits). It refines rather than reuses the generic prim:Float64/prim:CoordinateFloat64 machine encodings precisely so its prim:interpretAs points at prim:Longitude itself, making the mutual-colimit round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal land REFLEXIVELY on Longitude — its OWN direct self-grounding. prim:Longitude ⊂ prim:Coordinate is taxonomic subtyping; Longitude's PRIMARY grounding is DIRECT (this own reflexive carrier), NOT ancestral — yet, as STEP B now materialises, Longitude IS ALSO grounded through that ancestor by the real, resolvable path prim:agp_Longitude_Coordinate, whose prim:ancestralViaTransport prim:rt_sub_Longitude_Coordinate is the rho-image of the subtype arrow and carries THIS LongitudeFloat64 carrier up to prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding = rho(Coordinate); so the categorical also-grounded-through-ancestor fact is a genuine edge and Longitude is dual-grounded TWO ways (direct plus transported), the direct one unchanged. Subatomically the occurrence spans eight prim:Octet carriers serialized least-significant-octet-first; each Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:Bit (equivalently through prim:Byte), so the value bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the physical.ttl carrier ladder — no coordinate escapes the byte substrate. The antimeridian wrap-around at ±180 and the prime meridian and datum inherited from the enclosing Point/CRS are documented realization facets; forcing a real-valued degree onto the finite IEEE-754 double grid is the honest typed cost carried on the realization Frame as prim:RoundingIEEE754." .

# ---- (b) Realization — the Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ with the REAL typed effect ----------
prim:Longitude prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Longitude_Float64 .

prim:realize_Longitude_Float64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Longitude ⟼ Float64 · 8B · LE (east-west angular degrees)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Longitude ;
    prim:encoding prim:LongitudeFloat64 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:LongitudeFloat64 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ for Longitude, the unary law's Yoneda point Longitude → Frame(output: prim:LongitudeFloat64, effect: prim:RoundingIEEE754): it prim:realizesFrom prim:Longitude, produces the type-specific prim:LongitudeFloat64 double encoding as both its prim:encoding and its prim:frameOutput coordinate X, and carries the typed prim:RoundingIEEE754 effect Y that mapping a real-valued angular degree onto the finite IEEE-754 double grid incurs (all but exactly-representable degree values round to the nearest representable double). It is a GENUINE DIRECT realization, not a provisional one and not a bare inheritance: per spec §9a direct-for-concrete, prim:Longitude is a concrete leaf of the Coordinate tower, so it carries this own arrow for its DIRECT grounding rather than only inheriting the ancestral prim:realize_Coordinate_Float64 (whose encoding interprets back to the generic prim:Coordinate); STEP B additionally materialises the ancestral grounding as the real path prim:agp_Longitude_Coordinate (via the transport prim:rt_sub_Longitude_Coordinate landing in rho(Coordinate) = prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding), so the also-grounded-through-ancestor claim is now a resolvable edge complementing, never replacing, this direct arrow. No silm:isProvisional dodge is admitted — the arrow's encoding prim:interpretAs closes reflexively onto prim:Longitude, satisfying the true-identity requirement of spec §9d. As a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad it composes with the monad unit prim:realize_unit as the identity (left/right unit law), and its byte-descent to the prim:Bit floor is Kleisli composition down the Octet → Nibble → Bit ladder of physical.ttl." .

prim:Map a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: canonically key-sorted length-prefixed kv run → Map -
# A UNIQUE-KEYED, UNORDERED-over-entries, VARIABLE-length carrier, NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous
# single product and NOT an order-preserving element sequence: a UINT32 entry-count prefix followed by
# exactly that many contiguous (key, value) encoding PAIRS, the keys all of one key type and the
# values all of one value type, each key appearing AT MOST ONCE and the whole run laid in a CANONICAL
# KEY-SORT order chosen only for byte-level determinism (not for identity — a Map is unordered over
# its entries). It reuses the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl
# (which byteDescendsTo prim:Octet) for the run of entries, while each entry's key·value pair is a
# fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:CompositeCarrier product. Because a Map's entry count is not pinned,
# the carrier is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet),
# prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count
# ceiling (≤ 128, the ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits), since the
# total span is the entry-count prefix plus the sum of the key and value widths across entries and
# varies with the map. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 entry-count prefix is a
# little-endian unsigned integer (least-significant octet first). NO prim:signedness is declared:
# signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) and does not
# apply to an associative frame (as prim:List, prim:Set and prim:Tuple likewise omit it).
# prim:interpretAs prim:Map makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:MapAssociativeEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "canonically key-sorted length-prefixed key-value pair run (UINT32 entry-count + sorted unique-keyed key·value pairs) · octet-granular · LE → Map [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Map ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the map / dictionary / associative array: a CANONICALLY KEY-SORTED, LENGTH-PREFIXED run of (key, value) PAIRS — a UINT32 entry-count prefix (the machine type prim:Uint32 of physical.ttl, little-endian) followed by exactly that many contiguous key·value encoding pairs, the keys ALL of one key type and the values ALL of one value type, each key appearing AT MOST ONCE (the unique-key / partial-function invariant) and the whole run laid in a canonical KEY-SORT order chosen only for byte-level determinism — laid over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl for the run of entries. The unary law's UNORDERED collection families have their own separately identified alternatives and NEVER supply sequence order by convention, so, exactly as prim:Set does, the physical run is deliberately given a canonical sort (here BY KEY) purely to make the bytes deterministic; the abstract Map itself remains an orderless finite function on unique keys. Because a map's entry count is not pinned, the carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR — prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet of the code space, cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits; the scalar prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 cap does not bind a variable-length run) — since a map's total span is the entry-count prefix PLUS the sum of its key and value widths across all entries and so varies with the map. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 entry-count prefix serializes least-significant octet first; NO prim:signedness is declared because signedness is an integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) inapplicable to an associative frame, exactly as prim:List, prim:Set and prim:Tuple omit it. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): each ENTRY is a fixed-arity, heterogeneous prim:CompositeCarrier product of a key encoding then a value encoding — the key IS itself a floor prim:PhysicalEncoding of the ONE key type and the value IS itself a floor prim:PhysicalEncoding of the ONE value type — so a Map from prim:String to prim:Integer lays, per entry, a prim:StringUtf8 key octet run followed by a prim:Int32 two's-complement value word; a Map from prim:Codepoint to a nested prim:Map lays a prim:Utf8 key run followed by a nested key-sorted key-value run — and the map's bytes are exactly the entry-count prefix followed by those key·value pairs abutted in canonical key order. Because the key and value types are each uniform across entries and the entry count is explicit, there is NO per-entry tag and NO terminator, and the run of entries is a homogeneous prim:ByteVector-family run WHILE each individual entry is a two-component prim:CompositeCarrier product (a key of one width/encoding and a value of another). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ordered-vector carrier prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet and the composite entry carrier prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of the entry-count prefix, of every key, and of every value bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Map is a direct subclass of prim:FormalType in the Aggregate tower; it can be viewed as a prim:Set of key·value prim:Tuple pairs bearing a key-uniqueness constraint, but is kept its own object so the functional-lookup (partial function key → value) semantics are explicit, and it is kept distinct from the ordered prim:List / prim:Vector (order and positional duplicates ARE identity), the fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:Tuple (a single product, no length prefix, no key→value function), the plain unordered prim:Set (bare elements, not key→value entries), and prim:Bag (per-element multiplicities, not unique-keyed entries). TYPED EFFECT: because the abstract Map has NO order over its entries, imposing the canonical KEY-SORT serialization order is a real, typed transformation — two presentations of the same finite function that differ only in entry order map to the SAME bytes, so the realization carries prim:OrderingCanonicalization (entry-order information is deliberately discarded); it is NOT prim:NoEffect precisely because a genuine transformation (canonical key sorting) occurs — which is exactly why this canonical facet differs from the pre-existing prim:MapLenKv that stored a bare unordered run at prim:NoEffect — and it is NOT prim:EncodingLoss because no KEY and no VALUE is lost and the unique-key partial function is preserved exactly; only the meaningless entry ordering is normalized away. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Map closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the map type itself (spec §9d), not onto a prim:Set of pairs nor a bare prim:ByteVector, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Map to the encoding -----------------
# Map ⟼ canonically key-sorted length-prefixed key-value pair run as a Frame: realizesFrom Map,
# encoding/frameOutput the associative encoding above, frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization (a
# Map has no intrinsic order over its entries, so serializing it imposes a canonical key sort — a
# real transform that maps differently-ordered presentations to identical bytes while preserving the
# key→value function and key-uniqueness exactly). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional
# dodge, because Map is a concrete, directly-realizable finite partial function grounded on the
# key-sorted-kv-run-over-ByteVector carrier. This ADDS a second realizesAs edge alongside the
# pre-existing prim:realize_Map_LenKv (monad polymorphism).
prim:Map prim:realizesAs prim:MapRealizesAssociative .

prim:MapRealizesAssociative a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Map ⟼ canonically key-sorted length-prefixed key-value pair run · octet-granular · LE; frameEffect ordering-canonicalization" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Map ;
    prim:encoding prim:MapAssociativeEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:MapAssociativeEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Map — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Map (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType in the Aggregate tower — a finite partial function from a key type to a value type on unique keys, viewable as a prim:Set of key·value prim:Tuple pairs under a key-uniqueness constraint) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:MapAssociativeEncoding (yang X — the canonically key-sorted, length-prefixed run of the map's (key, value) encoding pairs over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier, each entry a prim:CompositeCarrier key·value product), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector / prim:CompositeCarrier → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Map realizesAs this canonical key-sorted arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_Map_LenKv (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings, each with its own typed effect, is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a hash-table layout, an insertion-order-preserving run (which would instead carry prim:NoEffect, like the pre-existing prim:MapLenKv), a sorted balanced-tree serialization, or a length-prefixed run of prim:CompositeCarrier key·value tuples are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same map realization, and this fragment commits to the CANONICALLY KEY-SORTED unique-keyed pair run as the general deterministic canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization — because a Map has NO intrinsic order over its entries, realizing it into a necessarily ordered byte serialization must impose a canonical KEY sort, so two presentations of the same finite function that differ only in entry order map to the SAME bytes; the mapping is therefore NOT the identity on entry-presentation order (that meaningless ordering is deliberately discarded), even though the key→value FUNCTION and the key-uniqueness invariant are preserved exactly. This is in deliberate contrast to prim:List, whose realization carries prim:NoEffect because a list's order and positional duplicates ARE its identity and are laid down verbatim, and to the pre-existing prim:MapLenKv, which carries prim:NoEffect because it stores a bare unordered run without canonicalizing; and it is distinct from prim:EncodingLoss because no key and no value is lost — only the input entry ordering is normalized away. It mirrors exactly the prim:OrderingCanonicalization prim:Set incurs for the same reason (an unordered collection forced into an ordered serialization). Any rounding, overflow, or encoding loss belongs to the individual key and value encodings (an prim:Int32 value's prim:OverflowDomain, a prim:Utf8 key's prim:EncodingLoss), not to the associative framing itself. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Map is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type — a finite partial function on unique keys — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy." .

prim:Measurement a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: Product(Float64 value, Float64 uncertainty) → Measurement ---
# A categorical PRODUCT carrier, NOT a scalar and NOT a homogeneous ByteVector: two same-WIDTH but
# distinct-ROLE prim:Real members (the measured value, then its uncertainty) laid contiguously. It
# reuses the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier and the IEEE-754 machine-type ladder of
# physical.ttl — each member IS a prim:Float64 encoding (bitWidth 64, IEEE-754 double, LE) — so the
# pair is a fixed-width scalar-family product of total bitWidth 128, exactly meeting the scalar cap
# prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c, the two-64-bit-component ceiling); it declares NO prim:byteCount
# (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap, which this fixed-arity product is not) and NO
# prim:signedness (an integer facet; each member's ± sign is its own prim:IEEE754Sign field, not the
# integer Signed/Unsigned facet). prim:alignment 8 packs each Float64 member on its natural 8-byte
# boundary; prim:padding 0 records that the two equal-width members abut with no filler.
# prim:interpretAs prim:Measurement makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:MeasurementProductEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(Float64 value, Float64 uncertainty) · 16B · LE · IEEE-754 → Measurement [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 128 ;
    prim:byteWidth 16 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ;
    prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Measurement ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of prim:Measurement: a categorical PRODUCT of two IEEE-754 binary64 (Float64) member encodings — the measured value first, then its paired uncertainty (error bar / standard deviation / confidence half-width) IN THE SAME UNIT — laid contiguously little-endian (least-significant octet first) over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl, for a total prim:bitWidth 128 and prim:byteWidth 16, aligned to the 8-byte Float64 boundary (prim:alignment 8) with no filler (prim:padding 0). MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law — the members are themselves floor primitives, turtles all the way down): each of the two members IS a prim:Float64 encoding from the IEEE-754 float machine-type ladder — prim:bitWidth 64 partitioned as one prim:IEEE754Sign bit + an 11-bit prim:IEEE754Exponent field + a 52-bit prim:IEEE754Mantissa significand (1 + 11 + 52 = 64), prim:byteOrder prim:LE — each occupying 8 whole prim:Octet occurrences and each itself interpreting to prim:Real (the value member is the measured magnitude, the uncertainty member is a same-unit non-negative dispersion of that same Number-tower kind). The fixed arity two (a value coordinate and an uncertainty coordinate) is part of the encoding's identity and carries no length prefix, which is exactly why a Measurement is a prim:CompositeCarrier PRODUCT and NOT a homogeneous variable-length prim:ByteVector counted by prim:byteCount. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the product carrier prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each of the 16 constituent octets (8 in the value member, 8 in the uncertainty member) descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of both Float64 members bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (the byte-stream carrier closure) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Measurement is a direct subclass of prim:Quantity — every measurement IS a quantity that additionally records how well it is known — so where a bare prim:Quantity carrier holds a single magnitude·unit and asserts an EXACT value, this carrier WIDENS the value coordinate into a value·uncertainty pair, making the empirical imprecision explicit and error propagation a first-class fact rather than an unstated assumption; the two members deliberately reuse the same real-axis Float64 carrier that grounds prim:Real (as prim:Complex reuses it for its real and imaginary axes), because a value and its uncertainty are the same kind of Number-tower quantity in the same unit. TYPED EFFECT: mapping the continuum of both the value and the uncertainty onto the finite grid of representable doubles is lossy, so the realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the same finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real Float64 carries, now borne on both the value and the uncertainty coordinates). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Measurement closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Measurement itself (spec §9d), not onto its parent prim:Quantity, not onto the prim:Real of a single member, and not onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Measurement to the encoding -----------
# Measurement ⟼ Product(Float64 value, Float64 uncertainty) as a Frame: realizesFrom Measurement,
# encoding/frameOutput the product encoding above, frameEffect the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754
# (the real typed cost of the two IEEE-754 double members). A total, well-typed realization — no
# isProvisional dodge, because Measurement is a concrete composite type directly grounded on the
# pair-of-doubles carrier. The forward dual-grounding edge prim:Measurement prim:realizesAs
# prim:MeasurementRealizesProduct is asserted so the fragment is self-sufficient for the
# dual-grounding law when read standalone.
prim:Measurement prim:realizesAs prim:MeasurementRealizesProduct .

prim:MeasurementRealizesProduct a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Measurement ⟼ Product(Float64 value, Float64 uncertainty) · 16B · LE; frameEffect IEEE754-rounding" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Measurement ;
    prim:encoding prim:MeasurementProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:MeasurementProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Measurement — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Measurement (yin, formal.ttl — a Quantity enriched with an uncertainty, the Product(Real value, Real uncertainty) that specializes prim:Quantity) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:MeasurementProductEncoding (yang X — the value·uncertainty product of two Float64 members), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier to prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Measurement realizesAs this canonical (value, uncertainty) Float64-pair arrow; a wider pair of Float128 members, an (interval low, interval high) endpoint pair, a value-plus-relative-error scalar, or a value-plus-significant-figures encoding are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same measurement realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the symmetric value·uncertainty Float64-pair as Measurement's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 — each of the two members maps its coordinate from the continuum ℝ onto the finite grid of representable IEEE-754 doubles, so all but exactly-representable values round to the nearest grid point; the effect is the SAME finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real Float64 realization carries, now borne on both the value and the uncertainty coordinates (the rounding of the uncertainty member is itself part of the honest error budget). Because the effect is a genuine non-neutral member (not prim:NoEffect), it records that realizing a measurement is never free — exactly why realization is monadic rather than a bare total function. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Measurement is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type (a value·uncertainty pair), not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:Natural a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) type-specific PhysicalEncoding (yang) -------------------------------------
# The canonical physical facet of ℕ: a 64-bit UNSIGNED machine word, little-endian, selected
# from the UINT8/UINT16/UINT32/UINT64 unsigned machine-type ladder in physical.ttl as the
# widest common register width (matching the INT64 canonical choice of frags/Integer.ttl for
# its signed sibling ℤ). interpretAs prim:Natural makes the colimit round-trip reflexive.
prim:NaturalCanonicalEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT64 · 8B · LE · unsigned (canonical physical facet of ℕ)" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ;
    prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Natural ;
    prim:uefiName "UINT64" ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the natural numbers ℕ: an unsigned 64-bit machine word laid down little-endian (least-significant octet first), a scalar fixed-width encoding whose prim:bitWidth 64 stays under the 128-bit scalar cap (spec §9c) — prim:byteCount is deliberately ABSENT because a fixed-width scalar is not a variable-length prim:ByteVector, and the second (byteCount ≤ 128) cap governs only the ByteVector family. It is selected from the UINT8/UINT16/UINT32/UINT64 unsigned machine-type ladder authored in physical.ttl as the widest common register width; the narrower UINT8/16/32 widths are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same unsigned realization, differing only in their prim:bitWidth and (for UINT8) prim:byteOrder facets. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:signedness prim:Unsigned is EXACTLY what distinguishes ℕ's carrier from the signed INT carrier of its supertype prim:Integer (whose domain straddles zero via prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement); an unsigned encoding admits no negative values and therefore declares NO integer-representation negative scheme at all, and its non-negative domain 0..2⁶⁴−1 fixes the ordinal floor at 0 for the prim:NaturalWithZero reading or 1 for the prim:NaturalWithoutZero reading — the two named sub-objects that split the honest zero-inclusion fork rather than force-fitting one (Präriehund honesty). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 8 octets of this word each descend through the physical carrier ladder prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the encoding bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor of the byte-stream carrier closure (unary law) — there are NO member sub-encodings, since ℕ is a scalar count and not a categorical product. TYPED EFFECT: the fixed 64-bit window represents only a finite prefix 0..2⁶⁴−1 of the unbounded counting line, so realizing an out-of-window magnitude carries prim:OverflowDomain — the honest, width-bounded cost that the arbitrary-precision relative prim:Bignum escapes by trading this fixed carrier for a prim:ByteVector. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:Natural closes the round-trip REFLEXIVELY onto ℕ itself (spec §9d), not onto a shared proxy, so yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ (the gluing Kleisli/Frame arrow) ------------------------
# ℕ ⟼ UINT64 as a Frame: realizesFrom Natural, encoding/frameOutput the encoding above,
# frameEffect the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain (the real typed cost of a fixed carrier).
prim:Natural prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Natural_Canonical .

prim:realize_Natural_Canonical a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℕ ⟼ UINT64 · 8B · LE (canonical Kleisli/Frame realization of Natural)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Natural ;
    prim:encoding prim:NaturalCanonicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:NaturalCanonicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical realization ρ of the natural numbers ℕ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) rather than a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Natural (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Integer) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:NaturalCanonicalEncoding (yang, the unsigned 64-bit little-endian word), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying physical encoding that descends through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Natural realizesAs this arrow AND the sibling UINT8/UINT16/UINT32 arrows of the unsigned machine-type ladder — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is exactly the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the UINT64 width as ℕ's canonical facet (the narrower widths trade range for compactness under the same prim:OverflowDomain effect). TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain — the fixed 64-bit carrier holds only the finite window 0..2⁶⁴−1 of the unbounded counting line, so magnitudes above that window wrap, saturate, or trap; the bound is exact and known per width, the honest cost of a bounded carrier that the arbitrary-precision prim:Bignum relative avoids by realizing into a prim:ByteVector with a prim:MayOverflow effect instead. This arrow is a total, well-typed realization — NO silm:isProvisional dodge — because prim:Natural is a concrete, directly-realizable unsigned type, not an unresolved abstract supertype (spec §9a/§9b)." .

prim:NaturalWithZero a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) type-specific PhysicalEncoding (yang) -------------------------------------
# The canonical physical facet of ℕ₀: a single-octet UNSIGNED UINT8, endian-neutral (one
# octet has no cross-octet ordering), selected from the UINT8/UINT16/UINT32/UINT64 unsigned
# machine-type ladder in physical.ttl (structurally identical to prim:Uint8). Its unsigned
# code space 0..255 has ordinal floor 0 — the offset-free home of the 0-inclusive reading —
# and interpretAs prim:NaturalWithZero makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:NaturalWithZeroCanonicalEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT8 · 1B · endian-neutral · unsigned, ordinal floor 0 (canonical physical facet of ℕ₀)" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:NaturalWithZero ;
    prim:uefiName "UINT8" ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the natural-numbers-with-zero ℕ₀ = {0, 1, 2, ...}: an unsigned single-octet UINT8, a scalar fixed-width encoding whose prim:bitWidth 8 sits far under the 128-bit scalar cap (spec §9c). prim:byteCount is deliberately ABSENT because a fixed-width scalar is NOT a variable-length prim:ByteVector — the second (byteCount ≤ 128) cap governs only the ByteVector family — and prim:byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because a single octet has no multi-octet ordering to fix (matching prim:Uint8 in physical.ttl). SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:NaturalWithZero rdfs:subClassOf prim:Natural, and its distinguishing feature versus the sibling prim:NaturalWithoutZero (ordinal floor 1) is the 0-INCLUSIVE ordinal floor. An unsigned code space is exactly 0..2ⁿ−1 with ordinal floor 0, so ℕ₀ is the OFFSET-FREE identification of the counting value with the unsigned code (whereas ℕ⁺ would need a +1 interpretation offset) — this is why UINT is ℕ₀'s natural carrier. prim:signedness prim:Unsigned — the unsigned facet shared across the Natural sub-tower — is what separates the whole Natural sub-tower from the signed INT carrier of the supertype prim:Integer; an unsigned encoding admits no negatives and so declares NO integer-representation negative scheme (two's-/one's-complement / sign-magnitude apply only to signed encodings). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: this ONE octet descends through the physical carrier ladder prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the encoding bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor of the byte-stream carrier closure (unary law) — there are NO member sub-encodings, since ℕ₀ is a scalar count and not a categorical product. TYPED EFFECT: the fixed 8-bit window represents only the finite prefix 0..255 of the unbounded counting line, so realizing an out-of-window magnitude carries prim:OverflowDomain — the honest, width-bounded cost; the wider sibling UINT16/32/64 widths merely enlarge that finite window under the same effect, and the arbitrary-precision relative prim:Bignum escapes it entirely by trading the fixed carrier for a prim:ByteVector with prim:MayOverflow. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:NaturalWithZero closes the round-trip REFLEXIVELY onto ℕ₀ itself (spec §9d), NOT onto its taxonomic supertype ℕ (whose own arrows interpret back to ℕ, not ℕ₀), so yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ (the gluing Kleisli/Frame arrow) — the OWN realization ---
# ℕ₀ ⟼ UINT8 as a Frame: realizesFrom NaturalWithZero, encoding/frameOutput the encoding
# above, frameEffect the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain (the real typed cost of a fixed
# carrier). This is ℕ₀'s OWN direct realization onto its OWN reflexive carrier; prim:Natural is
# only its taxonomic supertype (rdfs:subClassOf), not the source of ℕ₀'s grounding (spec §9a).
prim:NaturalWithZero prim:realizesAs prim:realize_NaturalWithZero_Canonical .

prim:realize_NaturalWithZero_Canonical a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℕ₀ ⟼ UINT8 · 1B · endian-neutral (own canonical Kleisli/Frame realization of NaturalWithZero)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:NaturalWithZero ;
    prim:encoding prim:NaturalWithZeroCanonicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:NaturalWithZeroCanonicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The OWN canonical realization ρ of the natural-numbers-with-zero ℕ₀ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) rather than a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:NaturalWithZero (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Natural) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:NaturalWithZeroCanonicalEncoding (yang, the unsigned single-octet UINT8), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying physical encoding that descends through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. OWN DIRECT GROUNDING (spec §9a, task): ℕ₀ is grounded by THIS, its OWN direct realization, whose encoding closes the round-trip as a TRUE IDENTITY onto NaturalWithZero (spec §9d). prim:NaturalWithZero rdfs:subClassOf prim:Natural is taxonomic subtyping only, NOT the grounding mechanism: prim:Natural's own UINT8/UINT16/UINT32/UINT64 arrows interpret back to prim:Natural (landing on ℕ, not reflexively on ℕ₀), which is exactly why ℕ₀ needs and carries this own reflexive arrow. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: one formal type fanning out to many encodings (this own UINT8 arrow plus its sibling polymorphic wider unsigned widths) is exactly the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; this fragment commits to the minimal single-octet UINT8 as ℕ₀'s canonical facet for the tightest subatomic byte descent. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain — the fixed 8-bit carrier holds only the finite window 0..255 of the unbounded counting line, so magnitudes above it wrap, saturate, or trap; the bound is exact and known per width, the honest cost of a bounded carrier. This arrow is a total, well-typed realization — NO silm:isProvisional dodge — because prim:NaturalWithZero is a concrete, directly-realizable 0-inclusive unsigned type, not an unresolved abstract supertype (spec §9a/§9b)." .

prim:NaturalWithoutZero a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding — UINT32 with ordinal floor 1 --------------
prim:NaturalWithoutZeroUint32 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT32 (ordinal floor 1; strictly positive) → NaturalWithoutZero" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ; prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ; prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:NaturalWithoutZero ;
    rdfs:comment "The strictly-positive natural-number carrier: an unsigned 32-bit machine integer holding a value of ℕ⁺ = {1, 2, 3, ...}, laid out as four little-endian octets, signedness prim:Unsigned — the same UINT32 bit-layout precedent physical.ttl records on prim:Uint32, but a DISTINCT encoding because its ordinal floor is 1, not 0. The code point 0 is excluded from its interpreted domain, so the represented window is exactly {1 .. 2^32-1} of the unbounded strictly-positive counting line; that excluded-zero floor is precisely what separates ℕ⁺ from the zero-inclusive prim:Natural in the formal tower. This is a fixed-width SCALAR encoding, so it is ceilinged by the prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 scalar cap (32 ≤ 128) and carries no prim:byteCount — byteCount is the second, ByteVector-family occurrence cap and is not applicable to a scalar machine word. Subatomic descent: the 32-bit occurrence spans exactly four prim:Octet carriers, each of which prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence to the width-1 prim:Bit floor along the physical.ttl carrier ladder, so no bit of the value escapes the byte substrate (Byte ≠ Octet holds — this width is octet-composed, not a single addressable Byte). prim:interpretAs prim:NaturalWithoutZero closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY: the round-trip physical → interpretAs lands back on ℕ⁺ itself, not on prim:Natural as a zero-inclusive proxy would, so the identity seal is true (spec §9d). The finite 32-bit window pitched against an unbounded ℕ⁺ is the honest typed prim:OverflowDomain cost carried on the gluing Frame." .

# ---- (b) The ρ realization — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the REAL typed effect --------
prim:NaturalWithoutZero prim:realizesAs prim:realize_NaturalWithoutZero_Uint32 .

prim:realize_NaturalWithoutZero_Uint32 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ℕ⁺ ⟼ UINT32 (ordinal floor 1; overflow-domain)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:NaturalWithoutZero ;
    prim:encoding prim:NaturalWithoutZeroUint32 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:NaturalWithoutZeroUint32 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ of the colimit taiji for ℕ⁺: a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad that IS the unary-law Yoneda point ℕ⁺ → Frame(output: UINT32 with ordinal floor 1, error|effect: overflow-domain). It prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:NaturalWithoutZero (the strictly-positive ℕ⁺ that sits directly beneath prim:Natural, hence ⊂ prim:Integer ⊂ prim:Rational ⊂ prim:Real ⊂ prim:Complex), and its prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput is the dedicated prim:NaturalWithoutZeroUint32 whose prim:interpretAs back-pointer closes onto ℕ⁺ again. Its prim:frameEffect is prim:OverflowDomain — the genuine, non-decorative cost of forcing the mathematically unbounded strictly-positive counting line into a fixed 32-bit window {1 .. 2^32-1}, past which a value wraps, saturates, or traps; this is the same width-bounded effect the prim:Natural → UINT arrows carry, and it is what makes the realization monadic rather than a bare total function. ℕ⁺ is polymorphic in its realization (a UINT8/UINT16/UINT64 arrow of matching ordinal-floor-1 discipline would be a sibling of this one, each with its own overflow window); this 32-bit arrow is the canonical realization the taiji primitive below selects. It is NOT provisional: ℕ⁺ is a resolvable in-scope type with a real, reflexive carrier, so no silm:isProvisional dodge is warranted (spec §9b)." .

prim:Optional a prim:FormalType .

# The dual-grounding forward edge: prim:Optional realizesAs its own Kleisli/Frame arrow.
prim:Optional prim:realizesAs prim:OptionalRealizesPresence .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: presence tag (0|1) + optional payload → Optional ----
# A TAGGED, two-alternative frame — the coproduct Sum(Unit, T) laid to bytes: a UINT8 presence tag
# (0 = none, 1 = some) followed, WHEN and only when the tag is 1, by the payload encoding of the wrapped
# type T. It reuses the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl (which
# byteDescendsTo prim:Octet). Because the some-payload's width varies with the wrapped type and the none
# case carries zero payload octets, the carrier is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the
# per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128
# the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (≤ 128, the ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c,
# up to 1024 bits), since the total span is one tag octet plus zero-or-the-payload width. prim:byteOrder
# prim:LE because the UINT8 tag is an unsigned integer serialized least-significant octet first (a single
# octet is order-trivial, but the frame as a whole commits to little-endian for its multi-octet payloads).
# NO prim:signedness is declared: signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned →
# Integer/Natural) and does not apply to a tagged-union frame (as prim:Sum and prim:Tuple likewise omit
# it). prim:interpretAs prim:Optional makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:OptionalPresenceEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "presence tag (UINT8 0=none | 1=some) + optional payload · octet-granular · LE → Optional [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Optional ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the optional / maybe type: a PRESENCE-TAG-PLUS-OPTIONAL-PAYLOAD frame — a UINT8 presence tag (the machine type prim:Uint8 of physical.ttl, 0 = none, 1 = some, little-endian) followed, WHEN and only when the tag is 1, by the payload encoding of the wrapped type T — laid over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl. This is the coproduct Sum(Unit, T) rendered to bytes: the none arm is the terminal prim:Unit realized as the zero-width prim:UnitEncoding (tag byte only, zero payload octets), and the some arm is the tag byte followed by T's own floor encoding. Because an option's some-payload width varies with the wrapped type and the none case carries zero payload octets, the carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR under the two-cap model (spec §9c): prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap, up to 1024 bits), since an option's total span is exactly one tag octet plus zero-or-the-payload width and so varies with the wrapped value. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT8 presence tag serializes least-significant octet first and the frame commits little-endian for any multi-octet payload; NO prim:signedness is declared because signedness is an integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) inapplicable to a tagged-union frame, exactly as prim:Sum and prim:Tuple omit it. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): the tag member IS the floor machine encoding prim:Uint8 (one octet, a two-state discriminant used here over exactly the two alternatives none | some — a specialisation of prim:Sum's general N-way UINT8 tag), and the payload member, when present, IS itself any floor prim:PhysicalEncoding of the wrapped type T — an optional prim:Codepoint lays the tag octet then a prim:Utf8 octet run, an optional prim:Integer lays the tag octet then a prim:Int32 two's-complement word, an optional prim:Real lays the tag octet then a prim:Float64 — so the option's bytes are exactly the presence octet followed by zero payload octets (none) or the wrapped type's member byte run (some). Contrast the sibling prim:Sum frame, whose UINT8 tag ranges over arbitrarily many typed alternatives each with its own payload; Optional fixes the tag domain to exactly {absent, present} and fixes the absent arm's payload to the empty product prim:Unit. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ordered-vector carrier prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the presence tag octet and every octet of the some-payload bottom out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it; the presence bit itself is, at its floor, exactly one prim:Bit even though it is carried in a whole-octet tag for addressability. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Optional is a direct subclass of prim:Sum in the Algebraic-constructor tower — it is precisely the two-alternative tagged union none | some(value), a Sum specialised to two arms whose first is the terminal prim:Unit — and it is how this floor represents NULLABILITY structurally, deliberately kept distinct from the three-valued prim:Kleene3 (absence is a STRUCTURAL fact, a missing value, not a third truth value) and from the general N-way prim:Sum. TYPED EFFECT: a faithful presence-tag-plus-payload distinguishes ABSENT from PRESENT exactly and lays the payload verbatim in position after the tag, so the option framing is LOSSLESS and the realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect; any rounding, overflow, or encoding loss belongs to the individual payload encoding of the wrapped type T, not to the presence framing. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Optional closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the option type itself (spec §9d), not onto its prim:Sum supertype nor a bare prim:ByteVector, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Optional to the encoding --------------
# Optional ⟼ presence-tag-plus-optional-payload as a Frame: realizesFrom Optional, encoding/frameOutput
# the presence encoding above, frameEffect prim:NoEffect (faithful presence-tag-plus-payload is lossless
# framing). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Optional is a concrete,
# directly-realizable two-alternative tagged union grounded on the presence-tag-plus-payload-over-
# ByteVector carrier. This ADDS a second realizesAs edge alongside the pre-existing
# prim:realize_Optional_Presence (monad polymorphism).
prim:OptionalRealizesPresence a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Optional ⟼ presence tag (0|1) + optional payload · octet-granular · LE; frameEffect none (lossless framing)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Optional ;
    prim:encoding prim:OptionalPresenceEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:OptionalPresenceEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Optional — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Optional (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Sum in the Algebraic-constructor tower — the two-alternative tagged union none | some, the coproduct Sum(Unit, T)) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:OptionalPresenceEncoding (yang X — the presence-tag-plus-optional-payload frame over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier: a UINT8 tag then, when present, the wrapped type's member encoding), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. This mirrors the unary law's discipline that an admission gate's Accepted|Rejected state is carried inside a Frame product, never substituted for it: Optional's none|some is likewise the output coordinate of a genuine Frame, not a raw sum standing in for the Frame. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Optional realizesAs this canonical presence-tag arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_Optional_Presence (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a single presence BIT packed into a flag word, a sentinel/null-pointer representation, or a distinguished out-of-band none value are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same option realization, and this fragment commits to the one-octet presence tag plus payload as the general canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — a faithful presence-tag-plus-payload is LOSSLESS framing: the tag octet decides absent from present unambiguously and, in the present case, the wrapped payload's byte run is laid down verbatim in position after the tag, so the option framing preserves the present/absent distinction and the payload exactly and introduces no rounding, narrowing, overflow, encoding loss, or ordering canonicalization of its own; any such effect belongs to the individual payload encoding of the wrapped type T (an prim:Int32 payload's prim:OverflowDomain, a prim:Float64 payload's prim:RoundingIEEE754, a prim:Utf8 payload's prim:EncodingLoss), not to the presence frame that wraps it. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Optional is a concrete, directly-realizable algebraic-constructor type — a two-alternative tagged union — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy. Composing this arrow byte-descends to the prim:Bit floor along the physical carrier ladder; the identity of that composition is the monad unit η." .

prim:Ordinal a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific physical encoding — Ordinal as an ORDER-PRESERVING UINT32 code.
# =====================================================================================
prim:Ordinal_Uint32Index a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT32 order-preserving member code → Ordinal (monotone ranked index) [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 32 ;
    prim:byteWidth 4 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Ordinal ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of an ordinal (ranked) enumeration: an ORDER-PRESERVING unsigned 32-bit member code (UINT32), 32 bits laid across four whole prim:Octet occurrences, in which each named member is assigned a distinct integer code SUCH THAT the numeric ordering of the codes reproduces the intended member ordering exactly (code(a) < code(b) iff a precedes b in the total order). SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Ordinal rdfs:subClassOf prim:Enumeration rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType — an Ordinal IS an Enumeration that additionally carries a total order, so it structurally reuses its parent's UINT32 member-code carrier but SPECIALISES it with a monotonicity invariant the bare Enumeration lacks: where a generic enumeration code is an arbitrary identity label (only equality is meaningful), an ordinal code is a RANK on which comparison and monotone operations are meaningful. This is precisely what distinguishes prim:Ordinal from its sibling prim:Categorical, the unordered reading whose codes must NOT leak a spurious order. WIDTH CHOICE: UINT32 (not UINT8/UINT16) because a totally ordered finite named set may hold far more than 256 or 65,536 ranked members while staying finite (long ranked vocabularies, ordered taxonomies, the 21-column Atlas ring extended); the ~4.29-billion-code window admits any in-scope ordinal, and UINT8/UINT16/UINT64 are the sibling polymorphic facets a small or very large ordinal may narrow or widen onto (monad fan-out). GOVERNING CAP: the fixed-width scalar cap prim:bitWidth 32 ≤ 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately NO prim:byteCount (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap, not a scalar's). prim:signedness prim:Unsigned marks the rank code as a non-negative index (there is no negative member ordinal; ranks begin at a non-negative floor and increase monotonically). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the 32 bits occupy exactly four whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:LE — least-significant octet first), each prim:Octet of cardinality 256 and ordinal range 0..255; each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so via the physical.ttl carrier ladder every bit of this encoding bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it (the byte-stream carrier closure, subatomic to the bit). Count-256, ordinal-255 and bit-width-128 remain three distinct navigable coordinates per the unary law. NO COMPOSITE MEMBERS: an ordinal value is a single atomic rank code, not a product of components, so this is a scalar carrier and not a prim:CompositeCarrier product; the order-preserving code-to-member mapping is part of the ordinal's identity, held in its schema rather than serialized alongside each value. TYPED EFFECT: the assignment of a distinct ORDER-PRESERVING code to each ranked member is a MONOTONE BIJECTION onto a chosen finite range, so storing and reading back the code is exact and lossless AND the reconstructed order is faithful — its realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect, the neutral element; it is genuinely NoEffect and not prim:OverflowDomain because the set is finite and its codes are chosen up front to fit the 32-bit window, and not prim:OrderingCanonicalization because the order is PRESERVED not normalised away (an Ordinal already HAS an order to keep, unlike a Set whose absent order must be imposed). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Ordinal closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Ordinal itself (spec §9d), NOT onto the bare prim:Natural (whose UINT32 layout it structurally mirrors), NOT onto the supertype prim:Enumeration (whose generic prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index would ground Ordinal only by ancestry, losing the order-preservation distinction) and NOT onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the REAL typed effect.
# =====================================================================================
# Ordinal ⟼ order-preserving UINT32 code as a Frame: realizesFrom Ordinal, encoding/frameOutput
# the encoding above, frameEffect the exact-and-lossless prim:NoEffect. The forward dual-grounding
# edge prim:Ordinal prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Ordinal_Uint32Index is asserted so the fragment is
# self-sufficient for the dual-grounding law when read standalone.
prim:Ordinal prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Ordinal_Uint32Index .

prim:realize_Ordinal_Uint32Index a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Ordinal ⟼ UINT32 order-preserving member code (monotone identity index; frameEffect NoEffect)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Ordinal ;
    prim:encoding prim:Ordinal_Uint32Index ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:Ordinal_Uint32Index ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Ordinal — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Ordinal (yin, formal.ttl — a finite named set of members carrying a TOTAL ORDER, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Enumeration, the ordered sibling of prim:Categorical) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:Ordinal_Uint32Index (yang X — the order-preserving unsigned 32-bit rank code), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through four prim:Octet occurrences to prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. OWN DIRECT GROUNDING (spec §9a): prim:Ordinal is grounded by THIS, its OWN direct realization, whose encoding prim:Ordinal_Uint32Index interpretAs lands reflexively on Ordinal itself. prim:Ordinal rdfs:subClassOf prim:Enumeration is taxonomic subtyping only, NOT the grounding mechanism: the generic prim:realize_Enumeration_Uint32Index carrier interprets back to prim:Enumeration and would lose the specialising ORDER-PRESERVATION invariant (numeric code order reproduces member order), which is exactly why Ordinal carries this own reflexive arrow rather than leaning on the parent. It is a type-specific sibling of prim:realize_Enumeration_Uint32Index exactly as prim:realize_Latitude_Float64 is of prim:realize_Coordinate_Float64. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Ordinal realizesAs this canonical UINT32 code arrow; a UINT8 rank (for an ordinal of ≤256 members), a UINT16 rank (≤65,536), or a UINT64 rank are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same ordinal realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the UINT32 order-preserving code as Ordinal's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — the assignment of a distinct order-preserving code to each ranked member is a MONOTONE BIJECTION onto a finite range, so the realization is exact and lossless AND the total order is faithfully reconstructed; it introduces no rounding (there is no continuum), no narrowing, and no overflow (the set is finite and its codes fit the width up front), and it is distinct from prim:OrderingCanonicalization precisely because the order is PRESERVED, not discarded-and-reimposed as it is for an unordered prim:Set. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Ordinal is a concrete, directly-realizable ordered-finite-named-set type, not an unresolved abstract supertype; composing this arrow with the monad unit η leaves it unchanged (left/right unit law), and byte-descent is the Kleisli composition of this realization down through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor." .

prim:Point a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:Point — a Well-Known-Binary geometry.
#     interpretAs prim:Point (the mutual-colimit back-pointer) → the round-trip is a TRUE
#     IDENTITY, landing back on Point, not on its constituent Coordinate/Real members.
# =====================================================================================
prim:PointWkbGeometry a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "WKB Point (byte-order flag + geometry-type UINT32 + Product(Float64 X, Float64 Y)) → Point" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 21 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 1 ; prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Point ;
    rdfs:comment "The point-geometry carrier: a Well-Known-Binary (ISO 13249 / OGC WKB) serialization of a zero-dimensional Point — the subtype grounding for the geospatial tower's atom that Line and Polygon are built from by sequence-composition (they are composites over Point, not subtypes of it). Subatomically it is a packed, octet-granular byte string of three member encodings laid contiguously with NO inter-field alignment padding (prim:alignment 1, prim:padding 0): a one-octet prim:Uint8 byte-order flag (0x01 NDR little-endian / 0x00 XDR big-endian — WKB is self-describing about its own endianness), a four-octet prim:Uint32 geometry-type code (1 = Point, 1001 = PointZ, 2001 = PointM, 3001 = PointZM), then the coordinate payload: a Product(prim:Float64 X/longitude, prim:Float64 Y/latitude) of two IEEE-754 doubles over prim:CompositeCarrier (optional Z/M ordinates extend the payload and the byteCount). The canonical 2D form is exactly 21 byte occurrences (1 + 4 + 8 + 8), so prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence octet granule and prim:byteCount 21 the occurrence count, comfortably under the 128-byte ByteVector cap. Byte descent: each occurrence is one prim:Octet, and prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so the whole geometry bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor; each embedded Float64 coordinate is eight octets descending the same Octet → Nibble → Bit ladder. The axis order (lon-lat vs lat-lon) and the referencing CRS are load-bearing facets recorded on the realization, not guessed. prim:interpretAs prim:Point closes the mutual colimit reflexively — the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal returns Point itself — and because the coordinate members are finite-grid IEEE-754 Float64 the realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the framing bytes themselves are lossless)." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing prim:Point → prim:PointWkbGeometry
#     with the REAL typed effect (IEEE-754 coordinate rounding).
# =====================================================================================
prim:Point prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Point_WkbGeometry .

prim:realize_Point_WkbGeometry a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Point ⟼ Well-Known-Binary point geometry (Product(Float64, Float64); IEEE-754 rounding)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Point ;
    prim:encoding prim:PointWkbGeometry ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:PointWkbGeometry ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ gluing the formal prim:Point (yin) to its physical WKB carrier prim:PointWkbGeometry (yang): prim:realizesFrom prim:Point, prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:PointWkbGeometry, prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754. It IS a Frame in the unary law's T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) sense — the output X is the WKB byte string and the typed effect Y is the IEEE-754 rounding incurred because a Point's real-valued coordinates are forced onto the finite Float64 grid. The WKB framing itself (the prim:Uint8 byte-order flag, the prim:Uint32 geometry-type code, and the contiguous concatenation of the two prim:Float64 ordinates) is exact and lossless, so the honest DOMINANT cost is the coordinate rounding carried up from the Float64 members, not a framing loss — which is why the effect is prim:RoundingIEEE754 and not prim:EncodingLoss or prim:NoEffect. Point is a concrete geometry with no subtypes (Line and Polygon are built OVER it by sequence-composition, not derived FROM it), so it carries its OWN realization directly rather than inheriting one from a realized ancestor or being covered by a descendant — it is fully resolvable this iteration, so NO silm:isProvisional dodge is used. Composing this arrow with the monad unit η is identity (the left/right unit law); byte-descending it is Kleisli composition down to the prim:Bit floor through the physical carrier ladder its encoding rides." .

prim:PolygonWkbEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Well-Known-Binary polygon geometry (fragment carrier) -> Polygon" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Polygon ;
    rdfs:comment "The Polygon Well-Known-Binary carrier: an octet-granular serialized-binary encoding of a two-dimensional area geometry bounded by one closed exterior ring and zero or more interior rings (holes), laid into a prim:ByteVector of up to 128 byte occurrences (prim:byteCount 128, the ByteVector second width cap of spec §9c; prim:bitWidth 8 is the per-occurrence octet granule, not a scalar width, and prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule). Subatomically it is a categorical product over prim:CompositeCarrier whose MEMBER ENCODINGS are drawn from physical.ttl: one leading prim:Octet byte-order flag (0x01 NDR little-endian / 0x00 XDR big-endian, so the byte stream is self-describing — prim:LE is the canonical NDR variant chosen here), a prim:Uint32 geometry-type code (3 = Polygon), a prim:Uint32 ring count, and per ring a prim:Uint32 vertex count followed by that many (prim:Float64 X, prim:Float64 Y) coordinate pairs. prim:signedness is deliberately omitted because the vector is heterogeneous — the counts are unsigned UINT32 while the coordinates carry the IEEE-754 sign bit — so no single signedness applies to the whole carrier (Präriehund honesty: name the mixture, do not force-fit one). BYTE DESCENT: the encoding is a prim:ByteVector, which prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet -> prim:Nibble -> prim:Bit along the ladder edges in physical.ttl, so no geometry escapes the width-1 Bit substrate. Ring closure (first vertex equals last) and winding order are well-formedness facets recorded so exterior and holes stay unambiguous. prim:interpretAs prim:Polygon closes the mutual colimit reflexively — the round-trip physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet lands back on prim:Polygon, a TRUE identity rather than a shared-carrier proxy." .

# ---- (b) the Realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the real typed effect ---------
prim:realize_Polygon_WkbFrame a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Polygon ⟼ Well-Known-Binary polygon geometry" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Polygon ;
    prim:encoding prim:PolygonWkbEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:PolygonWkbEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The Polygon realization ρ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad gluing the formal geometry prim:Polygon (yin, formal.ttl) to its byte carrier prim:PolygonWkbEncoding (yang): it declares prim:realizesFrom prim:Polygon, prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput the WKB encoding (the Frame output coordinate X, which descends to the byte floor), and prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 (the typed cost coordinate Y). The effect is real and load-bearing, not decorative: the polygon's ring vertices are embedded as IEEE-754 prim:Float64 coordinate pairs, so every coordinate that is not exactly representable is rounded to the nearest representable grid point when the geometry is realized — the same finite-grid rounding a bare prim:Real carries, inherited here through the composite geometry. This is a genuine grounding, NOT a provisional dodge: prim:Polygon is a concrete geometry (a direct subclass of prim:FormalType with no abstract descendant to defer through), so it carries its own realization and NO silm:isProvisional marker (spec §9b, Directive on no PROVISIONAL-dodge for resolvable types). Composing this arrow with the monad unit η leaves it unchanged (unit law), and byte-descending its output is Kleisli composition down the prim:ByteVector -> prim:Octet -> prim:Bit ladder." .

prim:QName a prim:FormalType .

# ---- Forward dual-grounding edge: the FormalType realizesAs its ρ arrow -----------------
prim:QName prim:realizesAs prim:realize_QName_Utf8 .

# ---- (a) Type-specific PhysicalEncoding — interpretAs prim:QName (reflexive identity) ---
prim:QNameUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet stream (compact prefix:local CURIE) → QName" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:QName ;
    rdfs:comment "The qualified-name (CURIE / compact-IRI) carrier: an interpreted UTF-8 octet stream serializing a compact prefix:local identifier such as prim:Real, octet-granular (prim:bitWidth 8 is the per-occurrence octet granule, prim:byteCount 128 the ByteVector-family occurrence ceiling of the second width cap, spec §9c) and endian-neutral because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering to fix. QName is the leaf of the identifier tower QName ⊂ IRI ⊂ URI ⊂ URN, denoting an IRI in an abbreviated lexical form rather than a distinct kind of resource; its two lexical parts — a namespace prefix and a local name joined by a single colon — are each a run of Unicode Codepoints, and every Codepoint realizes to 1..4 UTF-8 octets (the prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf8 idiom), so the whole compact name is a bounded ordered sequence of Octet occurrences over the byte carrier. Subatomic descent: each Octet prim:byteDescendsTo Nibble then Bit, so this carrier bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor with no residue, exactly as prim:UrnUtf8 and prim:StringUtf8 do. prim:interpretAs prim:QName closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY — the physical → interpretAs → formal round-trip lands back on QName itself, a TRUE identity (spec §9d), not on the ancestor URN whose bytes the same octet run could also be read as. The realization Frame carries prim:EncodingLoss: UTF-8 surrogate/normalization edge cases plus the load-bearing fact that a QName's expansion to its full IRI depends on an out-of-band prefix-to-namespace map that is NOT present in the octet run, so the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract QName only under a stated prefix-binding and Unicode normalization discipline." .

# ---- (b) Realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the real typed effect ---------------
prim:realize_QName_Utf8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "QName ⟼ UTF-8 compact prefix:local name" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:QName ;
    prim:encoding prim:QNameUtf8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:QNameUtf8 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The QName realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the realization monad gluing the formal leaf prim:QName (yin, formal.ttl) to the concrete prim:QNameUtf8 encoding (yang). As the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) it declares prim:realizesFrom prim:QName (its source formal type), prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput prim:QNameUtf8 (the Frame output X, a genuine byte-descending PhysicalEncoding that reaches the prim:Bit floor via Octet → Nibble → Bit), and prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss (the typed cost Y that makes the map monadic rather than a bare total function). QName is the compact-IRI leaf of the identifier tower QName ⊂ IRI ⊂ URI ⊂ URN; that chain is taxonomic subtyping only, NOT the grounding mechanism. QName is grounded by THIS, its OWN direct realization (spec §9a), whose target encoding prim:QNameUtf8 interprets reflexively back to QName itself rather than to URN. The effect is prim:EncodingLoss rather than prim:NoEffect for two honest reasons: the prefix:local compact form is not self-contained — its byte run carries the lexical characters but not the prefix-to-namespace bindings needed to expand it to a full IRI — and UTF-8 surrogate/normalization variance applies to the underlying codepoints, so the round-trip is faithful only under a stated prefix map and normalization form. No silm:isProvisional dodge is used: QName is a resolvable concrete type with its own direct realization." .

prim:Quad a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific physical encoding — yang. A composite/product carrier over the
#     reusable prim:CompositeCarrier kind of physical.ttl. interpretAs prim:Quad (reflexive).
# =====================================================================================

prim:QuadProductSpog a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(subject, predicate, object, graph term encodings) → Quad" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:alignment 1 ; prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Quad ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-quad carrier: the categorical PRODUCT Product(subject, predicate, object, graph) that lays the four navigable term coordinates of one quad contiguously over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier kind of physical.ttl. SUBTYPING RATIONALE — a Quad is a Triple EXTENDED with a fourth graph-name coordinate: adding a coordinate makes a WIDER product, not a narrower subtype, so prim:Quad is a direct child of prim:FormalType and NOT rdfs:subClassOf prim:Triple (asserting Quad ⊂ Triple would be a false arrow the honesty rule forbids); the physical witness of that width is precisely the fourth member below. PHYSICAL / SUBATOMIC GROUNDING — the product has exactly four member term encodings, each already authored in physical.ttl: the subject is an identifier realized through prim:UrnUtf8 (or a graph-local prim:BlankNodeLabel); the predicate is always an identifier through prim:UrnUtf8; the object is prim:LiteralUtf8 for a literal term or prim:UrnUtf8 / prim:BlankNodeLabel for a resource term; and the graph coordinate is itself an Identifier-tower value through prim:UrnUtf8 (or prim:BlankNodeLabel for a blank-node graph). Because those members are variable-length interpreted UTF-8 octet runs, the product is OCTET-GRANULAR rather than fixed-scalar: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence octet granule and prim:byteCount 128 records the occurrence-count ceiling of the SECOND width cap (spec §9c — the ByteVector/serialized cap of ≤128 byte occurrences, up to 1024 bits), so both caps hold (bitWidth 8 ≤ 128, byteCount 128 ≤ 128). It is prim:EndianNeutral because each constituent is a single-octet-granular UTF-8 run with no cross-octet ordering, and prim:alignment 1 / prim:padding 0 because the members are packed with no filler. The carrier byte-descends through prim:CompositeCarrier → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit (the prim:byteDescendsTo ladder in physical.ttl), so no coordinate of the quad escapes the width-1 Bit floor. EFFECT — the framing concatenation is faithful, but the member identifier and literal encodings carry prim:EncodingLoss (percent-encoding/normalization of URN octets, lexical canonicalization of the literal, and graph-local blank-node relabelling on merge), so the aggregate quad's byte round-trip is not the identity without a stated normalization/blank-node-canonicalization discipline; that inherited loss is the honest typed cost. COLIMIT — prim:interpretAs prim:Quad closes the mutual round-trip reflexively onto the very type it realizes, so yang holds yin at the Quad level (not at Triple, not at a bare octet run)." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — the gluing Kleisli/Frame arrow. realizesFrom prim:Quad,
#     encoding/frameOutput the encoding above, frameEffect the real typed effect.
# =====================================================================================

prim:Quad prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Quad_ProductSpog .

prim:realize_Quad_ProductSpog a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Quad ⟼ Product(subject, predicate, object, graph)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Quad ;
    prim:encoding prim:QuadProductSpog ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:QuadProductSpog ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ for prim:Quad — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and the gluing morphism of this fragment's colimit taiji, carrying the formal RDF quad (yin, formal.ttl) into its product physical carrier (yang, prim:QuadProductSpog above). It IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): its prim:realizesFrom is the source formal type prim:Quad, its prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput are the single output coordinate X (the product encoding), and its prim:frameEffect is the one error-or-effect coordinate Y. EFFECT RATIONALE — Y is prim:EncodingLoss, NOT prim:NoEffect: although concatenating the four member coordinates is itself faithful framing, a quad is built from identifier and literal terms whose UTF-8 byte round-trip is not the identity without a normalization discipline (percent-encoding/normalization of the subject/predicate/graph URNs, lexical canonicalization of a literal object such as '1.0' vs '1.00') and whose blank-node labels must be freshened on graph merge, so the aggregate realization honestly inherits that surrogate/normalization/relabelling loss. POLYMORPHISM — this is one of prim:Quad's realizesAs arrows; its sibling prim:realize_Quad_NQuads (realization.ttl) instead flattens the quad into a single N-Quads UTF-8 octet line. That fan-out (one formal type, many encodings, each with its own effect) is exactly the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; this product arrow is the one the fragment's Primitive commits to because it exposes the four member coordinates subatomically. BYTE-DESCENT — composing this arrow with the member and carrier realizations Kleisli-descends the whole quad through prim:CompositeCarrier → prim:Octet → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, so the gluing bottoms out at the byte substrate with no exemption." .

prim:Quantity a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: Product(Float64 magnitude, UINT16 unit index) → Quantity ---
# A categorical PRODUCT carrier, NOT a scalar and NOT a homogeneous ByteVector: two heterogeneous
# members — a Float64 magnitude first, then a UINT16 unit-of-measure index — laid contiguously. It
# reuses the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl and two machine-type
# kinds: the IEEE-754 float ladder (the magnitude member IS a prim:Float64: bitWidth 64, IEEE-754
# sign/exponent/mantissa, LE) and the unsigned-integer ladder (the unit member IS a prim:Uint16:
# bitWidth 16, unsigned, LE). Total bitWidth 80, ceilinged by the scalar cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128
# (spec §9c); it declares NO prim:byteCount (that is the variable-length ByteVector-family cap,
# which this fixed-arity product is not). prim:alignment 8 packs the wider Float64 member on its
# natural 8-byte boundary; prim:padding 0 records that the two members abut with no filler. It
# byteDescendsTo prim:Octet and thence to the prim:Bit floor. prim:interpretAs prim:Quantity makes
# the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:QuantityProductEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(Float64 magnitude, UINT16 unit index) · 10B · LE → Quantity [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 80 ;
    prim:byteWidth 10 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:alignment 8 ;
    prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Quantity ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of prim:Quantity: a categorical PRODUCT of two heterogeneous member encodings — an IEEE-754 Float64 magnitude first, then a UINT16 unit-of-measure registry index — laid contiguously little-endian (least-significant octet first) over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl, for a total prim:bitWidth 80 and prim:byteWidth 10, aligned to the wider member's 8-byte boundary (prim:alignment 8) with no filler (prim:padding 0). MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law — the members are themselves floor primitives, turtles all the way down): the magnitude member IS a prim:Float64 encoding from the IEEE-754 float machine-type ladder — prim:bitWidth 64 partitioned as 1 prim:IEEE754Sign bit + 11 prim:IEEE754Exponent bits + 52 prim:IEEE754Mantissa bits, prim:byteOrder prim:LE — occupying 8 whole prim:Octet occurrences and itself interpreting to prim:Real (the Number-tower magnitude of the Product(Real, UnitOfMeasure)); the unit member IS a prim:Uint16 encoding from the unsigned machine-type ladder — prim:bitWidth 16, prim:signedness prim:Unsigned, prim:byteOrder prim:LE — occupying 2 whole prim:Octet occurrences and itself interpreting to a prim:UnitOfMeasure registry index (metre, second, kilogram, or a derived unit such as newton, each carrying a conversion factor and offset to the SI base of its prim:Dimension). The fixed arity two is part of the encoding's identity and carries no length prefix, which is exactly why a Quantity is a prim:CompositeCarrier PRODUCT and NOT a homogeneous variable-length prim:ByteVector counted by prim:byteCount. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the product carrier prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each of the 10 constituent octets (8 in the magnitude, 2 in the unit index) descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of both members bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (the byte-stream carrier closure) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Quantity is a direct subclass of prim:FormalType and the parent of prim:Measurement (a Measurement is a Quantity that additionally records an uncertainty — its carrier widens the magnitude member into a value·uncertainty pair); a bare Number without a unit is only the magnitude member alone, and pairing it with the unit index is precisely what turns a dimensionless value into a dimensioned quantity whose comparability is governed by its prim:Dimension. TYPED EFFECT: the Float64 magnitude member maps the continuum ℝ onto a finite grid of representable doubles, so the realization rounds — the honest prim:RoundingIEEE754 cost (the exact UINT16 unit-index member is lossless and adds none). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Quantity closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Quantity itself (spec §9d), not onto a shared proxy carrier such as a bare Float64→Real or a bare Uint16→Natural, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Quantity to the encoding --------------
# Quantity ⟼ Product(Float64 magnitude, UINT16 unit index) as a Frame: realizesFrom Quantity,
# encoding/frameOutput the product encoding above, frameEffect the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754
# (the real typed cost of the Float64 magnitude member; the exact unit index adds none). A total,
# well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Quantity is a concrete composite type
# directly grounded on the product-of-(magnitude, unit) carrier.
prim:Quantity prim:realizesAs prim:QuantityRealizesProduct .

prim:QuantityRealizesProduct a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Quantity ⟼ Product(Float64 magnitude, UINT16 unit index) · 10B · LE; frameEffect IEEE754-rounding" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Quantity ;
    prim:encoding prim:QuantityProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:QuantityProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Quantity — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Quantity (yin, formal.ttl — a magnitude paired with a unit of measure, the Product(Real, UnitOfMeasure) that parents prim:Measurement) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:QuantityProductEncoding (yang X — the magnitude·unit product of a Float64 member and a UINT16 unit-index member), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier to prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Quantity realizesAs this canonical (Float64 magnitude, UINT16 unit) arrow; a wider Float128 or a base-10 Decimal magnitude, or a wider UINT32/URN-addressed unit coordinate, are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same quantity realization, and a unit conversion that rescales the magnitude into a commensurable unit of the same prim:Dimension is a different arrow again — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the (Float64, UINT16) product as Quantity's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 — the Float64 magnitude member maps the real continuum ℝ onto a finite binary grid, so all but exactly-representable magnitudes round to the nearest representable double; the UINT16 unit index is an exact, lossless registry label that contributes no additional effect. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Quantity is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type (a magnitude·unit pair), not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:RDFGraph a prim:FormalType .

# ---- The typed effect this fragment's arrow carries, typed for standalone self-containment.
#      Idempotent with realization.ttl's canonical declaration of the same individual; the
#      full prose rationale for prim:EncodingLoss lives on that canonical node in realization.ttl.
prim:EncodingLoss a prim:Effect .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific PhysicalEncoding — yang facet, TRUE-IDENTITY back-pointer.
# =====================================================================================

prim:RDFGraphTurtleEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Turtle / N-Triples UTF-8 byte stream → RDFGraph" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:RDFGraph ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-graph physical carrier: the byte stream a set of Triples serializes into through a concrete RDF syntax — canonically a Turtle document, equivalently an order-independent run of N-Triples lines. RDFGraph is the composite top of the RDF-term tower (formal.ttl: RDFGraph rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType, built by set-composition over prim:Triple, the same shape as the Aggregate-tower prim:Set is over its element type), so this encoding is a COMPOSITE stream, not a fixed-width scalar: it is octet-granular, with prim:bitWidth 8 recording the per-occurrence granule (one prim:Octet of the code space, cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255) and prim:byteCount 128 the occurrence-count ceiling of the ByteVector-family second width cap (spec §9c; the scalar prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 cap does not bind a variable-length stream). prim:byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering — endianness is inapplicable, exactly as it is for prim:StringUtf8 / prim:TripleNTriples — and prim:signedness is deliberately omitted: a serialized graph stream is not an integer encoding, so a sign facet would be a category error rather than a coordinate. Subatomic byte descent: the document rides the physical carrier ladder prim:Stream → prim:Block → prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit (the prim:byteDescendsTo chain in physical.ttl), transitively bottoming at the width-1 prim:Bit floor so no graph escapes the byte substrate. Member encodings (the composite's constituents): each element prim:Triple realizes as one UTF-8 line whose per-codepoint carrier is prim:Utf8 (Codepoint → 1..4 octets), i.e. the graph's bytes are the concatenation of its triples' N-Triples/Turtle-serialized UTF-8 octet runs plus the syntactic prefix, punctuation, and layout scaffolding of the chosen concrete syntax. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:RDFGraph closes the round-trip REFLEXIVELY onto the graph's own formal type (not onto prim:Triple, prim:Set, or prim:Codepoint), so physicalFacet → interpretAs is a true identity on prim:RDFGraph." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The Realization — the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing formal → physical, with effect.
# =====================================================================================

prim:RDFGraph prim:realizesAs prim:realize_RDFGraph_TurtleFrag .

prim:realize_RDFGraph_TurtleFrag a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: RDFGraph ⟼ Turtle/N-Triples UTF-8 document (encoding-loss)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:RDFGraph ;
    prim:encoding prim:RDFGraphTurtleEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:RDFGraphTurtleEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ for prim:RDFGraph — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, i.e. the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) instantiated at T = prim:RDFGraph. It is not a passive link but a genuine Frame: prim:realizesFrom names its source formal type (prim:RDFGraph, the composite top of the RDF-term tower built by set-composition over prim:Triple), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput name its target byte carrier X (prim:RDFGraphTurtleEncoding, the octet-granular Turtle/N-Triples UTF-8 stream descending prim:Stream → prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Bit), and prim:frameEffect names its typed cost Y. That effect is prim:EncodingLoss, the REAL and correct cost of graph serialization: the choice of concrete syntax (Turtle vs N-Triples vs others), prefix and blank-node relabelling, and statement/triple ordering mean the byte round-trip is NOT the identity on the abstract graph absent a canonicalization discipline (two byte documents can denote the same set of triples, and one graph admits many byte serializations). This is why RDFGraph realizes monadically rather than as a bare total function — serialization is lossy on presentation even while it preserves the triple set. It is one of prim:RDFGraph's several realizesAs arrows (fan-out = the monad's polymorphism); it is NOT the monad unit η, so it carries no prim:isMonadUnit flag. Composing this arrow with the byte-descent of its carrier ladder is Kleisli composition down to the prim:Bit floor." .

prim:Rational a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: Product(INT32 numerator, INT32 denominator) → ℚ ---
# A categorical PRODUCT carrier, NOT a scalar and NOT a homogeneous ByteVector: two heterogeneous-
# ROLE Integer members (numerator, then non-zero denominator) laid contiguously. It reuses the
# reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier and the two's-complement machine-type ladder of
# physical.ttl — each member IS a prim:Int32 encoding (bitWidth 32, signed, two's-complement, LE),
# so the pair is a fixed-width scalar-family product of total bitWidth 64, ceilinged by the scalar
# cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 (spec §9c); it declares NO prim:byteCount (that is the variable-length
# ByteVector-family cap, which this fixed-arity product is not). prim:alignment 4 packs each INT32
# member on its natural 4-byte boundary; prim:padding 0 records that the two equal-width members
# abut with no filler. prim:interpretAs prim:Rational makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:RationalProductEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Product(INT32 numerator, INT32 denominator) · 8B · LE · two's-complement → Rational [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ;
    prim:byteWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ;
    prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:alignment 4 ;
    prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Rational ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the rationals ℚ: a categorical PRODUCT of two two's-complement signed INT32 member encodings — the numerator first, then the non-zero denominator — laid contiguously little-endian (least-significant octet first) over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl, for a total prim:bitWidth 64 and prim:byteWidth 8, aligned to the 4-byte INT32 boundary (prim:alignment 4) with no filler (prim:padding 0). MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): each of the two members IS a prim:Int32 encoding from the INT8/INT16/INT32/INT64 machine-type ladder — prim:bitWidth 32, prim:signedness prim:Signed, prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement, prim:byteOrder prim:LE — occupying 4 whole prim:Octet occurrences; the fixed arity two is part of the encoding's identity and carries no length prefix, which is exactly why a product is a prim:CompositeCarrier and NOT a homogeneous variable-length prim:ByteVector counted by prim:byteCount. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the product carrier prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each of the 8 constituent octets (4 per member) descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of both members bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (the byte-stream carrier closure) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: this exact-pair carrier holds ℚ's value LOSSLESSLY as p/q, unlike a fixed-width float realization of ℚ which would round; it sits between the scalar carriers of prim:Integer ⊂ Rational (the pair n and 1 collapses to the numerator alone) and prim:Real ⊃ Rational (which realizes onto IEEE-754 floats instead). TYPED EFFECT: each INT32 member represents only a finite window of the unbounded integer line, so a numerator or denominator outside the INT32 range overflows — the honest, width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain cost the arbitrary-precision escape (a Bignum numerator/denominator over a prim:ByteVector) would trade for prim:MayOverflow instead. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Rational closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto ℚ itself (spec §9d), not onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Rational to the encoding --------------
# ℚ ⟼ Product(INT32,INT32) as a Frame: realizesFrom Rational, encoding/frameOutput the product
# encoding above, frameEffect the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain (the real typed cost of the
# fixed-width members). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Rational
# is a concrete composite type directly grounded on the product-of-integers carrier.
prim:Rational prim:realizesAs prim:RationalRealizesProduct .

prim:RationalRealizesProduct a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: ℚ ⟼ Product(INT32 numerator, INT32 denominator) · 8B · LE; frameEffect overflow-domain" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Rational ;
    prim:encoding prim:RationalProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:RationalProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Rational — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Rational (yin, formal.ttl, Integer ⊂ Rational ⊂ Real) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:RationalProductEncoding (yang X — the numerator·denominator product of two INT32 members), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier to prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Rational realizesAs this canonical exact-pair arrow; wider member widths (INT64 numerator/denominator) or an arbitrary-precision Bignum-pair form are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same rational realization, and a lossy Real→float coercion of a rational is a different arrow on a different carrier entirely — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the INT32-pair form as ℚ's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain — each fixed 32-bit member holds only a finite window of the unbounded integer line, so a numerator or denominator outside that window wraps, saturates, or traps; the bound is exact and known per member width, the honest cost of a bounded carrier that keeps ℚ's value EXACT within range (never rounding it the way a float realization would). This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Rational is a concrete, directly-realizable composite number type, not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:Real a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: IEEE-754 double → Real -----------------
# Scalar fixed-width machine type; governed by the scalar width cap prim:bitWidth ≤ 128
# (spec §9c), NOT the ByteVector byteCount cap — so no prim:byteCount is declared (this is a
# fixed-width scalar, not a variable-length ByteVector-family carrier). No prim:signedness is
# declared either: prim:signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/
# Natural), which does not apply to a float; the ± sign of an IEEE-754 value is carried instead by
# the prim:IEEE754Sign field below. It references the physical.ttl machine-type kinds
# (prim:IEEE754Sign/Exponent/Mantissa), the byte-order individual prim:LE, and descends through the
# physical.ttl carrier ladder to the prim:Bit floor.
prim:RealFloat64Encoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "IEEE-754 double (Float64 · 8B · LE) → Real [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:ieee754 true ;
    prim:ieee754Field prim:IEEE754Sign , prim:IEEE754Exponent , prim:IEEE754Mantissa ;
    prim:exponentBits 11 ; prim:mantissaBits 52 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Real ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of prim:Real: an IEEE-754 binary64 (double-precision) encoding, 64 bits laid out as one prim:IEEE754Sign bit + an 11-bit prim:IEEE754Exponent field (dynamic range) + a 52-bit prim:IEEE754Mantissa significand field (precision), so 1+11+52 = 64 = prim:bitWidth. SUBTYPING: Real ⊂ Complex (a Real is a Complex with zero imaginary part) and Real ⊃ Rational ⊃ Integer ⊃ Natural, so this same IEEE-754 realization mechanism is the number tower's real-axis carrier. PHYSICAL/SUBATOMIC DESCENT: the 64 bits occupy exactly 8 whole prim:Octet occurrences serialized little-endian (prim:LE — least-significant octet first); each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so via the physical.ttl carrier ladder every bit of this encoding bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it. It is a fixed-width SCALAR encoding, ceilinged by the scalar cap prim:bitWidth 64 ≤ 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately no prim:byteCount (that is the ByteVector-family cap) and no prim:signedness (an integer facet; the ± sign is the prim:IEEE754Sign field, not the integer Signed/Unsigned facet). EFFECT: mapping the continuum ℝ onto this finite grid of representable doubles is lossy, so its realization Frame carries prim:RoundingIEEE754. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Real closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Real itself (not onto a shared proxy carrier), making the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet a TRUE IDENTITY." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Real to the encoding ------
prim:RealRealizesFloat64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: ℝ ⟼ IEEE-754 double (Float64 · 8B · LE); frameEffect IEEE754-rounding" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Real ;
    prim:encoding prim:RealFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:RealFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Real — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It is not a passive link but IS a Frame: prim:realizesFrom prim:Real (its source, yin), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:RealFloat64Encoding (its target X, yang — the IEEE-754 double carrier), and prim:frameEffect prim:RoundingIEEE754 (its Y — the honest typed cost of forcing the continuum ℝ onto a finite binary grid). MONAD POLYMORPHISM: prim:Real realizesAs many arrows (Float64 here, Float32 adding prim:NarrowingLoss, base-10 Decimal carrying prim:Base10Precision); this fragment commits to the Float64 arrow as Real's canonical, tightest, non-narrowing grounding. Because the effect is a genuine non-neutral member (not prim:NoEffect), it records that realizing a real number is never free — exactly why realization is monadic rather than a bare total function. Composing this arrow further byte-descends through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor via the physical carrier ladder; its output X is a real byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding, never a bare formal class, so there is no byte-stream-closure exemption." .

prim:Set a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: canonically sorted distinct run → Set --------------
# An UNORDERED, DUPLICATE-FREE, VARIABLE-length carrier, NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous product and
# NOT an order-preserving sequence: a UINT32 cardinality prefix followed by exactly that many
# contiguous DISTINCT element encodings, all of the SAME element type, laid in a CANONICAL SORT order
# chosen only for byte-level determinism (not for identity — a Set is unordered). It reuses the
# reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl (which byteDescendsTo
# prim:Octet), whose schema-qualified Empty|Link algebra physically holds the distinct members while
# the unordered SET semantics live in the formal type. Because a Set's cardinality is not pinned, the
# carrier is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet),
# prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count
# ceiling (≤ 128, the ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits), since the
# total span is the cardinality prefix plus the sum of the distinct member widths and varies with the
# element set. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 cardinality prefix is a little-endian
# unsigned integer (least-significant octet first). NO prim:signedness is declared: signedness is the
# integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) and does not apply to a set frame
# (as prim:List and prim:Tuple likewise omit it). prim:interpretAs prim:Set makes the colimit
# round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:SetSortedEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "canonically sorted length-prefixed distinct run (UINT32 cardinality + sorted distinct element encodings) · octet-granular · LE → Set [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Set ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the set: a CANONICALLY SORTED, LENGTH-PREFIXED, DISTINCT run — a UINT32 cardinality prefix (the machine type prim:Uint32 of physical.ttl, little-endian) followed by exactly that many contiguous element encodings, ALL of one element type, each element appearing AT MOST ONCE and the whole run laid in a canonical sort order chosen only for byte-level determinism — laid over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl. The unary law's UNORDERED SET family has its own separately identified alternatives and NEVER supplies sequence order by convention, so the physical run is deliberately given a canonical sort purely to make the bytes deterministic; the abstract Set itself remains orderless. Because a set's cardinality is not pinned, the carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR — prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet of the code space, cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits; the scalar prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 cap does not bind a variable-length run) — since a set's total span is the cardinality prefix PLUS the sum of its distinct member widths and so varies with the element set. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because the UINT32 cardinality prefix serializes least-significant octet first; NO prim:signedness is declared because signedness is an integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) inapplicable to a set frame, exactly as prim:List and prim:Tuple omit it. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): every element IS itself a floor prim:PhysicalEncoding of the ONE element type — a set of prim:Codepoint lays prim:Utf8 octet runs, a set of prim:Integer lays prim:Int32 two's-complement words, a set of prim:Triple (the shape of an prim:RDFGraph) lays prim:TripleNTriples lines — and the set's bytes are exactly the cardinality prefix followed by those homogeneous DISTINCT member byte runs abutted in canonical sort order; because the element type is uniform, the members are distinct, and the cardinality is explicit, there is NO per-element tag and NO terminator, which is precisely why a set is a homogeneous prim:ByteVector-family run and NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:CompositeCarrier product (mixed member widths, arity without a length prefix). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ordered-vector carrier prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of the cardinality prefix and of every member bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Set is the direct subclass of prim:Bag (a Set is a Bag whose every element multiplicity is at most one, so it inherits the unordered-collection shape and adds the distinctness invariant), the shape behind an prim:RDFGraph (a set of prim:Triple), and is kept distinct from the ordered prim:List / prim:Vector (order and positional duplicates ARE identity), the fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:Tuple (no length prefix, mixed types), and its own supertype prim:Bag (per-element multiplicities stored, not capped at one). TYPED EFFECT: because the abstract Set has NO order, imposing the canonical serialization order is a real, typed transformation — two presentations differing only in input element order map to the SAME bytes, so the realization carries prim:OrderingCanonicalization (order information is deliberately discarded); it is NOT prim:NoEffect precisely because a genuine transformation (canonical sorting + de-duplication normalization) occurs, and NOT prim:EncodingLoss because no MEMBERSHIP is lost — only the meaningless input ordering is normalized away. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Set closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the set type itself (spec §9d), not onto its supertype prim:Bag nor a bare prim:ByteVector, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Set to the encoding ------------------
# Set ⟼ canonically sorted length-prefixed distinct run as a Frame: realizesFrom Set,
# encoding/frameOutput the sorted encoding above, frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization (a Set
# has no intrinsic order, so serializing it imposes a canonical sort — a real transform that maps
# differently-ordered presentations to identical bytes while preserving membership exactly). A total,
# well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Set is a concrete, directly-realizable
# unordered collection grounded on the sorted-distinct-run-over-ByteVector carrier. This ADDS a
# second realizesAs edge alongside the pre-existing prim:realize_Set_Sorted (monad polymorphism).
prim:Set prim:realizesAs prim:SetRealizesSorted .

prim:SetRealizesSorted a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Set ⟼ canonically sorted length-prefixed distinct run · octet-granular · LE; frameEffect ordering-canonicalization" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Set ;
    prim:encoding prim:SetSortedEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:SetSortedEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Set — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Set (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Bag in the Aggregate tower — a Set is a Bag whose every multiplicity is at most one — and the shape behind an prim:RDFGraph) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:SetSortedEncoding (yang X — the canonically sorted, length-prefixed, DISTINCT run of the set's member encodings over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Set realizesAs this canonical sorted-run arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_Set_Sorted (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a hash-set layout, a bitset over a small universe, or a Bloom-filter approximation are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same set realization, and this fragment commits to the canonically-sorted distinct length-prefixed run as the general deterministic canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:OrderingCanonicalization — because a Set has NO intrinsic element order, realizing it into a necessarily ordered byte serialization must impose a canonical sort, so two presentations of the same set that differ only in input element order map to the SAME bytes; the mapping is therefore NOT the identity on presentation order (that meaningless ordering is deliberately discarded), even though the set's MEMBERSHIP is preserved exactly. This is in deliberate contrast to prim:List, whose realization carries prim:NoEffect because a list's order and positional duplicates ARE its identity and are laid down verbatim; and it is distinct from prim:EncodingLoss because no membership is lost — only the input ordering is normalized away. Any rounding, overflow, or encoding loss belongs to the individual member encodings (an prim:Int32 member's prim:OverflowDomain, a prim:Utf8 codepoint member's prim:EncodingLoss), not to the set framing itself. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Set is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type — an unordered duplicate-free collection — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy." .

prim:StringUtf8ByteVector a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet ByteVector (whole sequence) → String" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:String ;
    rdfs:comment "The String carrier: a finite ordered UTF-8 octet stream — the WHOLE sequence-of-Codepoint serialized into a prim:ByteVector of up to 128 byte occurrences (the carrier-ladder rung above prim:Octet). It is octet-granular — prim:bitWidth 8 is the per-occurrence granule of exactly one prim:Octet and prim:byteCount 128 is the ByteVector occurrence-count ceiling of spec §9c's second width cap (up to 1024 bits) — and prim:EndianNeutral because a UTF-8 code unit is a single octet with no cross-octet order to fix. prim:signedness is deliberately OMITTED: a character stream carries no numeric sign, and force-fitting one onto text would be a false facet (Präriehund honesty — name the absence, do not fabricate a coordinate). Subatomically each element is itself a prim:Codepoint realized through UTF-8 as a 1..4-octet run (the member encoding prim:Utf8), and the String is their in-order concatenation; those octets byte-descend prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 eigenvalue floor, so no part of the string escapes the byte substrate. prim:interpretAs prim:String makes the mutual-colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY at the sequence level: the bytes read back as the whole String, not as a single prim:Codepoint (which is why this is a distinct carrier from prim:Utf8). Its realization Frame carries the surrogate/normalization prim:EncodingLoss." .

# ---- (b) The Realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow with the REAL typed effect --------
prim:String prim:realizesAs prim:realize_String_Utf8ByteVector .

prim:realize_String_Utf8ByteVector a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "String ⟼ UTF-8 octet ByteVector (encoding-loss: surrogate/normalization)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:String ;
    prim:encoding prim:StringUtf8ByteVector ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:StringUtf8ByteVector ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The String realization ρ — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad gluing the formal prim:String (yin, sequence-of-Codepoint in the Text tower of formal.ttl) to the physical prim:StringUtf8ByteVector (yang, the UTF-8 octet ByteVector). It IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): prim:realizesFrom prim:String is the source, prim:frameOutput / prim:encoding prim:StringUtf8ByteVector is the output coordinate X (a genuine byte-descending PhysicalEncoding, never a bare formal class), and prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss is the error/effect coordinate Y. The typed cost is real and not decorative: mapping an abstract Unicode-scalar sequence through UTF-8 admits surrogate handling and Unicode normalization-form (NFC/NFD/NFKC/NFKD) differences, so the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract String only under a stated normalization discipline — exactly the hint's encoding-loss(normalization). prim:String prim:realizesAs this arrow supplies the dual-grounding forward edge the SHACL FormalTypeShape and the litmus walk require. String is a concrete tower type directly realized here, so there is no transitive grounding and no silm:isProvisional dodge (Directive; spec §9b)." .

prim:Sum a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) type-specific physical carrier (yang) — the coproduct byte encoding -----------------
prim:SumTaggedUnionEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "Sum coproduct: UINT8 discriminant tag + selected alternative payload → Sum" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Sum ;
    rdfs:comment "The type-specific physical carrier for prim:Sum — the coproduct / tagged-union encoding: a leading discriminant tag realized as one prim:Uint8 machine type (an eight-bit UINT8 whose own prim:interpretAs is prim:Natural, giving 256 selectable alternatives) followed immediately by the byte encoding of whichever single alternative the tag selects. It is octet-granular and ByteVector-family, so it is ceilinged by the SECOND width cap (spec §9c): prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence octet granule, prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet unit, and prim:byteCount 128 the occurrence-count ceiling of a prim:ByteVector (up to 1024 bits), NOT the fixed-scalar prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 cap. Byte descent (subatomic grounding): the whole serialized sum is an ordered run of prim:Octet occurrences laid into a prim:ByteVector carrier — the first prim:Octet the tag, the remaining prim:Octet occurrences the selected alternative's payload encoding — and every prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble then transitively to the width-1 prim:Bit floor (prim:Byte likewise prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit), so no coproduct escapes the bit substrate. Member encodings: the tag member is exactly prim:Uint8; the payload member is polymorphic, being the encoding of the active alternative, so the sum descends member-wise through each alternative's own carrier. Subtyping rationale: Sum is the categorical dual of the prim:Tuple product and the supertype of prim:Optional (the two-alternative none|some union); this carrier is the shape behind the prim:ByteVector Empty|Link constructor alternatives and the admission Accepted|Rejected state of the unary law. prim:interpretAs prim:Sum closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY — the round-trip physical → interpretAs → formal lands back on Sum itself, a true identity (spec §9d), not on a proxy formal type. Effect rationale: the tag-width bound (a UINT8 discriminates at most 256 alternatives) is a recorded facet, not a loss — a sum exceeding 256 alternatives simply widens its tag — and within the bound the tag-plus-payload framing names exactly one alternative and copies that alternative's payload bytes verbatim, a lossless bijection, so its realization carries prim:NoEffect." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ (Kleisli/Frame arrow) — the gluing morphism -----------------------
#      Forward fan-out edge: Sum realizesAs this arrow. When merged with realization.ttl (which
#      already binds prim:Sum prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Sum_TagPayload) Sum carries two realizesAs
#      edges — honest monad polymorphism, one FormalType realizing as many encodings, not a conflict.
prim:Sum prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Sum_taggedUnion .

prim:realize_Sum_taggedUnion a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Sum ⟼ UINT8 tag + selected payload (lossless framing)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Sum ;
    prim:encoding prim:SumTaggedUnionEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:SumTaggedUnionEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ that glues prim:Sum (yin, formal.ttl) to its physical carrier prim:SumTaggedUnionEncoding (yang) — the unary law's T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) instantiated for the coproduct, so this realization IS a Frame, not a passive link. Its prim:realizesFrom is prim:Sum (the source formal type, the tagged-union / categorical-dual-of-Tuple constructor); its prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput are BOTH prim:SumTaggedUnionEncoding — the Frame output coordinate X is a genuine byte-descending PhysicalEncoding (a UINT8 discriminant tag over a prim:Octet plus the selected alternative's payload, laid into a prim:ByteVector that bottoms at the prim:Bit floor), never a bare formal class. Its prim:frameEffect is prim:NoEffect (the Frame error-or-effect coordinate Y): a faithful tag-plus-payload framing is a lossless bijection — it names exactly one alternative via the discriminant and copies that alternative's payload octets unchanged, introducing no rounding, narrowing, overflow, or encoding loss — so the neutral effect is the honest, real typed cost (the 256-alternative UINT8-tag ceiling being a recorded facet, not an effect). prim:Sum prim:realizesAs this arrow; it is one realization of a polymorphic constructor (wider-tag variants are admissible siblings), and because the encoding's prim:interpretAs closes back onto prim:Sum this arrow is the gluing morphism of a FULLY-SEALED taiji rather than a proxy — hence no silm:isProvisional: Sum is a resolvable in-scope type, not a Präriehund gap." .

prim:Tensor a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: shape header + row-major dense block → Tensor ------
# A two-part octet-granular byte block over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier:
# a SHAPE HEADER (a UINT32 rank n, then n UINT32 per-axis extents e0..e(n-1)) followed by a dense
# ROW-MAJOR (C-contiguous) payload of the numeric components. Strides are DERIVED from the shape in
# row-major order (the last axis varies fastest), not stored, so the descriptor is exactly rank+extents.
# It is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1
# the whole-octet granule, prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (≤ 128, the
# ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits), since the total span is the header
# plus the product-of-extents × element-width and varies with shape and dtype. prim:byteOrder prim:LE
# because the UINT32 header words and the multi-octet element components serialize least-significant
# octet first. NO prim:signedness is declared on the frame: signedness is a per-COMPONENT facet of the
# element machine-type encoding (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural), not of the tensor container, just
# as prim:List and prim:Tuple omit it. prim:interpretAs prim:Tensor makes the colimit round-trip a
# TRUE IDENTITY — the shape/stride metadata is exactly what distinguishes a rank-n Tensor from a flat
# prim:Vector or prim:List, so the carrier interprets back to Tensor, never to its element type.
prim:TensorShapedBlockEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "shape header (UINT32 rank + per-axis extents) + row-major dense component block · octet-granular · LE → Tensor [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Tensor ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the tensor and the AOB tensor block's LOAD-BEARING byte home: a two-part octet-granular byte block over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl — a SHAPE HEADER (a UINT32 rank n, the machine type prim:Uint32, followed by n UINT32 per-axis extents e0..e(n-1)) then a dense ROW-MAJOR (C-contiguous) payload of the (e0·e1·…·e(n-1)) numeric components. STRIDES are DERIVED from the shape in row-major order (the last axis varies fastest: stride(k) = product of extents after axis k), not serialized, so the descriptor is exactly rank + extents and the layout is fully reconstructible from the header. Because a tensor's total span depends on its shape and element dtype, the carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR — prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits) — since the span is the header plus (product-of-extents × element-width) and varies with rank, shape and dtype. prim:byteOrder prim:LE because both the UINT32 header words and the multi-octet element components serialize least-significant octet first; NO prim:signedness is declared on the FRAME because signedness is a per-COMPONENT integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) of the element machine-type encoding, inapplicable to the tensor container, exactly as prim:List and prim:Tuple omit it. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): the header extents ARE prim:Uint32 encodings, and every payload component IS itself a floor prim:PhysicalEncoding of the tensor's ONE element dtype — a real tensor lays prim:Float32 or prim:Float64 IEEE-754 words, an integer tensor lays prim:Int8/prim:Int16/prim:Int32/prim:Int64 two's-complement words or their prim:Uint* unsigned counterparts — and the tensor's bytes are exactly the shape header followed by those homogeneous component byte runs abutted in row-major position order; because the dtype is uniform and the extents are explicit, there is NO per-component tag and NO terminator, which is precisely why a tensor is a shape-tagged homogeneous prim:ByteVector-family block and NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:CompositeCarrier product. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ordered-vector carrier prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of the rank word, of every extent, and of every component bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Tensor is a DIRECT subclass of prim:FormalType (NOT of prim:List or prim:Vector) because it carries the explicit shape-and-stride structure a flat sequence lacks — a matrix / surface / shape is a distinct higher-dimensional Lambda-vector family above the byte carrier (unary law), retaining its own rank and coordinate geometry rather than being an alias for a nested host array; a rank-1 Tensor is isomorphic to a prim:Vector but the two are kept distinct so the shape metadata stays first-class, and Tensor generalises Vector (rank 1) to arbitrary rank. TYPED EFFECT: a faithful shape descriptor plus a row-major dense block preserves rank, every axis extent, and every component in its exact position and order, so the tensor FRAMING is LOSSLESS and the realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect; any rounding, narrowing, or overflow belongs to the individual component encodings (a prim:Float64 component's prim:RoundingIEEE754, a narrowed prim:Float32 component's additional prim:NarrowingLoss, an prim:Int32 component's prim:OverflowDomain), never to the shape-plus-block framing that concatenates them. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Tensor closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the tensor type itself (spec §9d), not onto its rank-1 prim:Vector isomorph nor a bare prim:ByteVector, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Tensor to the encoding ---------------
# Tensor ⟼ shape header + row-major dense block as a Frame: realizesFrom Tensor, encoding/frameOutput
# the shaped-block encoding above, frameEffect prim:NoEffect (a faithful shape descriptor + row-major
# block is lossless framing). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Tensor
# is a concrete, directly-realizable n-dimensional array type grounded on the shape-header-plus-
# row-major-block-over-ByteVector carrier. This ADDS a second realizesAs edge alongside the pre-existing
# prim:realize_Tensor_RowMajor (monad polymorphism).
prim:Tensor prim:realizesAs prim:TensorRealizesShapedBlock .

prim:TensorRealizesShapedBlock a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Tensor ⟼ shape header + row-major dense block · octet-granular · LE; frameEffect none (lossless framing)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Tensor ;
    prim:encoding prim:TensorShapedBlockEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:TensorShapedBlockEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Tensor — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Tensor (yin, formal.ttl, a DIRECT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType in the Aggregate tower, the n-dimensional generalisation of the rank-1 prim:Vector carrying explicit shape and stride) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:TensorShapedBlockEncoding (yang X — the shape header plus dense row-major component block over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Tensor realizesAs this canonical shaped-block arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_Tensor_RowMajor (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a column-major (Fortran-contiguous) layout, a strided/non-contiguous view, a sparse (coordinate-list or compressed-row) representation, or a differing element dtype are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same tensor realization, and this fragment commits to the row-major dense count-explicit block as the general canonical facet the AOB tensor block binds against. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — a faithful shape descriptor plus a row-major dense block is LOSSLESS framing: the rank and per-axis extents delimit the payload and the homogeneous component byte runs are laid down verbatim in row-major position order, so the tensor framing preserves rank, shape and every component's value and position exactly and introduces no rounding, narrowing, overflow, encoding loss, or ordering canonicalization of its own (in deliberate contrast to prim:Set, whose realization DOES carry prim:OrderingCanonicalization because a set has no intrinsic order — a tensor's axis order IS part of its identity and is preserved, not normalized away); any such effect belongs to the individual component encodings (a prim:Float64 component's prim:RoundingIEEE754, a narrowed prim:Float32 component's prim:NarrowingLoss, an prim:Int32 component's prim:OverflowDomain), not to the shape-plus-block that concatenates them. Byte-descent of this arrow is the Kleisli composition Tensor → TensorShapedBlockEncoding → prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Bit down to the width-1 floor. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Tensor is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type — an n-dimensional numeric array with explicit shape — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy." .

prim:Time a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding (yang) — a fixed-width scalar INT64 carrier.
# =====================================================================================
# References the physical.ttl ladder + machine-type kinds: it is an INT64-shaped scalar
# whose 64 bits occupy exactly eight prim:Octet occurrences (one machine prim:Word), each
# Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, so the whole carrier bottoms out at the
# width-1 prim:Bit floor. interpretAs prim:Time makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE identity.
prim:TimeInt64Nanos a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "INT64 (nanoseconds since midnight, two's-complement, LE) → Time" ;
    prim:bitWidth 64 ; prim:byteWidth 8 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Signed ; prim:integerRepresentation prim:TwosComplement ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Time ;
    prim:uefiName "INT64" ;
    rdfs:comment "The time-of-day carrier for prim:Time: a signed two's-complement INT64 counting nanoseconds elapsed since midnight (00:00:00) within an unspecified calendar day, serialized little-endian across exactly eight prim:Octet occurrences (one machine prim:Word), each octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble and thence prim:Bit so the whole scalar bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor of the carrier ladder. Being a fixed-width SCALAR it takes the first arm of the two-cap model — prim:bitWidth 64, comfortably ≤ 128 — and declares NO prim:byteCount, since that second cap governs only variable-length ByteVector-family carriers, not a single machine word (spec §9c). Signedness is prim:Signed with prim:TwosComplement so a leap-second overrun past 24h and a negative pre-midnight adjustment are both representable; the 64-bit window (±9.22×10^18 ticks) vastly exceeds a day's 8.64×10^13 ns, so the honest typed cost is the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain plus whole-nanosecond quantisation, not lexical or surrogate encoding loss. A bare Time names no absolute instant — it must be paired with a Date and a timezone to do so — so this interprets back to prim:Time itself, NOT to prim:Instant and NOT to the bare prim:Integer a plain INT64 would, making physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet a reflexive identity onto prim:Time." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ (the gluing Kleisli/Frame arrow) — Time ⟼ INT64 nanos.
# =====================================================================================
prim:Time prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Time_Int64 .

prim:realize_Time_Int64 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "Time ⟼ INT64 nanoseconds-since-midnight (overflow-domain effect)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Time ;
    prim:encoding prim:TimeInt64Nanos ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:TimeInt64Nanos ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:OverflowDomain ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ for prim:Time — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). It prim:realizesFrom prim:Time into the physical encoding prim:TimeInt64Nanos and exposes that same INT64-nanoseconds-since-midnight carrier as both its prim:encoding and its Frame output coordinate X (prim:frameOutput), so the output descends through the octet ladder to the prim:Bit floor with no boundary exemption. Its Frame error|effect coordinate Y (prim:frameEffect) is prim:OverflowDomain: forcing the continuous daily time cycle onto a fixed signed 64-bit tick count both represents only a finite window of the integer line and quantises to whole nanoseconds, so sub-nanosecond fractions truncate and any out-of-window tick wraps or saturates — the honest, typed cost that makes this realization monadic rather than a free total function. This is precisely the gluing morphism selected by the prim:prim_Time colimit atom below; composing it with the monad unit η (prim:realize_unit) leaves it unchanged (left/right unit law), which is the checkable trace of the Frame monad on this fragment." .

prim:Triple a prim:FormalType .

prim:EncodingLoss a prim:Effect .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding (yang carrier) --------------------------
# The RDF-triple carrier: one N-Triples UTF-8 line serializing the ordered Product(Subject,
# Predicate, Object). Octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 is the per-occurrence octet granule and
# prim:byteCount 128 the ByteVector occurrence-count ceiling (spec §9c, the second/variable-length
# width cap — a serialized term line is variable length, not a fixed scalar). Endian-neutral,
# because UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering. prim:interpretAs points
# back to prim:Triple itself (the whole proposition), so the mutual colimit closes REFLEXIVELY on
# Triple — never on one of its S/P/O member coordinates, and never on prim:Codepoint. MEMBER
# ENCODINGS (the composite S-P-O grounding): the Subject coordinate realizes as a name or graph-
# local label (member encoding prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN, or prim:BlankNodeLabel → prim:BlankNode);
# the Predicate coordinate always realizes as a name (prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN); the Object
# coordinate realizes as a name, a graph-local label, or a typed/lang-tagged literal (prim:UrnUtf8
# → prim:URN, prim:BlankNodeLabel → prim:BlankNode, or prim:LiteralUtf8 → prim:Literal). Each
# member is itself a realized carrier that descends to the byte floor. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the
# underlying carrier is a prim:ByteVector (equivalently a prim:CompositeCarrier over the three
# member runs), which prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble
# prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit — bottoming at the width-1 Bit eigenvalue floor, so no octet of any
# S/P/O coordinate escapes the substrate (byte-stream carrier closure, unary law).
prim:TripleNTriplesProduct a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "N-Triples UTF-8 line of Product(Subject, Predicate, Object) → Triple [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:alignment 1 ;
    prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Triple ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical (yang) facet of prim:Triple: one N-Triples UTF-8 line serializing the ordered Product(Subject, Predicate, Object) — the atomic subject-predicate-object proposition of the RDF graph, a product of THREE independently typed coordinates, not a flat row. Octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence octet granule and prim:byteCount 128 the ByteVector occurrence-count ceiling of the variable-length width cap (spec §9c), because a serialized term line is variable length rather than a fixed-width scalar; prim:alignment 1 / prim:padding 0 mark that the octet run is tightly packed with no filler. Endian-neutral, since UTF-8 code units are single octets with no cross-octet ordering. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite S-P-O grounding, unary law — S/O/P are independently typed URN/term towers, never three interchangeable strings): the Subject realizes as a name or graph-local label (member encoding prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN, or prim:BlankNodeLabel → prim:BlankNode); the Predicate always realizes as a name (prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN); the Object realizes as a name, a graph-local label, or a typed/lang-tagged literal (prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN, prim:BlankNodeLabel → prim:BlankNode, or prim:LiteralUtf8 → prim:Literal). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the underlying prim:ByteVector (equivalently a prim:CompositeCarrier over the three member runs) prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, so every octet of every S/P/O coordinate bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor with nothing below it (byte-stream carrier closure). SUBTYPING RATIONALE: Triple is a direct subclass of prim:FormalType with no descendants — a prim:Quad is a WIDER product adding a graph coordinate (not a subtype), and a prim:RDFGraph is a SET over Triple — so this carrier grounds Triple on itself, not through a descendant proxy. TYPED EFFECT: the byte round-trip is not the identity on the abstract triple without a canonicalization discipline — blank-node labels must be freshened on merge, literal lexical forms canonicalize ('1.0' vs '1.00', Unicode normalization, language-subtag case), and N-Triples whitespace/escaping varies — so the realization carries prim:EncodingLoss, the honest cost inherited from the member term encodings. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Triple closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the whole proposition itself (spec §9d), not onto a member coordinate or a shared proxy, so physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ (the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing yin → yang) ---------------
# prim:Triple realizesAs this arrow (the forward gluing edge → direct dual-grounding of the
# concrete type on ITSELF, spec §9a). The arrow IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point
# T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): realizesFrom prim:Triple (source, yin), encoding /
# frameOutput prim:TripleNTriplesProduct (target X, yang), frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss (the
# typed error|effect Y). No isProvisional: Triple is a resolvable, directly-realizable concrete type.
prim:Triple prim:realizesAs prim:realize_Triple_NTriplesProduct .

prim:realize_Triple_NTriplesProduct a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Triple ⟼ N-Triples UTF-8 Product(Subject, Predicate, Object) line; frameEffect encoding-loss" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Triple ;
    prim:encoding prim:TripleNTriplesProduct ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:TripleNTriplesProduct ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Triple — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Triple (yin, formal.ttl, a direct subclass of prim:FormalType and the atomic proposition of the RDF-term tower) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:TripleNTriplesProduct (yang X — the N-Triples UTF-8 serialization of the ordered Product(Subject, Predicate, Object)), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MEMBER STRUCTURE: the three coordinates the arrow serializes are held by the unary law as independently typed towers — the Subject and Object as URN or blank-node identifiers (prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN / prim:BlankNodeLabel → prim:BlankNode) plus, for the Object, a typed literal (prim:LiteralUtf8 → prim:Literal); the Predicate always a URN (prim:UrnUtf8 → prim:URN) — so this arrow composes the member realizations into the whole-proposition realization, honoring S/O/P as distinct geometry rather than three interchangeable strings. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Triple realizesAs this canonical N-Triples arrow; a fixed-arity prim:CompositeCarrier product of the three raw member encodings, or a Turtle/RDF-graph-embedded form, are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same triple realization — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is exactly the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model, and this fragment commits to the N-Triples UTF-8 line as Triple's canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss — the byte round-trip is not the identity on the abstract triple without a canonicalization discipline: blank-node labels are graph-local and must be freshened on merge, literal lexical forms canonicalize, and N-Triples escaping/whitespace varies, so the arrow is lossy exactly where the RDF term algebra says it is. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Triple is a concrete, directly-realizable RDF-term type, not an unresolved abstract supertype." .

prim:Tuple a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: ordered member-encoding concatenation → Tuple ------
# A categorical PRODUCT carrier, NOT a scalar and NOT a homogeneous ByteVector: a fixed-arity,
# HETEROGENEOUS, ordered concatenation of member encodings whose components may each be a different
# width, encoding, and byte-order. It reuses the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of
# physical.ttl (which byteDescendsTo prim:Octet). Because the general Tuple's member list is not
# pinned to one width, the carrier is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence
# granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount records the
# concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (≤ 128, the ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c),
# since the total span is the sum of the member widths and varies with the member list. prim:byteOrder
# prim:EndianNeutral because the concatenation itself imposes NO cross-member ordering — each member
# encoding carries its own endianness internally, so there is no whole-Tuple byte order to fix. NO
# prim:signedness is declared: signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned →
# Integer/Natural) and does not apply to a heterogeneous product (as prim:Real likewise omits it).
# prim:alignment 1 / prim:padding 0 record a tightly packed concatenation with no inter-member filler
# (a naturally aligned specialization such as the Rational INT32-pair may instead declare alignment 4).
# prim:interpretAs prim:Tuple makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:TupleProductEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "ordered member-encoding concatenation · octet-granular · EndianNeutral → Tuple [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:alignment 1 ;
    prim:padding 0 ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Tuple ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the tuple / finite categorical PRODUCT: the in-order CONCATENATION of the byte encodings of a fixed-arity, HETEROGENEOUS component list laid contiguously over the reusable prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier of physical.ttl. Because the general tuple's members are not pinned to a single width, the carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR — prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits) — since a tuple's total span is the SUM of its member widths and so varies with the member list; a fixed-member specialization (e.g. the Rational INT32-pair) instead pins a single scalar prim:bitWidth. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): each component IS itself a floor prim:PhysicalEncoding — a prim:Int32, a prim:Float64, a prim:StringUtf8, a nested product, and so on — and the tuple's bytes are exactly those member byte runs abutted in declaration order; the fixed arity is part of the encoding's identity and carries NO length prefix, which is precisely why a product is a prim:CompositeCarrier and NOT a homogeneous variable-length prim:ByteVector (whose equal-schema occurrences are counted by prim:byteCount alone). prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral because the concatenation imposes no CROSS-member ordering — each member carries its own endianness internally — and NO prim:signedness is declared because signedness is an integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) inapplicable to a heterogeneous product, exactly as prim:Real omits it for a float. prim:alignment 1 with prim:padding 0 records a tightly packed concatenation with no inter-member filler. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the product carrier prim:CompositeCarrier prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of every member bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (the byte-stream carrier closure — stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Tuple is the direct subclass of prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's product branch and the general shape BEHIND the floor's named products — a Rational numerator·denominator pair, a Complex real·imaginary pair, an Interval start·end pair, a Quantity magnitude·unit pair, and the unary law's Frame output·effect product are all fixed-arity Tuples; it is kept distinct from the ordered HOMOGENEOUS prim:List (variable length, one element type, length-prefixed) and the categorical-dual prim:Sum (tagged union, exactly one alternative). TYPED EFFECT: a faithful in-order concatenation is LOSSLESS framing — the member byte runs are preserved verbatim and the fixed arity delimits them — so the realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect; any rounding, overflow, or encoding loss belongs to the individual member encodings, not to the tuple framing. IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Tuple closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the tuple type itself (spec §9d), not onto a shared proxy carrier, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Tuple to the encoding ----------------
# Tuple ⟼ ordered member-encoding concatenation as a Frame: realizesFrom Tuple, encoding/frameOutput
# the product encoding above, frameEffect prim:NoEffect (faithful concatenation is lossless framing).
# A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Tuple is a concrete, directly-
# realizable product type grounded on the concatenation-over-CompositeCarrier carrier. This ADDS a
# second realizesAs edge alongside the pre-existing prim:realize_Tuple_Concat (monad polymorphism).
prim:Tuple prim:realizesAs prim:TupleRealizesProduct .

prim:TupleRealizesProduct a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Tuple ⟼ ordered member-encoding concatenation · octet-granular · EndianNeutral; frameEffect none (lossless framing)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Tuple ;
    prim:encoding prim:TupleProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:TupleProductEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Tuple — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Tuple (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's product branch) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:TupleProductEncoding (yang X — the in-order concatenation of the tuple's member encodings over the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:CompositeCarrier → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Tuple realizesAs this canonical concatenation arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_Tuple_Concat (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a naturally aligned padded product form, or a specialization pinning fixed-width members (the Rational INT32-pair, the Complex Float64-pair), are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same tuple realization, and this fragment commits to the tightly packed octet-granular concatenation as the general canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — a faithful in-order concatenation is LOSSLESS framing: the member byte runs are laid down verbatim and the fixed arity delimits them, so the tuple framing introduces no rounding, narrowing, overflow, encoding loss, or ordering canonicalization of its own; any such effect belongs to the individual member encodings (an INT32 member's prim:OverflowDomain, a Float64 member's prim:RoundingIEEE754), not to the product that concatenates them. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Tuple is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type — the product carrier itself — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy." .

prim:URI a prim:FormalType .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:URI — reflexive interpretAs → URI.
# =====================================================================================
# Mirrors the carrier shape of physical.ttl's prim:UrnUtf8 (an octet-granular interpreted
# UTF-8 octet stream) but interprets back to prim:URI, not prim:URN, so URI's own round-trip
# is a true identity. UTF-8 is the canonical identifier realization of the tower (plan T4:
# "URN/URI/IRI/QName/BlankNode → UTF-8"); because a URI's characters are ASCII-restricted and
# ASCII is the one-octet floor of UTF-8, the octets are the same bytes either lens reads.

prim:UriUtf8 a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet stream (interpreted ASCII-restricted identifier) → URI" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ; prim:byteWidth 1 ; prim:byteCount 128 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:URI ;
    rdfs:comment "The Uniform-Resource-Identifier carrier: an interpreted UTF-8 octet stream over a byte carrier, holding the ASCII-restricted global identifier a URI is. Octet-granular — prim:bitWidth 8 is the per-occurrence granule (one prim:Octet, cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255) and prim:byteCount 128 is the ordered-vector occurrence ceiling of the second width cap (spec §9c) — and prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral because a UTF-8 code unit is a single octet with no cross-octet ordering to fix. SUBATOMIC DESCENT: the stream is an ordered prim:ByteVector of octets, so it byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, then prim:Octet byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble, then prim:Nibble byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, bottoming out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor with no carrier escaping the byte substrate; it is a homogeneous octet run, not a heterogeneous product, so it has no member encodings other than the repeated octet. SUBTYPING: URI ⊂ URN in the formal tower, and this carrier is the ASCII-restricted specialisation of the same UTF-8 identifier space prim:UrnUtf8 grounds; non-ASCII octets are handled by percent-encoding as a realization facet, which is why the realizing Frame carries prim:EncodingLoss (percent-encoding / normalization variance). REFLEXIVE SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:URI closes the mutual colimit back onto URI itself (a TRUE identity, spec §9d), rather than onto URN as the inherited prim:UrnUtf8 proxy would." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ — a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad from prim:URI.
# =====================================================================================
# realizesFrom prim:URI ; encoding/frameOutput prim:UriUtf8 ; frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss.
# This is the forward gluing edge; the paired prim:URI prim:realizesAs edge below completes
# the dual-grounding the FormalTypeShape checks (≥1 realizesAs on every realized FormalType).

prim:URI prim:realizesAs prim:realize_URI_Utf8 .

prim:realize_URI_Utf8 a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "URI ⟼ UTF-8 interpreted identifier (percent-encoding / normalization loss)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:URI ;
    prim:encoding prim:UriUtf8 ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:UriUtf8 ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:URI — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and thus itself the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): its source is prim:realizesFrom prim:URI, its output coordinate X is prim:frameOutput prim:UriUtf8 (the same encoding named by prim:encoding, descending octet → nibble → bit to the prim:Bit floor), and its error/effect coordinate Y is prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss. The effect is REAL, not decorative: a URI is ASCII-restricted, so realizing an identifier that carries non-ASCII intent forces percent-encoding of the offending octets, and Unicode normalization-form differences mean the byte round-trip is not the bare identity on the abstract identifier without a stated normalization discipline — exactly the surrogate/normalization prim:EncodingLoss the text encodings carry. This arrow is prim:URI's OWN direct realization onto its OWN reflexive carrier prim:UriUtf8 (interpretAs URI); it is the canonical one this fragment commits prim:prim_URI to. URI ⊂ URN and URI's subtypes IRI/QName are taxonomic subtyping: each of URN, IRI and QName carries its own DIRECT realization onto its own reflexive carrier, so none depends on ancestry for its PRIMARY grounding — but STEP B now materialises the ancestral grounding too, so URI IS ALSO grounded through its ancestor URN by the real, resolvable path prim:agp_URI_URN (via the transport prim:rt_sub_URI_URN landing in rho(URN) = prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding), and IRI/QName likewise via prim:agp_IRI_URN / prim:agp_QName_URN; the direct groundings are unchanged and the also-grounded-through-ancestor facts are now genuine edges (dual-grounded two ways). No silm:isProvisional: URI is a resolvable concrete type and this realization is genuine (spec §9b)." .

prim:URN a prim:FormalType .

# The dual-grounding forward edge: prim:URN realizesAs its own Kleisli/Frame arrow.
prim:URN prim:realizesAs prim:URN_Realization .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The TYPE-SPECIFIC physical encoding for prim:URN — the yang facet.
#     An interpreted UTF-8 octet name laid into a bounded prim:ByteVector; each occurrence
#     a prim:Octet. The mutual-colimit back-pointer prim:interpretAs prim:URN makes the
#     round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY (spec §9d).
# =====================================================================================

prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UTF-8 octet ByteVector (interpreted Resource/Uniform/Name) → URN" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:URN ;
    rdfs:comment "The Uniform-Resource-Name carrier — the yang facet of the URN taiji, the reflexive physical grounding of prim:URN itself (and, as the tower root, the addressing substrate the whole Identifier tower is shaped over). SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:URN is a direct subclass of prim:FormalType and the load-bearing root of the identifier tower URN ⊃ URI ⊃ IRI ⊃ QName (this floor deliberately inverts the classical W3C containment so the URN root stays load-bearing per the unary law); its full-lexical identity is Resource/Uniform/Name, and because S/O/P are URN towers this carrier is the addressing substrate of the entire ontology. PHYSICAL REALIZATION: an interpreted UTF-8 octet name serialized into an ordered prim:ByteVector of up to 128 byte occurrences (the carrier-ladder rung above prim:Octet), octet-granular under the two-cap model (spec §9c) — prim:bitWidth 8 is the per-occurrence granule of exactly one prim:Octet and prim:byteCount 128 is the ByteVector occurrence-count ceiling (up to 1024 bits), deliberately NOT a fixed scalar machine word. prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral because a UTF-8 code unit is a single octet with no cross-octet order to fix; prim:signedness is deliberately OMITTED because a name is not a numeric value and force-fitting a sign onto text would be a false facet (Präriehund honesty — name the absence, do not fabricate a coordinate). SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: each element of the name is itself a prim:Codepoint realized through the member encoding prim:Utf8 as a 1..4-octet run, and the URN is their in-order concatenation; those octets byte-descend prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor of the byte-stream carrier closure, so no part of the name escapes the byte substrate. DIRECT GROUNDING (spec §9a): this carrier grounds prim:URN itself reflexively. Its tower descendants prim:URI, prim:IRI and prim:QName are NOT grounded by ancestry through it — each carries its OWN direct realization onto its OWN reflexive carrier (prim:UriUtf8, prim:IriUtf8, prim:QNameUtf8), where a URI is the ASCII-restricted reading (percent-encoding non-ASCII octets), an IRI the full-Unicode reading, and a QName a prefix-expansion shortcut. URN ⊃ URI ⊃ IRI ⊃ QName is taxonomic subtyping only. TYPED EFFECT: percent-encoding and Unicode normalization of non-ASCII octets mean the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract name only under a stated normalization discipline — the honest prim:EncodingLoss carried on the gluing Frame. The prim:interpretAs prim:URN back-pointer closes the mutual colimit REFLEXIVELY (physical → interpretAs → formal = URN), a true identity at the Name level (not at prim:String or prim:Codepoint), so yang holds yin exactly." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The realization ρ for prim:URN — the gluing morphism (Kleisli/Frame arrow).
#     T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): realizesFrom prim:URN, output/encoding the
#     UTF-8 ByteVector encoding above, effect the typed prim:EncodingLoss.
# =====================================================================================

prim:URN_Realization a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "URN ⟼ UTF-8 octet ByteVector (encoding-loss: percent-encoding/normalization)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:URN ;
    prim:encoding prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:EncodingLoss ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization ρ of prim:URN — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad and the gluing morphism of the URN colimit taiji, not a passive link. It IS a Frame, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): its prim:realizesFrom is the source formal type prim:URN (yin, the Resource/Uniform/Name root of the Identifier tower in formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType), its prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput is the target byte carrier prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding (the X coordinate, a genuine byte-descending prim:PhysicalEncoding that reaches prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption), and its prim:frameEffect is the typed prim:EncodingLoss (the Y coordinate). MONADIC EFFECT: the effect is what makes this realization monadic rather than a total function — mapping an abstract identifier name through UTF-8 admits percent-encoding of non-ASCII octets and Unicode normalization-form (NFC/NFD/NFKC/NFKD) differences, so the byte round-trip is the identity on the abstract name only under a stated normalization discipline. POLYMORPHISM / DIRECT GROUNDING: prim:URN realizesAs this arrow, its OWN direct realization onto the reflexive prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding. As the tower root URN ⊃ URI ⊃ IRI ⊃ QName is taxonomic subtyping only, NOT the grounding mechanism: its descendants prim:URI, prim:IRI and prim:QName are each grounded by their OWN direct narrower arrows (the ASCII-restricted URI reading via prim:realize_URI_Utf8, the full-Unicode IRI reading via prim:realize_IRI_Utf8, the prefix-expansion QName shortcut via prim:realize_QName_Utf8), each onto its own reflexive carrier — none leans on this Frame by ancestry (spec §9a). prim:URN prim:realizesAs this arrow supplies the dual-grounding forward edge the SHACL FormalTypeShape and the litmus walk require. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:URN is a concrete, directly-realizable identifier root and not an unresolved abstract supertype (spec §9a/§9b); composing it byte-descends to the prim:Bit floor, the identity of that composition being the monad unit η." .

prim:Unit a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) the type-specific physical encoding of prim:Unit ---------------------------
# The empty-product carrier: zero bits, zero bytes, the physical realization of the terminal
# type's single information-free value. A fixed-width encoding governed by the SCALAR width cap
# prim:bitWidth <= 128 (spec §9c) at the degenerate floor bitWidth 0, NOT a variable-length
# ByteVector-family carrier — so no prim:byteCount is declared. No prim:signedness is declared:
# signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned -> Integer/Natural), which does
# not apply to the empty product. prim:byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because a zero-octet
# encoding has nothing to order. Fresh frag-local node (distinct from physical.ttl's zero-width
# prim:UnitEncoding) so this fragment merges additively.
prim:UnitTerminalEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "zero-width encoding (empty product) → Unit [type-specific terminal encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 0 ; prim:byteWidth 0 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Unit ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of prim:Unit: the zero-width encoding — zero bits, zero bytes, zero byte occurrences — the empty-product carrier into which the terminal type's single information-free value is realized. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Unit is the terminal object of the formal category (the empty product, the nullary prim:Tuple, the identity for the categorical product), so its physical facet must be the empty product too; a value carrying no information occupies no bits, which is why bitWidth and byteWidth are both 0. It is deliberately kept DISTINCT from prim:Void's zero-width carrier: both span zero octets, but Unit is inhabited by EXACTLY ONE value (this empty tuple) whereas Void is inhabited by NONE — the difference is carried by prim:interpretAs (Unit vs Void) and by the realization effect (prim:NoEffect vs prim:Uninhabited), never by the byte width. PHYSICAL / SUBATOMIC DESCENT: this encoding is exactly the unary law's registered empty byte-vector occurrence — the Byte.Vector.Empty alternative of the byte-stream carrier closure, an ordered empty-stream revision attaching the registered schema. Its carrier lineage to the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue-trust-root floor is the physical.ttl ladder prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit; this particular occurrence spans zero octets, but the carrier's descent to the Bit floor is intact and no boundary is exempted (the base unit of the whole floor remains bit- and byte-vectors). It references the physical.ttl carrier ladder and the prim:EndianNeutral byte-order individual. WIDTH CAP: a fixed-width SCALAR encoding at the degenerate floor, ceilinged by the scalar cap prim:bitWidth 0 <= 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately no prim:byteCount (that is the ByteVector-family cap) and no prim:signedness (an integer facet inapplicable to the empty product). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Unit closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Unit itself, making the round-trip physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet a TRUE IDENTITY, not a proxy. A one-octet constant-tag encoding (a single reserved code) is the packed sibling grounding, the zero-width empty product being the canonical, tightest terminal carrier." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ: a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad ----------------
# Unit realizesAs this arrow; the arrow realizesFrom Unit into prim:UnitTerminalEncoding,
# exposing that same encoding as the Frame output and carrying prim:NoEffect (the terminal
# type's one value maps to the empty product with nothing rounded, narrowed, overflowed, or lost).
prim:Unit prim:realizesAs prim:UnitRealizesTerminal .

prim:UnitRealizesTerminal a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Unit ⟼ zero-width encoding (empty product); frameEffect NoEffect" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Unit ;
    prim:encoding prim:UnitTerminalEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:UnitTerminalEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Unit — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary byte-frame law's Yoneda point T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) with T = prim:Unit, X = prim:UnitTerminalEncoding, and Y = prim:NoEffect. It is not a passive link but IS a Frame: prim:realizesFrom prim:Unit (its source formal type, yin), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:UnitTerminalEncoding (its chosen physical target X, yang — the zero-width empty-product carrier, exposed as the Frame's output coordinate), and prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect (its Y). EFFECT RATIONALE: the effect is genuinely prim:NoEffect — the neutral element of effect composition — because the terminal type has exactly ONE value and it maps bijectively onto the single empty-product occurrence: no rounding, no narrowing, no width-bounded overflow, and no encoding loss is possible on a value that carries no information, so the byte round-trip is trivially the identity. MONADIC ROLE: prim:Unit is the natural home of the monad unit; this arrow is the trivial identity-shaped realization of the terminal object, and prim:NoEffect is exactly the neutral effect the monad's unit law relies on. Because its output encoding is the registered empty byte-vector occurrence whose carrier byte-descends prim:ByteVector -> prim:Octet -> prim:Nibble -> prim:Bit, this arrow's Frame output reaches the width-1 prim:Bit floor with no boundary exemption; X is a real byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding, never a bare formal class. This is the gluing morphism the colimit-taiji Unit primitive selects (prim:gluedBy), and it is a genuine realization — not an isProvisional placeholder — since prim:Unit is a concrete, fully resolvable terminal type." .

prim:UnitOfMeasure a prim:FormalType .

# Forward dual-grounding edge (FormalType -> Realization): prim:UnitOfMeasure realizesAs
# this fragment's Kleisli arrow, so the type is dual-grounded on ITSELF (self, not via an
# ancestor or descendant) — the direct-for-concrete half of spec §9a.
prim:UnitOfMeasure prim:realizesAs prim:realize_UnitOfMeasure_Uint16Index .

# =====================================================================================
# (a) The type-specific PhysicalEncoding — a UINT16 unit-registry index -> UnitOfMeasure
# =====================================================================================
# Grounding choice (hint: SI+derived; URN or index): the canonical concrete carrier for a
# named reference magnitude is an INDEX into a unit registry — the same UINT16 machine-type
# layout physical.ttl fixes for prim:Uint16 (bitWidth 16, byteWidth 2, byteOrder LE,
# unsigned), but with prim:interpretAs closing onto prim:UnitOfMeasure itself rather than
# onto prim:Natural, so the colimit round-trip is a TRUE IDENTITY (spec §9d) instead of
# landing on the integer proxy a bare prim:Uint16 would. A fixed-width SCALAR encoding, so
# it is capped by prim:bitWidth <= 128 (the first of the two width caps, spec §9c) and
# declares NO prim:byteCount (that cap is for the variable-length ByteVector family).
prim:UnitOfMeasureUint16Index a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "UINT16 unit-registry index -> UnitOfMeasure (reflexive)" ;
    prim:bitWidth 16 ; prim:byteWidth 2 ; prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:signedness prim:Unsigned ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:UnitOfMeasure ;
    rdfs:comment "The unit-of-measure carrier: an unsigned 16-bit index into a registry of named reference magnitudes, one registry entry per unit (the SI base units metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole, candela, plus derived units such as newton or pascal, each entry holding a conversion factor and offset to the SI base of its prim:Dimension). SUBTYPING: prim:UnitOfMeasure is a subclass of prim:FormalType (Quantity tower) and the parent of prim:Currency; it is named UnitOfMeasure precisely to avoid colliding with the type-theoretic terminal prim:Unit of the algebraic tower (Praeriehund honesty: the collision is named, not overloaded). PHYSICAL: this encoding mirrors the prim:Uint16 machine type of physical.ttl exactly (bitWidth 16, byteWidth 2, byteOrder prim:LE, signedness prim:Unsigned) but is a distinct individual because its prim:interpretAs closes REFLEXIVELY onto prim:UnitOfMeasure (identity round-trip, spec §9d) whereas prim:Uint16 interprets onto prim:Natural. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: one occurrence is two prim:Octet occurrences laid least-significant-octet first; each prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit (the ladder edges live in physical.ttl), so the encoding bottoms out at the width-1 prim:Bit floor with nothing beneath it. It is a scalar index, not a composite/product, so it has no member encodings to enumerate. EFFECT: an exact registry index is a lossless bijective label (each code names exactly one unit, and back), so the realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect." .

# =====================================================================================
# (b) The Realization — the Kleisli/Frame arrow of the realization monad rho
# =====================================================================================
# T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y): realizesFrom the formal source, encoding /
# frameOutput the physical target X (the encoding above), frameEffect the typed cost Y.
prim:realize_UnitOfMeasure_Uint16Index a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "UnitOfMeasure |-> UINT16 unit-registry index" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:UnitOfMeasure ;
    prim:encoding prim:UnitOfMeasureUint16Index ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:UnitOfMeasureUint16Index ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The realization rho gluing the FormalType prim:UnitOfMeasure (yin, formal.ttl) to the PhysicalEncoding prim:UnitOfMeasureUint16Index (yang, this fragment) as a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad — not a passive link but the unary law's Yoneda point T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y). Its prim:realizesFrom back-link names the source formal type, its prim:encoding and prim:frameOutput both name the chosen physical target X (the UINT16 registry index), and its prim:frameEffect names the typed cost Y. EFFECT RATIONALE: assigning a named unit a distinct registry code and reading it back is an exact, lossless, bijective operation — no rounding, no narrowing, no overflow within the 65536-entry code space, no encoding loss — so the honest typed effect is prim:NoEffect, the neutral element of effect composition (contrast prim:Currency, whose descendant realization also carries no representational loss on the code itself although its conversion FACTORS are time-varying exchange rates recorded as a separate facet). One formal type may realizesAs many arrows (monad polymorphism); this is UnitOfMeasure's canonical index arrow, and byte-descent is the Kleisli composition of its output down through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor." .

prim:Vector a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) The type-specific physical encoding: dense fixed-length equal-width array → Vector ------
# An ORDERED, HOMOGENEOUS, FIXED-length carrier of EQUAL-WIDTH elements, NOT a fixed-arity
# heterogeneous product and NOT a variable-length length-prefixed sequence: a DENSE contiguous run
# of a pinned count of equal-width element encodings, all of the SAME element type, laid abutted in
# index order with NO count prefix, NO per-element tag, and NO terminator (the count is part of the
# Vector's own type identity, not carried in the byte stream), so the k-th element sits at exactly
# offset k × element-width and the layout is directly index-addressable. It reuses the reusable
# prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl (which byteDescendsTo prim:Octet),
# whose schema-qualified Empty|Link algebra physically holds the equal-width members. Because the
# concrete count varies from Vector value to Vector value (the type pins A count, the carrier bounds
# the ceiling), the carrier is octet-granular: prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule
# (one octet), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated
# occurrence-count ceiling (≤ 128, the ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024
# bits). prim:byteOrder prim:LE governs the octet order WITHIN each multi-octet element (a Vector of
# prim:Float32 or prim:Int32 lays each element least-significant octet first); for the canonical
# byte-array case (elements are single prim:Octet / prim:Uint8) the per-element order is
# endian-neutral and LE is the declared multi-octet default. NO prim:signedness is declared:
# signedness is the integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) and does not
# apply to an array frame (as prim:List and prim:Tuple likewise omit it); the ELEMENTS carry their
# own signedness. prim:interpretAs prim:Vector makes the colimit round-trip a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:VectorDenseArrayEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "dense fixed-length equal-width array (contiguous equal-width element encodings, no prefix/tag/terminator, index-addressable) · octet-granular · LE → Vector [type-specific canonical encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 8 ;
    prim:byteWidth 1 ;
    prim:byteCount 128 ;
    prim:byteOrder prim:LE ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Vector ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of the vector: a DENSE, FIXED-length, EQUAL-WIDTH array — a contiguous run of a pinned count of element encodings, ALL of one element type and ALL of the same width, laid abutted in index order with NO count prefix, NO per-element tag, and NO terminator (the count belongs to the Vector's own type identity, so it is not carried in the byte stream) — laid over the reusable prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier of physical.ttl, whose schema-qualified Empty|Link algebra IS an ordered run (a Link carries one admitted head element and a tail of the same schema, the byte-stream carrier closure of the unary law). Because every element has the SAME width, the k-th element sits at exactly offset k × element-width, so the layout is DIRECTLY INDEX-ADDRESSABLE — the defining physical distinction from the variable-length prim:List (whose UINT32 length prefix and possibly-composite members forbid constant-time offset arithmetic) and from the heterogeneous prim:Tuple (whose members differ in width, so element positions must be computed from a per-member layout, not a single stride). The carrier is OCTET-GRANULAR — prim:bitWidth 8 records the per-occurrence granule (one octet of the code space, cardinality 256, ordinal 0..255), prim:byteWidth 1 the whole-octet granule, and prim:byteCount 128 the concatenated occurrence-count ceiling (the variable-length ByteVector-family second width cap of spec §9c, up to 1024 bits; the scalar prim:bitWidth ≤ 128 cap governs a single element, not the whole array) — because a vector's total span is the pinned count times the element width and so varies from value to value. prim:byteOrder prim:LE governs the octet order WITHIN each multi-octet element (a Vector of prim:Float32 lays each IEEE-754 single least-significant octet first, a Vector of prim:Int32 lays each two's-complement word LE); for the canonical fixed-length BYTE-ARRAY case — elements are single prim:Octet / prim:Uint8, the homogeneous byte array of the type hint — the per-element order is endian-neutral and LE is merely the declared multi-octet default. NO prim:signedness is declared because signedness is an integer facet (Signed/Unsigned → Integer/Natural) inapplicable to an array frame, exactly as prim:List and prim:Tuple omit it; the individual ELEMENTS carry their own signedness. MEMBER ENCODINGS (composite grounding, unary law): every element IS itself a floor prim:PhysicalEncoding of the ONE element type — a byte vector lays raw prim:Octet occurrences, a numeric vector lays prim:Float32 IEEE-754 singles or prim:Int32 two's-complement words, an embedding vector lays equal-width prim:Float32 components — and the vector's bytes are exactly those equal-width member byte runs abutted in index order; because the element type and width are uniform and the count is pinned by the type, there is NO per-element tag and NO length prefix, which is precisely why a Vector is a dense homogeneous prim:ByteVector-family run and NOT a fixed-arity heterogeneous prim:CompositeCarrier product (mixed member widths, arity carried without dense stride) and NOT a length-prefixed prim:List. SUBATOMIC BYTE DESCENT: the ordered-vector carrier prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet, and each constituent octet descends prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit, so every bit of every equal-width element bottoms out transitively at the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue floor (stream → block → container → value) with nothing below it. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Vector is the pinned-length subclass of prim:List (a Vector is a List whose length is fixed and whose elements are equal-width components) and the rank-1 case of prim:Tensor (a rank-1 Tensor is isomorphic to a Vector, but Tensor is kept distinct because it adds explicit shape-and-stride metadata); it is kept distinct from the variable-length prim:List (length prefix, possibly-composite members), the fixed-arity HETEROGENEOUS prim:Tuple (mixed types/widths), the unordered prim:Set (order canonicalized away), and the unordered prim:Bag (multiplicities stored) — a pinned count, equal-width homogeneous elements, and dense index-addressability ARE part of a Vector's identity, and it is the physical home of the AOB tensor/aggregate block's rank-1 layer (Directives 1/2/3). TYPED EFFECT: a faithful dense equal-width array preserves component VALUES and their INDEX ORDER exactly — the members are laid down verbatim at their computed offsets with no prefix, tag, or reordering — so the vector framing is LOSSLESS and the realization Frame carries prim:NoEffect; any rounding, overflow, or encoding loss belongs to the individual element encodings (a prim:Float32 component's prim:RoundingIEEE754, a prim:Int32 component's prim:OverflowDomain), NOT to the array framing that concatenates them (in deliberate contrast to prim:Set's prim:OrderingCanonicalization, since a Vector's order is intrinsic and preserved verbatim). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Vector closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto the vector type itself (spec §9d), not onto its supertype prim:List, nor onto prim:Tensor, nor a bare prim:ByteVector, so the round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY and yang holds yin exactly." .

# ---- (b) The realization ρ: the Kleisli/Frame arrow gluing Vector to the encoding ---------------
# Vector ⟼ dense fixed-length equal-width array as a Frame: realizesFrom Vector, encoding/frameOutput
# the dense-array encoding above, frameEffect prim:NoEffect (a faithful dense equal-width array is
# lossless framing). A total, well-typed realization — no isProvisional dodge, because Vector is a
# concrete, directly-realizable fixed-length homogeneous array grounded on the dense-equal-width-run-
# over-ByteVector carrier. This ADDS a second realizesAs edge alongside the pre-existing
# prim:realize_Vector_FixedArray (monad polymorphism).
prim:Vector prim:realizesAs prim:VectorRealizesDenseArray .

prim:VectorRealizesDenseArray a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Vector ⟼ dense fixed-length equal-width array · octet-granular · LE; frameEffect none (lossless framing)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Vector ;
    prim:encoding prim:VectorDenseArrayEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:VectorDenseArrayEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Vector — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary law's Yoneda point T → Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y), not a passive link. It departs prim:realizesFrom the formal type prim:Vector (yin, formal.ttl, rdfs:subClassOf prim:List in the Aggregate tower — the pinned-length subclass of List and the rank-1 case of prim:Tensor) and lands prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput on prim:VectorDenseArrayEncoding (yang X — the dense, fixed-length, equal-width array of the vector's member encodings over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier), so the Frame output X is a genuine byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, never a bare formal class — there is no byte-stream-closure exemption. MONADIC POLYMORPHISM: prim:Vector realizesAs this canonical dense-array arrow AND, in the merged floor, the pre-existing prim:realize_Vector_FixedArray (physical.ttl / realization.ttl) — one formal type fanning out to many encodings is precisely the polymorphism the realization monad exists to model; a strided/padded array, a SIMD-aligned lane layout, or a big-endian element order are the sibling polymorphic choices of the same vector realization, and this fragment commits to the tag-free prefix-free dense equal-width run as the general index-addressable canonical facet. TYPED EFFECT (Y): prim:frameEffect prim:NoEffect — a faithful dense equal-width array is LOSSLESS framing: the equal-width member byte runs are laid down verbatim at their computed offsets (offset k × element-width), so the vector framing preserves component values and their index order exactly and introduces no rounding, narrowing, overflow, encoding loss, or ordering canonicalization OF ITS OWN (in deliberate contrast to prim:Set, whose realization carries prim:OrderingCanonicalization because a set has no intrinsic order; a Vector's order IS its identity and is preserved verbatim). Any such effect belongs to the individual element encodings (a prim:Float32 component's prim:RoundingIEEE754, a prim:Int32 component's prim:OverflowDomain, a prim:Utf8 element's prim:EncodingLoss), not to the array that concatenates them. This is a total, well-typed realization with NO silm:isProvisional dodge, because prim:Vector is a concrete, directly-realizable composite type — a fixed-length homogeneous equal-width array, the rank-1 layer of the AOB tensor block — not an unresolved abstract supertype needing a descendant proxy." .

prim:Void a prim:FormalType .

# ---- (a) the type-specific physical encoding of prim:Void ---------------------------
# The empty carrier: zero bits, zero bytes, zero occurrences — the physical nothing of the
# initial type. A fixed-width encoding governed by the SCALAR width cap prim:bitWidth <= 128
# (spec §9c) at the degenerate floor bitWidth 0, NOT a variable-length ByteVector-family
# carrier — so no prim:byteCount is declared. No prim:signedness is declared: signedness is the
# integer-representation facet (Signed/Unsigned -> Integer/Natural), which does not apply to the
# empty coproduct. prim:byteOrder is prim:EndianNeutral because a zero-octet encoding has nothing
# to order. Fresh frag-local node (distinct from physical.ttl's zero-width prim:VoidEmpty) so
# this fragment merges additively.
prim:VoidEmptyEncoding a prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "empty 0-byte encoding (uninhabited empty coproduct) → Void [type-specific initial encoding]" ;
    prim:bitWidth 0 ; prim:byteWidth 0 ; prim:byteOrder prim:EndianNeutral ;
    prim:interpretAs prim:Void ;
    rdfs:comment "The canonical physical facet of prim:Void: the empty 0-byte encoding — zero bits, zero bytes, zero byte occurrences — the physical nothing into which the initial/uninhabited type is realized. SUBTYPING RATIONALE: prim:Void is the initial object of the formal category (the empty coproduct, the nullary prim:Sum, the dual of the terminal prim:Unit), a type with NO values at all, so a value of Void can never be constructed and a function out of Void exists uniquely and vacuously; its physical facet is therefore the empty encoding, which is why bitWidth and byteWidth are both 0. It is deliberately kept DISTINCT from prim:Unit's zero-width carrier: both span zero octets, but Unit is inhabited by EXACTLY ONE value (the empty tuple) whereas Void is inhabited by NONE — the difference is carried by prim:interpretAs (Void vs Unit) and by the realization effect (prim:Uninhabited vs prim:NoEffect), never by the byte width. This is the ONE honest edge of the floor (spec §9f): its width being zero is NOT a dodge but the truthful physical image of an empty domain. PHYSICAL / SUBATOMIC DESCENT: this encoding is exactly the unary law's registered empty byte-vector occurrence — the Byte.Vector.Empty alternative of the byte-stream carrier closure, an ordered empty-stream revision attaching the registered schema. Its carrier lineage to the width-1 prim:Bit eigenvalue-trust-root floor is the physical.ttl ladder prim:ByteVector prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Octet prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Nibble prim:byteDescendsTo prim:Bit; this particular occurrence spans zero octets, but the carrier's descent to the Bit floor is intact and no boundary is exempted (the base unit of the whole floor remains bit- and byte-vectors). It references the physical.ttl carrier ladder and the prim:EndianNeutral byte-order individual. WIDTH CAP: a fixed-width SCALAR encoding at the degenerate floor, ceilinged by the scalar cap prim:bitWidth 0 <= 128 (spec §9c) — deliberately no prim:byteCount (that is the ByteVector-family cap) and no prim:signedness (an integer facet inapplicable to the empty coproduct). IDENTITY SEAL: prim:interpretAs prim:Void closes the mutual colimit reflexively onto Void itself, making the round-trip physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet a TRUE IDENTITY, not a proxy — the seal is total even though the type is uninhabited, because reflexive closure is a fact about the naming (interpretAs = formalFacet = prim:Void), not about the number of inhabitants." .

# ---- (b) the realization ρ: a Kleisli/Frame arrow of the Frame monad ----------------
# Void realizesAs this arrow; the arrow realizesFrom Void into prim:VoidEmptyEncoding, exposing
# that same encoding as the Frame output and carrying prim:Uninhabited — the honest edge effect:
# the domain is empty, so this is the realization of a value that can never be constructed. It is
# deliberately NOT prim:NoEffect (a lossless map of nothing is qualitatively different from a
# lossless map of a real value) and deliberately NOT silm:isProvisional (spec §9b/§9f): Void is a
# resolvable, in-scope initial type, genuinely realized, its typed cost being that the domain is empty.
prim:Void prim:realizesAs prim:VoidRealizesEmpty .

prim:VoidRealizesEmpty a prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:label "ρ: Void ⟼ empty 0-byte encoding; frameEffect Uninhabited (the honest edge)" ;
    prim:realizesFrom prim:Void ;
    prim:encoding prim:VoidEmptyEncoding ;
    prim:frameOutput prim:VoidEmptyEncoding ;
    prim:frameEffect prim:Uninhabited ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism ρ for prim:Void — a Kleisli arrow of the Frame monad, the unary byte-frame law's Yoneda point T -> Frame(output: X, error|effect: Y) with T = prim:Void, X = prim:VoidEmptyEncoding, and Y = prim:Uninhabited. It is not a passive link but IS a Frame: prim:realizesFrom prim:Void (its source formal type, yin), prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput prim:VoidEmptyEncoding (its chosen physical target X, yang — the empty 0-byte carrier, exposed as the Frame's output coordinate), and prim:frameEffect prim:Uninhabited (its Y). EFFECT RATIONALE: the effect is genuinely prim:Uninhabited — the honest edge effect of spec §9f — because the initial type has NO values at all, so this is the realization of an impossible value: there is nothing to round, narrow, overflow, or lose, yet the situation is qualitatively different from a lossless map of a real value, so it is deliberately NOT prim:NoEffect (which is Unit's neutral element, the map of the one empty-product value) and NOT prim:OverflowDomain or prim:EncodingLoss (which presuppose inhabitants). This is precisely how universality of dual-grounding stays TOTAL without a dodge: Void is genuinely realized rather than marked silm:isProvisional, its typed cost being the emptiness of its domain (Praeriehund honesty — the one honest edge is NAMED as an effect, not force-fit into a false 'no effect' and not hidden as provisional). MONADIC ROLE: prim:Void is the initial object and the dual of the terminal prim:Unit that houses the monad unit; where Unit's arrow carries prim:NoEffect, Void's carries prim:Uninhabited, the effect-level dual. Because its output encoding is the registered empty byte-vector occurrence whose carrier byte-descends prim:ByteVector -> prim:Octet -> prim:Nibble -> prim:Bit, this arrow's Frame output reaches the width-1 prim:Bit floor with no boundary exemption; X is a real byte-carrying prim:PhysicalEncoding, never a bare formal class. This is the gluing morphism the colimit-taiji Void primitive selects (prim:gluedBy), and it is a genuine realization — not an isProvisional placeholder — since prim:Void is a concrete, fully resolvable initial type." .

# =====================================================================================
# STEP B — rho AS A GENUINE FUNCTOR ON ARROWS + MATERIALISED ANCESTRAL GROUNDING
# =====================================================================================
# The panel residual "X is also grounded through its ancestor" is TRUE categorically but was
# UNMATERIALISED: rho (realization) acted only on OBJECTS (each FormalType -> its carrier).
# This block makes rho a REAL FUNCTOR ON MORPHISMS over the thin formal category authored in
# formal.ttl (STEP A). It ADDS structure and deletes no claim (enrich-never-strip):
#
#   1. rho on OBJECTS is made explicit and single-valued as prim:rhoObject: each of the 59
#      tower classes maps to its OWN canonical reflexive carrier (an encoding whose
#      prim:interpretAs closes back to that class), and the abstract apex prim:FormalType maps
#      to the generic prim:ByteVector carrier (the erasure of "some value" to "some bytes").
#   2. rho on ARROWS is materialised as prim:RealizationTransport: for every prim:SubtypeArrow
#      f: A sub B a transport prim:rt_sub_A_B: rho(A) -> rho(B) (matching prim:transportDom /
#      prim:transportCod), the realization-compatibility morphism between the two carriers.
#   3. The FUNCTOR LAWS are real edges, not prose:
#        * IDENTITY  rho(id_A) = id on rho(A): prim:rt_id_A carries prim:isTransportIdentity true
#          with prim:transportDom = prim:transportCod = rho(A) (one per object of the category).
#        * COMPOSITION  rho(g after f) = rho(g) after rho(f): prim:rt_comp_A_C reifies its
#          decomposition through prim:transportComposeFirst/Second/Into, mirroring the formal
#          CompositeArrow's composeArrowFirst/Second/Into edge-for-edge; thin-ness makes it the
#          unique decomposition so the law holds on the nose.
#   4. The residual "also grounded through an ancestor" is now a REAL, RESOLVABLE edge
#      prim:AncestralGroundingPath: for every subtype A with a realized ancestor B (B one of the
#      59, not the unrealized apex), prim:agp_A_B carries prim:ancestralViaArrow (the formal
#      arrow A -> B), prim:ancestralViaTransport (its rho-image), and prim:ancestralLandsIn
#      (rho(B) = the ancestor's own realization carrier, which the transport's cod resolves to).
#      So a subtype is dual-grounded TWO WAYS - directly on its own carrier AND transported up
#      into the realization of its ancestor - both materialised (Currency, URI, Categorical,
#      Longitude and every similar case).
#
# These transport nodes are deliberately NOT prim:Realization / prim:Composition individuals and
# use their OWN property names (prim:transportCompose* not prim:compose*), so the RealizationShape,
# EncodingShape and KleisliCompositionShape of primitives.shapes.ttl never target them and the
# floor stays green. Their dom/cod range over prim:PhysicalObject (a common supertype of encodings
# and the ByteVector apex) so rdfs range-inference never mistypes prim:ByteVector as a
# prim:PhysicalEncoding (which would trip EncodingShape). The new classes are NOT rdfs:subClassOf
# prim:FormalType, so the DualGroundingShape (which demands a realization only of tower objects,
# not of arrows) never fires on them. STEP D adds the teeth (q_functor_identity,
# q_functor_composition, q_ancestral_transport_resolves, FunctorTransportShape).
# =====================================================================================

# ---- the common physical-target type (encodings AND the ByteVector apex carrier) ----
prim:PhysicalObject a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "PhysicalObject" ;
    rdfs:comment "The common supertype of everything the realization functor rho lands an OBJECT on: every concrete prim:PhysicalEncoding of physical.ttl / realization.ttl AND the generic prim:ByteVector apex carrier that rho assigns to the abstract root prim:FormalType. It exists so that prim:rhoObject, prim:transportDom and prim:transportCod can declare an rdfs:range that covers both an encoding individual and the ByteVector carrier class WITHOUT letting rdfs range-inference stamp prim:ByteVector as a prim:PhysicalEncoding (which would make the EncodingShape demand a bitWidth / byteOrder / interpretAs of it and break the floor). It is deliberately NOT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType, so the DualGroundingShape never demands a realization of it; it is a plumbing type for the functor's codomain, the physical carrier category in which the RealizationTransport morphisms live." .

# ---- rho on arrows: the RealizationTransport morphism -------------------------------
prim:RealizationTransport a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "RealizationTransport" ;
    rdfs:comment "The image under the realization functor rho of ONE arrow of the thin formal category (a prim:SubtypeArrow, prim:IdentityArrow or prim:CompositeArrow of formal.ttl): a genuine morphism rho(arrowDom) -> rho(arrowCod) in the physical carrier category, recorded with prim:transportOf (the formal arrow it is the rho-image of), prim:transportDom (= rho of that arrow's domain) and prim:transportCod (= rho of that arrow's codomain). It is what promotes rho from a bare object map (type -> carrier) to a real FUNCTOR ON MORPHISMS: an identity transport carries prim:isTransportIdentity true with equal dom and cod (rho(id_A) = id on rho(A)); a composite transport reifies prim:transportComposeFirst / prim:transportComposeSecond / prim:transportComposeInto mirroring the formal CompositeArrow's decomposition (rho(g after f) = rho(g) after rho(f)). It is deliberately distinct from prim:Realization (a Kleisli/Frame arrow FormalType -> PhysicalEncoding) and from prim:Composition (a reified Kleisli >=> of realizations): a RealizationTransport composes CARRIERS along transported SUBTYPING, so it uses its own prim:transportCompose* property family and is never targeted by the RealizationShape or KleisliCompositionShape." .

prim:AncestralGroundingPath a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "AncestralGroundingPath" ;
    rdfs:comment "The materialisation of the panel residual finding 'X is also grounded through its ancestor': for a tower subtype A that has a realized proper ancestor B (B one of the 59 realized classes, never the unrealized apex prim:FormalType), an AncestralGroundingPath is the real, resolvable edge that carries A's grounding UP the tower to B's realization, in ADDITION to A's own direct grounding (never replacing it). It records prim:ancestralSubtype A, prim:ancestralAncestor B, prim:ancestralViaArrow (the formal subtyping arrow A -> B, direct or composite), prim:ancestralViaTransport (that arrow's rho-image, a prim:RealizationTransport whose transportDom is rho(A) and whose transportCod is rho(B)), and prim:ancestralLandsIn (rho(B) = the ancestor's own realization carrier that the transport resolves to). This is how a subtype becomes inspectably dual-grounded TWO WAYS - directly on its own reflexive carrier and, along this path, transported into the realization of its ancestor - turning a categorical truth that was only prose into a teeth-provable graph fact for Currency, URI, Categorical, Longitude and every similar subtype." .

# ---- properties of the functor-on-arrows + ancestral layer -------------------------
prim:rhoObject a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "rhoObject" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:range prim:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:comment "The OBJECT action of the realization functor rho, made explicit and single-valued: prim:rhoObject maps each FormalType to its ONE canonical physical carrier - for each of the 59 tower classes the reflexive carrier whose prim:interpretAs closes back to that same class (so rho(A) is genuinely A's own carrier), and for the abstract apex prim:FormalType the generic prim:ByteVector carrier (the honest erasure of 'a value of some type' to 'some bytes'). Where prim:realizesAs fans a type out to MANY encodings (the monad's polymorphism), rhoObject pins the single canonical choice the functor transports over, so every prim:RealizationTransport has well-defined dom = rhoObject(arrowDom) and cod = rhoObject(arrowCod). It agrees with the physicalFacet a taiji prim:Primitive selects for that type." .

prim:transportOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transportOf" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "Names the FormalArrow of formal.ttl (a prim:SubtypeArrow, prim:IdentityArrow or prim:CompositeArrow) whose image under the realization functor rho this prim:RealizationTransport is. It is the link that ties the on-arrows action back to the on-objects action: the transport's prim:transportDom must equal rhoObject of the named arrow's prim:arrowDom and its prim:transportCod must equal rhoObject of the arrow's prim:arrowCod, which is exactly the statement that the realization square over that arrow commutes. Reading transportOf lets a query recover, for any transported carrier morphism, the precise subtyping fact in the formal olog it realizes." .

prim:transportDom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transportDom" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:range prim:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:comment "The domain (source carrier) of a prim:RealizationTransport: the physical carrier rho(arrowDom) the transported morphism departs from, equal to prim:rhoObject applied to the domain of the formal arrow named by prim:transportOf. For an identity transport it equals prim:transportCod (rho(id_A) is the loop at rho(A)); for a composite transport it equals the transportDom of prim:transportComposeFirst (the first leg's source). Its range is prim:PhysicalObject rather than prim:PhysicalEncoding so that the apex case rho(FormalType) = prim:ByteVector is admissible without rdfs range-inference mistyping the ByteVector carrier as an encoding." .

prim:transportCod a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transportCod" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:range prim:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:comment "The codomain (target carrier) of a prim:RealizationTransport: the physical carrier rho(arrowCod) the transported morphism lands in, equal to prim:rhoObject applied to the codomain of the formal arrow named by prim:transportOf. It is the axis along which ancestral grounding resolves: for an ancestral grounding path A -> B the transport's transportCod is exactly rho(B), the realization carrier of the ancestor, so prim:ancestralLandsIn resolves to it. For a composite transport it equals the transportCod of prim:transportComposeSecond (the second leg's target); its range is prim:PhysicalObject for the same apex-admissibility reason as prim:transportDom." .

prim:isTransportIdentity a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "isTransportIdentity" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment "Flags a prim:RealizationTransport that is an IDENTITY transport - the rho-image of a prim:IdentityArrow id_A, witnessing the functor identity law rho(id_A) = id on rho(A). When true the transport's prim:transportDom equals its prim:transportCod (both rho(A)) and it is the reflexive loop at that carrier, the unit for transport composition. There is exactly one such identity transport per object of the formal category (60 in this floor: the 59 tower classes plus the FormalType apex), so the identity law is total over the objects - a requirement for the transports to form the arrows of a genuine category rather than a bare edge set. The flag is the checkable trace STEP D's q_functor_identity reads." .

prim:transportComposeFirst a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transportComposeFirst" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:range prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:comment "The first leg of a composite transport's reified decomposition rho(g after f) = rho(g) after rho(f): the prim:RealizationTransport that is rho(f), the transported image of the first operand of the corresponding formal CompositeArrow (its prim:composeArrowFirst). It is named apart from the Kleisli prim:composeFirst of realization composition precisely so the functor-on-arrows composition and the realization-monad composition never collide and so the KleisliCompositionShape (which targets prim:Composition via prim:composeFirst/Second) never fires on a transport. Well-typedness requires this leg's prim:transportCod to equal prim:transportComposeSecond's prim:transportDom (the legs meet at rho of the middle object)." .

prim:transportComposeSecond a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transportComposeSecond" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:range prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:comment "The second leg of a composite transport's reified decomposition rho(g after f) = rho(g) after rho(f): the prim:RealizationTransport that is rho(g), the transported image of the second operand of the corresponding formal CompositeArrow (its prim:composeArrowSecond) - itself either a subtype transport (when the ancestor covers the middle object) or a lower composite transport. With prim:transportComposeFirst and prim:transportComposeInto it makes a composite transport a self-describing witness of one functor-composition equation; because the formal category is thin this second leg is uniquely determined, so the transported composition is associative on the nose exactly as the formal composition is." .

prim:transportComposeInto a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transportComposeInto" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:range prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:comment "The result of a composite transport's reified decomposition rho(g after f) = rho(g) after rho(f): the composed transport rho(A) -> rho(C) that prim:transportComposeFirst (rho(f)) and prim:transportComposeSecond (rho(g)) compose to. In this thin category the composite of a given ordered carrier pair is unique, so prim:transportComposeInto points at THIS composite transport individual itself - the transport is its own composition witness, mirroring exactly how the formal prim:composeArrowInto points at its own CompositeArrow. This edge-for-edge mirroring is what makes rho(g after f) = rho(g) after rho(f) a definitional graph fact rather than an asserted law." .

prim:ancestralSubtype a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ancestralSubtype" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:AncestralGroundingPath ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "The subtype A whose additional, ancestor-mediated grounding a prim:AncestralGroundingPath witnesses: the domain of the formal subtyping arrow the path transports. A keeps its OWN direct grounding untouched; this coordinate records which subtype is being shown to ALSO be grounded through a realized ancestor, so a query can enumerate, for any tower class, all the realized ancestors it is transitively grounded into (its prim:ancestralGroundsAlso paths) alongside its own reflexive carrier." .

prim:ancestralAncestor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ancestralAncestor" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:AncestralGroundingPath ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "The realized ancestor B a prim:AncestralGroundingPath grounds its subtype into: the codomain of the transported formal subtyping arrow, always one of the 59 realized tower classes (never the unrealized apex prim:FormalType, which is why direct-child-of-FormalType classes have no ancestral grounding path). B carries its OWN realization, and prim:ancestralLandsIn resolves to rho(B) = that realization's carrier, so the path proves the subtype is grounded into a genuinely realized ancestor rather than into a dangling abstract node." .

prim:ancestralViaArrow a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ancestralViaArrow" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:AncestralGroundingPath ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The formal subtyping arrow A -> B (a prim:SubtypeArrow when B is A's immediate parent, a prim:CompositeArrow when B is a higher ancestor) of formal.ttl that a prim:AncestralGroundingPath transports under rho. It is the formal-olog witness that A really is below B in the ISA of the mathematics; the path's prim:ancestralViaTransport is precisely this arrow's rho-image, so the path pairs a subtyping fact with its realization-carrier consequence." .

prim:ancestralViaTransport a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ancestralViaTransport" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:AncestralGroundingPath ;
    rdfs:range prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:comment "The prim:RealizationTransport that is the rho-image of the path's prim:ancestralViaArrow: the transported carrier morphism rho(A) -> rho(B) that carries the subtype's canonical carrier UP to the ancestor's realization carrier. This is the load-bearing edge that makes 'A is also grounded through B' resolvable rather than asserted: following prim:ancestralViaTransport then prim:transportCod lands on rho(B), which must equal the path's prim:ancestralLandsIn. It is the concrete materialisation the residual finding demanded - the grounding is transported, not merely claimed." .

prim:ancestralLandsIn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ancestralLandsIn" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:AncestralGroundingPath ;
    rdfs:range prim:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:comment "The carrier the ancestral grounding resolves to: rho(B) = the canonical realization carrier of the ancestor prim:ancestralAncestor, which equals the prim:transportCod of the path's prim:ancestralViaTransport. It is the endpoint proving the subtype's transported grounding actually lands in a realized ancestor's encoding (that carrier's prim:interpretAs closes reflexively onto B), so 'grounded through the ancestor' is a real, inspectable, terminating resolution rather than a dangling reference." .

prim:ancestralGroundsAlso a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "ancestralGroundsAlso" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:range prim:AncestralGroundingPath ;
    rdfs:comment "The convenience back-edge from a tower subtype A to each prim:AncestralGroundingPath in which A is the prim:ancestralSubtype: A ancestrallyGroundsAlso the path that carries it into a realized ancestor. It makes the 'also grounded through ancestor' relationship directly navigable from the subtype (without an inverse join), so a reader of prim:Currency, prim:URI, prim:Categorical, prim:Longitude or any similar class can enumerate straight from the class all the additional ancestor-mediated groundings it enjoys on top of its own direct reflexive grounding." .

